I’m not a CPA, but here’s a clear, practical breakdown of how your 2024 federal return will generally work given the facts you provided, what’s deductible, which filing approach likely makes sense, and the IRS forms involved. I’ll also flag a few things I need to confirm to give exact numbers.

Quick summary (recommendation)
- Likely take the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2024 standard deduction ≈ $29,200) — your itemizable deductions (only $2,000 in charitable gifts shown) are almost certainly smaller than the standard deduction.
- Claim your web-design income and business expenses (including a home-office deduction) on Schedule C. Use the regular home-office method (allocating rent) — it looks like it will give you a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Expect to pay self‑employment tax on your net business profit; you can deduct half of that on Form 1040 (above the line).
- Get or confirm the loan-interest and 1099 forms (1098‑E for student loan interest, 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV from banks/brokers, 1099‑NEC/1099‑K for freelance pay if applicable).

Details and rough calculations
Income to report
- W‑2 (Riverside Hospital): $82,350 — reported on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900 — reported on Schedule C.
- Interest from Discover savings: $890 — reported as interest (1099‑INT).
- $195 from TD Ameritrade — report as interest or dividends (1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV; if it’s proceeds from sales you may have a Form 1099‑B and capital gain/loss reporting).

Home office
- Space: 155 sq ft exclusive out of 750 sq ft total → business-use % = 155/750 ≈ 20.67%.
- Rent: $2,050/month → annual rent = $24,600.
- Regular (actual-expense) method allocates a share of rent: 24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,087 (you could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if applicable). You would report these home‑office expenses on Schedule C and use Form 8829 to calculate the allowable amount (if you use the regular method).
- Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 (no Form 8829). Regular gives a much larger deduction here, so regular method is likely better.
- Important: Home office deduction is only for self‑employment/business use (it won’t help your W‑2 job). You already stated the 155 sq ft is exclusive — that meets the exclusivity test if it’s used regularly and solely for business.

Business expenses / unreimbursed expenses
- If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the web-design work (supplies, software, travel, client costs), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment taxable profit.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, those are not deductible for most taxpayers under current law (suspended since 2018).

Self‑employment tax and above‑the‑line deductions (approximate)
Using the regular home office + treating the $890 as business expenses:
- Schedule C net profit ≈ 8,900 − 5,087 − 890 = ~$2,923.
- Net SE earnings subject to SE tax = 2,923 × 0.9235 ≈ $2,700.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ $2,700 × 15.3% ≈ $412. (You pay this on Schedule SE.)
- You can deduct half of SE tax on Form 1040 as an above‑the‑line deduction (≈ $206).

Student loan interest ($4,400)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year (2024) is potentially deductible. The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (and there are income phaseouts based on your MAGI).
- If you really paid $4,400 in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phaseout). If the $4,400 refers to interest paid in prior years (2016–2019), it is not deductible on your 2024 return — only interest paid in 2024 counts.
- The lender should provide a Form 1098‑E for any interest paid in the year; you can use that to claim the deduction on Schedule 1 of Form 1040 (above the line).

Itemize vs standard (conclusion)
- Itemized deductions you listed: charitable contributions $2,000. You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, or other large deductible items. Even adding potential SALT (property + state income taxes) is capped at $10,000.
- 2024 MFJ standard deduction ≈ $29,200 — this is almost certainly larger than your itemized total, so take the standard deduction.
- Note: the home‑office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it reduces business income on Schedule C, so it helps either way.

Forms you’ll use (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and other adjustments flow here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for web-design income and business expenses; home-office expenses reported here
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (but you will likely not itemize)
- 1099‑INT and 1099‑DIV (report interest/dividend income), 1098‑E (student loan interest), and any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑K you received for freelance income

State return (Illinois)
- Illinois uses your federal AGI as a starting point but has different deductions/exemptions. I don’t want to give incorrect specifics — check Illinois Department of Revenue instructions or I can look up the exact IL rules if you want. In many cases, the federal standard deduction vs. itemize decision is the main driver for IL.

Recordkeeping & receipts
- Keep: receipts for charitable gift (you said you have one), proof of student loan interest paid (1098‑E), rent receipts/lease, record of square footage and photos showing exclusive use, invoices/receipts for business expenses, 1099s.
- If you use the regular home office method, keep clear documentation of square footage and how you allocated rent/utilities.

Other points
- If you owe tax beyond withholding (including SE tax), you may need to make quarterly estimated payments during the year to avoid penalties.
- If any of your freelance clients issued 1099‑NEC or you got 1099‑K, you must include those amounts even if no 1099 was issued — your obligation is to report all income.
- Confirm whether your spouse has income and any other deductions or credits (child tax credits, education credits, retirement contributions, IRA/401(k) contributions) — these can affect whether you itemize or other tax calculations.

What I need from you to be precise
1. Was the $4,400 in student loan interest actually paid in tax year 2024 (or is that total interest paid over earlier years)? Do you have a 1098‑E for 2024?
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web design work (i.e., deductible on Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses from the W‑2 job?
3. Is the $195 from TD Ameritrade interest, dividends, or proceeds from sales (do you have 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B)?
4. Any mortgage interest, property taxes, or other potential itemizable deductions not listed?
5. Does your spouse have other income or deductions I should include?

If you want, I can:
- Run a more detailed, line‑by‑line rough tax calculation if you answer the clarifying questions above.
- Draft a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer or to use if you prepare your own return.
- Look up Illinois state rules and how this affects your IL return.

