Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize, and which federal/state forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also call out a few clarifying questions where your answers change the outcome.

Summary (quick)
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly). The standard deduction for MFJ (tax year 2024) is $29,200, which will be larger than the itemized deductions you listed.
- Your web-design income is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C; business expenses (including a home‑office share or the simplified home‑office option) reduce that income.
- Student loan interest may be deductible (up to $2,500 maximum) as an above‑the‑line adjustment — but only interest you actually paid in the tax year and subject to income phaseouts.
- Charitable giving ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize; since the standard deduction exceeds your listed itemizable items, charitable contributions alone won’t make itemizing beneficial.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are not deductible for federal returns through at least 2025 (suspended by TCJA). If the $890 is a business expense for your self‑employment work, it’s deductible on Schedule C.

Detailed treatment and calculations

1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $82,350 — report on Form 1040 (W‑2 information).
- Self‑employment (web design) income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest from Discover Bank: $890 — report as interest income on Form 1040 (if under $1,500 total interest you don’t need Schedule B; combined interest & dividends under $1,500 generally don’t require Schedule B).
- TD Ameritrade $195 — if this is interest or dividends, also report on Form 1040; again Schedule B only if total > $1,500 (or other reasons).

2) Self‑employment tax and related items
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax, calculated on Schedule SE. You pay ~15.3% SE tax on net earnings (after the 0.9235 adjustment); half of the SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- You may also owe estimated taxes during the year (if not withheld elsewhere) — note for planning.

3) Home office deduction (you qualify because you said the 155 sq ft is used exclusively)
Options:
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction. No Form 8829 required; deduction is taken directly on Schedule C.
- Regular method: allocate actual home-related expenses by business percentage.
  - Business % = 155 / 750 = 20.667% (about 20.67%).
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Business share of rent ≈ $24,600 × 20.667% ≈ $5,083.
  - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, and qualified repairs by the same percent. (If you own the home, depreciation on the business portion would also be a factor; as a renter you generally cannot depreciate the structure.)
- Important limits:
  - Home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business (but excess can carry forward under certain conditions).
  - If you use regular method, you generally file Form 8829 to compute allowable home office deduction, then transfer to Schedule C; for simplified, no Form 8829 is needed.

Which method makes sense here?
- Simplified gives $775; regular rent allocation for the home office portion looks like ~$5,083 (assuming no other allocable utilities or insurance). Because that $5,083 is less than your gross self‑employment income of $8,900, it’s allowable (assuming the expenses are indeed business-related) and will materially reduce net profit and SE tax.
- If you choose the regular method be sure you have records of rent, utilities, and other home expenses you’ll allocate.

4) Student loan interest ($4,400 you reported)
- Deduction rules: Only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible. The student loan interest deduction has a maximum of $2,500 per return (subject to income phaseouts).
- If the $4,400 figure is interest you paid across multiple earlier years (2016–2019) rather than in 2024, those prior‑year payments are not deductible on your 2024 return. If you actually paid $4,400 in 2024, the maximum you could claim in 2024 is $2,500 (or less if your MAGI exceeds the phaseout range).
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) so it reduces AGI whether or not you itemize.

5) Charitable contribution $2,000
- Deductible if you itemize. You said you have a receipt — good.
- Because the 2024 standard deduction for MFJ is $29,200, $2,000 of charitable giving alone generally won’t make itemizing beneficial unless you have substantially more deductible items (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the $10k cap, large medical expenses exceeding the AGI threshold, etc.).

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are employee business expenses related to your W‑2 hospital job, they are not deductible on federal returns (suspended through 2025).
- If the $890 is business expenses for your web‑design work (supplies, software, client travel, hosting, etc.), treat them as Schedule C expenses and deduct them there.

7) Itemize vs standard
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2024) = $29,200. Based on the items you listed (charity $2,000 and possibly student loan interest as an adjustment), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Itemize only if your total itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, state and local taxes up to $10k, charitable contributions, large medical expenses over threshold, etc.) exceed $29,200.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest deduction and for reporting certain other income adjustments.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web‑design income and business expenses, including the home office (or the home office deduction if using the simplified method goes on Schedule C).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method (not required for simplified).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax on your Schedule C net profit.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — potentially for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) if you qualify; whether you qualify depends on taxable income thresholds and whether your business is a specified service trade or business. With relatively low net profit QBI may be small but you may still be eligible.
- Schedule B — only if you have more than $1,500 of interest/dividends or other reasons to file it (your interest + dividends are below that).
- Illinois state return: IL‑1040 for Illinois income tax.

Other practical notes
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable gifts, records of rent and utilities, records supporting the 155 sq ft exclusive business use, 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC if you received them from clients (even if you didn’t, report income you received), 1099‑INT / brokerage statements for interest/dividends.
- If you take the regular home‑office method, you’ll need records of rent and utility bills for the year.
- If you have tax withholding on the W‑2, that will reduce what you owe at filing; self‑employment tax is in addition.
- Consider whether you need to make or adjust estimated tax payments for self‑employment income in future years.

