Thanks — I can walk through how each piece is treated, what forms you’ll need, and whether you should itemize. A few quick clarifying questions first (so my guidance is exact):

- That $4,400 in student-loan interest — was that interest you paid during tax year 2024, or is that interest paid in prior years (2016–2019)? Only interest paid during the tax year can be deducted for 2024.
- The $890 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those expenses for your web‑design work (self‑employment) or were they unreimbursed employee expenses related to the W‑2 job?
- Is there any spouse income, mortgage interest, property tax, or large state income tax withholding not listed? (SALT and mortgage interest can make itemizing more likely.)

Below is a clear, actionable summary based on the numbers you gave and common rules. I’ll flag where I’m making assumptions.

What you can (generally) deduct
- Self‑employment/business expenses (web design)
  - Report business income ($8,900) on Schedule C. Deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses (software, hosting, supplies, business portion of phone/internet if allocated). If your $890 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, they belong on Schedule C.
  - Home‑office deduction (see separate section below).
  - Resulting net profit flows to Form 1040 and is subject to income tax and self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

- Home office
  - You said 155 sq ft exclusive use, home = 750 sq ft, rent $2,050/month.
  - Business percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.667% (≈ 20.67%).
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
    - Actual expenses: business portion of rent = $24,600 (annual rent) × 20.6667% ≈ $5,080. You can also allocate a business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (if used partly for business) to increase the deduction.
  - Because actual rent portion (~$5,080) > simplified ($775), actual-expense method will likely give a larger deduction. If you use actual expenses, you report the home‑office portion on Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.

- Student loan interest
  - The student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (above the line) up to $2,500 for the tax year, subject to phase‑outs based on Modified AGI. Married filing jointly phaseouts can apply; if your MAGI exceeds the phaseout range the deduction is reduced or eliminated.
  - Important: only interest paid during the tax year can be claimed for that year. If the $4,400 is total interest paid across multiple years, only the 2024 payments are deductible (and capped at $2,500 if eligible). If you actually paid $4,400 in 2024, you still can deduct at most $2,500 (if your MAGI is low enough).

- Interest & investment income
  - The $890 from Discover (interest) and $195 from TD Ameritrade (likely dividends/interest) are taxable on Form 1040. You’ll receive 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B as applicable and report them.

- Charitable contributions
  - The $2,000 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible if you itemize. Keep receipt/proof of donation. If you do not itemize, you cannot claim it on federal return (no universal above‑the‑line deduction for cash gifts in current law).

- Unreimbursed employee expenses
  - If those $890 are unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 employment, they are not deductible for federal individual taxpayers through tax year 2025 under current law (TCJA suspension). If they are business expenses for your self‑employment, they are deductible on Schedule C (see above).

Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly): $29,200.
- Known itemizable amount from what you gave: $2,000 charitable. You didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, or large state/local tax payments. Without significant SALT/mortgage/medical deductions, your itemized total is likely far below $29,200.
- Conclusion: With the information provided, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on Form 1040 and still benefit from the home‑office deduction because the home‑office and other business expenses are above‑the‑line reductions via Schedule C and Schedule SE (they reduce AGI or are adjustments), independent of whether you itemize. The student loan interest (if you qualify) is also an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500). So taking the standard deduction is likely best, but confirm whether you have property tax, mortgage interest, or large SALT amounts that would change that.

Other tax items to note
- Self‑employment tax: Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net earnings), reported on Schedule SE. Half of the SE tax is an adjustment (Schedule 1) reducing AGI.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified self‑employment income (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). With fairly small net business income after expenses, you likely qualify for a simple 20% QBI deduction, but the actual benefit depends on taxable income and whether you exceed thresholds.
- Estimated taxes: Because you have self‑employment income and investment interest/dividends, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes if withholding on your W‑2 and withholding/credits don’t cover your tax. Keep that in mind for the year.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return) — main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web‑design income and business expenses.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office (recommended here because it’s larger than the simplified).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like half of SE tax and student loan interest (if claimed).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for QBI deduction (depending on your taxable income level).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (I expect you will not).
- Keep your 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if any), 1099‑INT (Discover), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (TD Ameritrade), and W‑2 (Riverside Hospital). Even if you don’t receive a 1099 for your self‑employment, you must still report the income.

Forms you’ll need (Illinois)
- IL‑1040 — Illinois individual income tax return. Illinois tax is based on federal AGI with limited adjustments. Chicago has no city income tax. Report all income sources per state rules; state withholding and payments apply.

Recordkeeping & documentation
- Keep receipts for charitable gift ($2,000 receipt), home‑office allocation support (floor plan/square footage, lease and rent statements), business expense receipts, and student loan 1098‑E (if you have it) or loan statements showing interest paid in 2024.
- Keep 1099s and W‑2s. For self‑employment, retain invoices/contracts from clients.

