Assistant: Thanks — I can walk you through how your 2024 federal (and basic Illinois) tax treatment looks, what you can deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions at the end because a couple of details matter for the final answer.

Summary up front (short)
- Your $8,900 web‑design work is self‑employment income (Schedule C); you’ll owe self‑employment tax on net profit and may use a home office deduction against that business income.
- The $890 (Discover) is interest income and the $195 (TD Ameritrade) is likely dividend/interest — report those on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500).
- Your $2,000 cash donation is deductible only if you itemize (keep the receipt).
- Student loan interest can be an adjustment to income (above the line) but is limited — only interest paid in 2024 qualifies and there’s a $2,500 maximum (subject to MAGI phaseouts).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers after 2017. If the $890 is a business expense for your self‑employment work, it’s deductible on Schedule C.
- Most likely you’ll take the standard deduction (for MFJ 2024 it’s substantially higher than small charitable donation alone), but we’ll check once you give missing details.
- Key federal forms: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule 1 (adjustments), possibly Schedule A (itemize), possibly Form 8995 (QBI), Schedule B (if interest/dividends > $1,500). Illinois: IL‑1040.

Details and calculations

1) Employment and other income
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $82,350 — report on Form 1040 (you’ll also have withholding shown on the W‑2).
- Self‑employment: $8,900 (web design) — report gross receipts on Schedule C. Subtract deductible business expenses (including allowable portion of home office and any ordinary business expenses such as supplies, software, mileage, contract labor, advertising). Net profit flows to Form 1040 and also to Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax.
- Interest/dividends: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085 total. Report on Form 1040; Schedule B only required if combined interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (so not required with amounts shown).

2) Home office
- You said the home office is exclusive and 155 sq ft out of 750 sq ft total = 155/750 ≈ 20.67%.
- Because the web‑design income is self‑employment income, you can take the home office deduction (only allowed for self‑employed or for some special employees — generally not for W‑2 employees).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction on Schedule C.
  b) Actual method: allocate a percentage of eligible home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, a portion of internet if used for business, etc.) to the business. With rent $2,050/month = $24,600/year, your rent portion attributable to the office = $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,086.67. If you have utilities or insurance, add the business share of those as well. The actual method almost certainly gives a larger deduction here (assuming no other costs that offset).
- Choose either method for the year; if you rent, you cannot claim depreciation for a home you do not own (but you still can use actual method to allocate rent).
- Home office reduces Schedule C net profit, which reduces income subject to income tax and reduces self‑employment tax.

3) Self‑employment tax and the self‑employment adjustment
- Self‑employment tax is computed on net self‑employment earnings (92.35% of net profit) at 15.3% (Social Security + Medicare). You then get an above‑the‑line deduction for half of the self‑employment tax on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Example (illustrative only): if you used actual home office deduction of about $5,087 and had no other business expenses, net profit = $8,900 − $5,087 = ~$3,813. SE taxable income ≈ $3,813 × 0.9235 ≈ $3,520; SE tax ≈ $3,520 × 15.3% ≈ $538; half of that (~$269) is deductible above the line.

4) Student loan interest ($4,400 you reported)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible. The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (as an adjustment to income) if you meet eligibility rules.
- Eligibility: you must be legally obligated to pay the loan and the loan must have been used for qualified education. Navient loans usually qualify.
- MAGI phaseouts apply for married filing jointly (the deduction phases out as MAGI rises above the IRS threshold for the year). With the income numbers you gave (approx $92k before adjustments), you’re likely below the phaseout range and can claim up to $2,500 — but I need to confirm the $4,400 figure is interest paid in 2024; only then is a deduction possible (and limited to $2,500).
- If the $4,400 is interest paid in prior years (2016–2019) you cannot deduct it on the 2024 return.

5) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Cash charitable contributions to qualified organizations (Doctors Without Borders qualifies) are deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. There is no universal above‑the‑line cash charitable deduction for 2024 (that was temporary in 2020).
- Keep the receipt — for cash gifts of $250 or more, you need written acknowledgment from the charity (which you said you have).
- If you do not have other itemized deductions large enough to exceed the standard deduction (see next), you would not deduct this on your federal return.

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are unreimbursed employee business expenses (expenses you incurred as a hospital employee that were not reimbursed), they are not deductible on the federal return for tax years through 2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- If, instead, they are expenses related to your web‑design business (supplies, software, client costs), report them on Schedule C as business expenses (deductible).

7) Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly increased vs prior years (IRS inflation adjustments). With the items you listed, your known itemized deductions are only $2,000 charitable + whatever state/local taxes and property taxes or mortgage interest you paid (you did not list these). The SALT cap limits state and local tax + property tax deductions to $10,000 federallly.
- Given only $2,000 of charitable contributions and no other big itemized items listed, you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction (so no benefit from itemizing).
- Action: add up all possible itemized items (state income tax withheld plus any estimated payments, property tax, mortgage interest, medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI if any, charitable contributions) and compare to the standard deduction. If total itemized > standard, itemize; otherwise take the standard.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) / Section 199A
- You may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from your web‑design self‑employment. With such a small net profit it will likely be 20% of your QBI (after adjustments), subject to overall taxable income limits. This is reported using Form 8995 or 8995‑A depending on complexity. For your income level it will likely be the simpler Form 8995.

9) State tax (Illinois)
- File IL‑1040 for Illinois. Illinois tax is generally based on federal AGI with some adjustments. Report your federal AGI; Illinois taxes most income at a flat rate. You may be able to deduct federal income tax? No — federal tax is not deductible on IL return; just follow IL guidance. If you had state tax withheld on the W‑2, that helps pay Illinois liability.

10) Forms you will likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and other adjustments reported here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and business expenses, including home office deduction
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — QBI deduction (likely 8995, the simplified form)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable contributions, SALT up to $10k, mortgage interest, etc.)
- Schedule B — only if interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (your total is $1,085 so not needed)
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑K — you should have or receive any 1099s from clients or payment processors; report all income even if you didn’t receive a 1099.

Records to keep
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099‑NEC, 1099‑MISC, or 1099‑K for self‑employment income (if applicable) and records of payments from clients
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses and the $890 unreimbursed items (if business-related)
- Rent receipts or lease showing rent amount, utility bills, renter’s insurance if using actual method for home office
- Charitable receipt from Doctors Without Borders (you said you have it)
- 1099‑INT from Discover, 1099‑DIV/1099‑INT from TD Ameritrade
- Navient statement showing interest paid in 2024 (if any)

Practical recommended next steps
