Thanks — this is enough to give practical, specific guidance. I have a few short clarifying questions first (they change the recommendation):

1. Is the $4,400 of student‑loan interest paid during 2024 (the year you’re filing for), or were those payments made in 2016–2019 (i.e., earlier years)? The student‑loan interest deduction only applies to interest actually paid in the tax year you’re filing for.  
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web‑design work (i.e., you paid them as part of that self‑employment), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses (expenses related to a W‑2 job)? Employee unreimbursed expenses are generally not deductible for federal taxes (through at least 2025).
3. Do you have any other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax withheld/paid, large medical expenses, casualty losses, etc.)? Those matter for deciding whether to itemize.

Below is a clear summary of what you can/don’t can deduct, how to treat the home office, a simple comparison for itemizing vs standard deduction, and the federal (and a couple state) forms you’ll likely need.

What you can (and can’t) deduct — overview
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital $82,350): reported on Form 1040 from your W‑2.  
- Self‑employment income (web design $8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see below).  
- Bank/brokerage interest/dividends ($890 Discover + $195 TD Ameritrade): report as taxable interest/dividends on Form 1040. If interest/dividends from all sources exceed $1,500 you would also file Schedule B; at your reported amounts you likely won’t need Schedule B but still report them on 1040 lines for interest/dividends. Expect 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B statements from the institutions.  
- Home office: allowed for self‑employed taxpayers if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. See detailed treatment below.  
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890): if these are business expenses for your web design (supplies, software, internet portion used for the business, etc.) claim them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal return (TCJA suspension through 2025).  
- Student‑loan interest ($4,400): only interest paid in the 2024 tax year can be claimed for 2024 taxes. The maximum annual above‑the‑line student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). If the $4,400 represents interest paid in 2024, you can claim up to $2,500 (if your modified adjusted gross income is under the phaseout thresholds). If these payments were made earlier (2016–2019), they cannot be deducted for 2024. Keep Form 1098‑E(s) you received from Navient (or payment records).  
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt. No above‑the‑line deduction unless special temporary law applies (no general universal charity deduction in 2024).  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if they are such): generally not deductible for federal income taxes for tax years affected by TCJA.

Home office — specifics and numbers
You said: 155 sq ft exclusive business use; total residence = 750 sq ft; rent = $2,050/month.

- Business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.6667% (≈ 20.67%).  
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.  
- Actual (regular) method home office portion of rent = $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,084. That is the portion of rent you can allocate as a business expense (you can also allocate qualified utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). To claim actual expenses for a self‑employed business you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the result to Schedule C.  
- Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft = 155 × $5 = $775. No Form 8829 required for the simplified method.  
- Comparison: $5,084 (actual) vs $775 (simplified). Actual method almost certainly gives a much larger deduction in your case. Use actual method (Form 8829) unless you prefer simplicity and paperwork avoidance.

Important home‑office notes:
- The space must be used regularly and exclusively for the business — that’s a strict requirement. If you meet that, deduct the business percentage of rent and utilities, and depreciation if you owned the home (not applicable if renting).  
- For self‑employed taxpayers, home‑office deductions reduce Schedule C net income and therefore reduce self‑employment tax and income tax.  
- If you were an employee working from home for your W‑2 employer, the home office is generally not deductible (employee deduction suspended).

Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Net self‑employment income after business expenses and home office is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on net earnings after the 7.65% adjustment). You also get to deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income.  
- Because your web design income is relatively modest, you will likely owe a small SE tax amount. You may also qualify for the QBI (Section 199A) 20% qualified business income deduction on your net business income (Form 8995 or 8995‑A) subject to rules and income thresholds; with your income level you will likely be able to claim some or all of QBI. (Exact calculation requires net business income after deductions.)  

Itemize vs Standard deduction — how to decide for your situation
- For Married Filing Jointly the standard deduction is large (for recent years it has been around $27k–$29k; confirm the exact IRS number for tax year 2024 when you prepare return).  
- Your only stated itemized amount is $2,000 charitable contributions. Unless you have other substantial itemized items (large mortgage interest, big state & local taxes, property taxes, large unreimbursed medical expenses above the 7.5% of AGI floor, etc.), your itemized total will be far below the standard deduction. In that case you should take the standard deduction.  
- The home office deduction is NOT an itemized deduction. It is a business deduction on Schedule C and does not affect the standard vs itemize decision.

Quick example (approximate) to show relative magnitudes (assume student interest deduction of $2,500 allowed and $890 is business expense — changeable based on your answers):
- Total income: 82,350 (W‑2) + 8,900 (self) + 890 + 195 = 92,335.  
- Home office (actual) ≈ 5,084; business expense $890 (if business) → net self‑employment ≈ 2,926.  
- SE tax roughly ≈ $400 (give or take). Half SE tax deductible ≈ $200.  
- Above‑the‑line student interest deduction (if eligible) up to $2,500.  
- Your itemizable charitable = $2,000. Even with that, your total itemized likely << standard deduction for MFJ, so the standard deduction will almost certainly be better unless you have other large itemizable items.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.  
- Schedule C — report web‑design income and business expenses (home office deduction is entered via Form 8829 or simplified worksheet).  
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you choose the actual expense method). If you use simplified method you do not file Form 8829.  
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.  
- Schedule 1 (if applicable) — for adjustments such as student‑loan interest deduction (Schedule 1 transfers totals to 1040).  
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (for the $2,000 charitable contribution and any other itemizable items).  
- Schedule B — only if you have more than $1,500 of interest/dividends or other reasons to file it. At $890 + $195 you’re under that threshold.  
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20%) from your self‑employment income.  
- Keep all 1099s and W‑2s: W‑2 from Riverside Hospital, 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC for web‑design clients (if issued), 1099‑INT (Discover), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B from TD Ameritrade, and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if applicable.

Forms/state
- Illinois state return: IL‑1040 (Illinois taxes are separate; many federal deductions are used in computing IL taxes). Check whether IL allows the same home‑office or other subtractions — typically state tax follow federal AGI but rules vary.

Records to keep / documentation
- Charity receipt for the $2,000 donation (you said you have it).  
- Form 1098‑E (or payment records) proving student‑loan interest paid in 2024 (if you claim the deduction).  
- Rent receipts or lease showing amount paid and square footage of residence + photos/floor plan to substantiate exclusive business use of the 155 sq ft. Keep utility bills if you allocate utilities.  
- Receipts/invoices for web‑design expenses (the $890 and any other business costs).  
- 1099s/W‑2s/1098s/1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B.

Practical recommendations / next steps
1. Confirm the two clarifying items I asked above (whether the $4,400 was paid in 2024 and whether the $890 is business expense). I’ll update the numbers and the recommendation once I have those answers.  
2. Very likely you should: (a) use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) because it gives a much larger deduction than the simplified method, and (b) take the standard deduction for 2024 unless you have other substantial itemized deductions (mortgage interest, significant state taxes, property tax, etc.).  
3. Consider making sure you have 1099s for the web‑design clients and have recorded all business expenses; file Schedule C + Schedule SE. Consider small‑business retirement (SEP/Solo401k) if you want to lower taxable income and save for retirement.  
4. If you expect to owe self‑employment tax beyond withholding, consider making estimated tax payments or increasing W‑2 withholding to avoid underpayment penalties.  
5. If you want, I can run a precise draft calculation of your federal taxable income and an estimate of income tax and SE tax if you confirm: (a) whether $4,400 interest was paid in 2024 or not, (b) whether $890 is business expense, and (c) whether you have any other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, state/local taxes, property tax, large medical expenses, etc.), plus ages (65+ increases standard deduction).

Final note: this is general tax guidance. For filing your return and for any complex situation (QBI filing choices, retirement contribution planning, state specifics, or if you had deductible student‑loan interest in earlier years that you think might change your current situation), consider a tax professional or CPA for final return preparation.

