Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through how each item should be treated, give a quick calculation example, and list the forms you’ll need. A few clarifying questions are below — please answer them so I can refine the numbers.

Summary (short)
- Report wages ($82,350), taxable interest ($890 + $195 = $1,085) and self‑employment/web design income ($8,900) on your Form 1040.
- Your web design work is reported on Schedule C; self‑employment tax on Schedule SE. Home office is a business expense on Schedule C (either simplified or actual method).
- Charitable gift ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize (most likely you’ll be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have large additional itemizable expenses).
- The $4,400 “student loan interest (Navient, 2016–2019)” as stated is not automatically deductible on 2024 returns — only interest you actually paid during the tax year on a qualified loan can be deducted (student loan interest deduction max is $2,500 and is subject to income limits).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are generally not deductible for most taxpayers under current law (suspended through 2025), unless you’re in a specific category allowed by law.

Details and calculations

1) Income you must report
- W-2 wages: $82,350 — enter on Form 1040 (wages line) with your W-2 attached.
- Taxable interest: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085 — report on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if your total interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts).
- Self-employment (web design) gross receipts: $8,900 — report on Schedule C.

2) Self-employment and home-office treatment
- Schedule C: report your $8,900 gross receipts and any allowed business expenses (supplies, software, advertising, mileage, etc.). You can deduct a home-office portion as a business expense on Schedule C.

Home-office methods:
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft: 155 * $5 = $775 deduction (easiest).
- Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses by business use percentage.
  - Business percentage = 155 sq ft ÷ 750 sq ft = 0.2067 (≈20.67%).
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion attributable to home office = $24,600 × 0.2067 ≈ $5,086.
  - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., you allocate the business percentage of those too.
  - To use the actual method you normally complete Form 8829 for a home used in a trade or business and carry the result to Schedule C.

Which method to pick?
- Based on rent alone, the actual method (≈$5,086) gives a larger deduction than the simplified method ($775). If you have no other home-related deductible expenses to allocate, actual method is likely better. Use actual method if you have records for rent, utilities, insurance and you want the bigger deduction. Simplified method is easier and requires no Form 8829.

Effect on self-employment tax and AGI:
- Example (using actual home-office only and no other business expenses):
  - Business profit = $8,900 − $5,086 = $3,814.
  - Net earnings subject to SE tax = $3,814 × 92.35% ≈ $3,523.
  - Self-employment (SE) tax ≈ 15.3% × $3,523 ≈ $539.
  - Half of SE tax (≈ $269) is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), reducing your AGI.
  - The Schedule C loss/reduction also lowers your income subject to income tax.

3) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Only deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction (see next), you cannot also deduct this $2,000 on Schedule A.
- Keep your receipt — you’ll need it if you itemize.

4) Student loan interest ($4,400, Navient 2016–2019)
- Important: The student loan interest deduction applies to interest you actually paid during the tax year on a qualified student loan. You cannot deduct interest from prior years on a current-year return.
- The deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1) with a maximum of $2,500 (phaseouts apply based on MAGI). If the $4,400 was not paid in 2024 (but instead represents payments made in 2016–2019), it is not deductible on your 2024 tax return.
- Action: find your 1098-E (student loan interest statement) for the year 2024 if you have interest paid in 2024.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these were unreimbursed employee business expenses (i.e., your employer didn’t reimburse you), the miscellaneous itemized deduction for unreimbursed employee expenses is suspended through 2025 for most employees — so they are not deductible on your federal return.
- If those $890 were actually business expenses for your self‑employment (web design) and not employee expenses, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible there. Please clarify which they are.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- You listed only $2,000 in charitable contributions and (apparently) no mortgage interest, no property taxes, and no large state/local taxes listed. For most married filing jointly taxpayers the standard deduction (which is substantially larger than $2,000) will be higher than your likely itemized total — so you will probably take the standard deduction.
- Because the home-office deduction is a business deduction on Schedule C (not on Schedule A), it does not help you reach the itemized total — it lowers Schedule C profit instead.
- Conclusion: Unless you have other itemizable amounts (large mortgage interest, substantial state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses above the floor, or substantial unreimbursed expenses that qualify), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction. If you want I can compare numerically if you supply mortgage interest, property tax, state income taxes paid, or any other itemizable expense.

7) Other considerations
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may qualify for a 20% pass‑through QBI deduction on qualified business income; this is computed on Form 8995 (or 8995-A) and depends on your taxable income and the nature of your business. With small net business income you likely get a QBI deduction equal to 20% of your qualified business income, but it’s subject to overall taxable income limits — I can estimate if you want.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (both income tax and SE tax) if you expect to owe $1,000 or more when you file. Consider making estimated payments or increasing withholding to avoid penalties next year.

8) Forms you will need (tax year 2024 filing)
- Form 1040 — main tax return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., half of SE tax, student loan interest if eligible).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend totals exceed $1,500 or you have foreign accounts (your interest total is $1,085 so you likely don’t need Schedule B).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report web design income and business expenses).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (calculate SE tax).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual method for the home office). Not needed if you use the simplified method.
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize; for just $2,000 charitable, you’ll likely skip this).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — Qualified Business Income deduction (if you claim the 20% QBI deduction).
- Keep 1099-NEC/1099-MISC (if you received any from clients), 1099-INTs (banks), 1099-B or 1099-DIV from brokerage if applicable, and 1098-E for student loan interest (if any), and your W-2.

9) Records to gather (action checklist)
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099-NEC/records of payments from the 6 web design clients (amounts and dates).
- Bank/broker 1099-INT, 1099-B, or account statements for interest ($890 and $195).
- Rent receipts / lease and utility bills showing rent and utilities (for home-office actual method).
- Receipts for business expenses (software, supplies, advertising, domain hosting, etc.).
- Receipt for $2,000 donation (charity receipt).
- 1098-E if any student loan interest was paid in 2024.
- Detailed breakdown of the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” (what they were and whether they relate to your business).

Questions for you
1. Was the $4,400 student loan interest paid during 2016–2019 (i.e., in those years), or did you pay $4,400 during 2024? (Only interest actually paid during the tax year can be deducted.)
2. Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your web design work, or are they employee expenses (job-related unreimbursed)? If business expenses, list what they are.
3. Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or state/local tax payments in 2024 (these affect itemizing)?
4. Do you want me to run the numbers using the actual home-office method versus the simplified method and give an estimate of taxable income and SE tax?

If you confirm those items I’ll run a precise estimate of your AGI, taxable income (itemized vs standard), SE tax, and a recommendation whether to use the simplified or actual home-office method. Also I’m not a CPA — if you want definitive filing advice tailored to complex student‑loan history or QBI optimization, I recommend checking with a tax professional.

