Short answer up front
- Likely take the standard deduction for 2024 (based on the items you listed) — your known itemizable deductions (charity $2,000) are much smaller than the MFJ standard deduction.  
- Your $8,900 web‑design income is self‑employment income: report it on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and you can take a home‑office deduction against that business income.  
- Home‑office choices: simplified method = $5 × 155 sq ft = $775; regular method (apportion rent & utilities) = about 155/750 ≈ 20.67% of qualifying home expenses (example rent = $24,600/year → home‑office share ≈ $5,086). The regular method will give a larger deduction here but is a little more paperwork and is limited to your net business profit.  
- You’ll use Form 1040 with Schedules C and SE (plus Schedule 1, maybe Schedule A if you end up itemizing) and IL‑1040 for Illinois state return.

Details and how to treat each item
1) Income
- W‑2 (Riverside Hospital) $82,350 → reported on Form 1040.  
- Web design $8,900 → self‑employment income; report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (software, hosting, supplies, advertising, part of your home office, etc.). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include them but report all income even if no 1099.  
- Discover savings $890 → taxable interest (reported on Form 1040, and Schedule B only if total interest/dividend > $1,500).  
- TD Ameritrade $195 → likely dividends/interest or investment income — report on Form 1040 (and Schedule B if applicable). If it’s from sales you may have a 1099‑B (capital gains) — include any basis/transactions.

2) Home office
- Space: 155 sq ft exclusive, total home 750 sq ft → business use percentage = 155/750 ≈ 20.67%.  
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 155 = $775 — easy, no Form 8829.  
  - Regular method: apportioned share (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, other allowable home expenses). Example using rent $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 → home‑office share ≈ $5,086 of rent alone (you could also include utilities, internet portion, etc.). Use Form 8829 to compute regular method.  
- Important limits: home‑office deduction for self‑employed reduces Schedule C net profit and cannot exceed your business net profit from that business. If the home‑office deduction would create or increase a loss, special rules apply (excess may be carried or become a carryover). The home office deduction is only available for the self‑employment (Schedule C) business, not for W‑2 employment (unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for federal under current law).

3) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) computed on Schedule SE (~15.3% on net earnings), but you may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).  
- You may need to make estimated quarterly tax payments for federal and Illinois income tax and possibly increase withholding on your W‑2 to cover additional tax.

4) Student loan interest ($4,400)
- Clarify: the student‑loan interest deduction is only for interest you actually paid in the tax year on a qualified student loan. The maximum deduction is limited (up to $2,500 of interest for eligible taxpayers) and is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1. There are income phaseouts for the student loan interest deduction (MAGI limits). If the $4,400 figure is interest paid in 2024, you can only deduct up to the allowed amount (and subject to phaseouts). If $4,400 is total interest from prior years (2016–2019), those earlier payments are NOT deductible on 2024 return. Please confirm the timing. I can help estimate the allowable amount once you confirm.

5) Charitable donation $2,000
- If you itemize (Schedule A) it’s deductible; if you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct it on federal 1040. You said you have the receipt — keep it.

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 job), they generally are not deductible for federal (suspended through 2025). If they are business expenses for your web‑design activity (materials, software, travel for clients, etc.), report them on Schedule C and they reduce net self‑employment income. Clarify what these were for.

7) Itemize vs Standard deduction
- From what you listed, your federal itemized deductions appear low: $2,000 charitable + (you have no mortgage interest or property tax listed, and unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible federally). Student loan interest is an adjustment (not part of Schedule A). Given that, you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction for married filing jointly. If you have additional itemizable items you didn’t mention (mortgage interest, significant state/local taxes paid, large medical expenses in excess of 7.5% of AGI, etc.), that could change the analysis. Tell me any other deductible items and I’ll recalc.

8) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax adjustment flow here.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for web design income/expenses.  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method. Not needed for simplified method.  
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize.  
- Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend totals exceed the threshold or certain conditions apply.  
- Any 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑NEC, W‑2 and 1099‑B (if applicable).  
State: IL‑1040 (Illinois) — report federal AGI as the starting point; Illinois tax is a separate return and has its own rules. IL generally uses federal AGI with limited additions/subtractions; check IL instructions or use software/CPA for state specifics.

9) Recordkeeping checklist
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.  
- Records/1099s for web‑design income (invoices, bank deposits, 1099‑NEC if received).  
- Receipts for business expenses (software, supplies, home office allocation support, internet bills, renter’s insurance, utilities).  
