Thanks — good detail. Summary first, then a short list of clarifying questions and suggested next steps.

Key points / what you can likely deduct
- Self-employment (web design) expenses: Your $8,900 from 6 clients is reported on Schedule C (Form 1040). Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work — e.g., the home-office share of rent, business supplies, software, business internet, and any of the $890 expenses that were business-related — are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your self-employment net income.
- Home-office: You said the office is 155 sq ft exclusive out of a 750 sq ft rental. That’s a business-use percentage of 155/750 = 20.666...% (exactly 31/150 = 0.2066667). With rent $2,050/month = $24,600/year, the business share of rent = 24,600 * 155/750 = $5,084. You can take this (plus the business share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) on Schedule C using the actual-expense method. The simplified method would be 155 sq ft * $5 = $775 — in your case the actual method (rent share) gives a much larger deduction, so the actual method is likely better.
- Self-employment tax and deductions: If you have net self-employment earnings of $400 or more, you’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE). Half of your SE tax is an “above-the-line” deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Interest and investment income: The $890 from Discover Bank is taxable interest and should be reported on Form 1040 (and on Schedule B only if your total interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or other triggers apply). The $195 from TD Ameritrade is reportable investment income (dividend or other) — include whichever 1099(s) you receive (1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B as applicable).
- Charitable gift: $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders is a charitable contribution. It is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt you mentioned.
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (up to $2,500 per year) for interest paid during the tax year on qualified student loans. Important: it only applies to interest you paid in the tax year you’re filing for (i.e., interest paid in 2024 for the 2024 return). You mentioned $4,400 (Navient, 2016–2019) — if that $4,400 is interest paid in prior years, it is NOT deductible on your 2024 return. If, instead, you paid $4,400 in 2024, the maximum deductible amount on 2024 returns is $2,500 (and that deduction phases out at higher MAGI for married filing jointly). You’ll need Form 1098-E from the loan servicer showing interest paid in 2024 to claim the deduction.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- For married filing jointly, the standard deduction is large (historically in the high $20k’s; it is inflation-adjusted each year). With only a $2,000 charitable gift and no mortgage interest, limited state/local taxes info, and no other large itemized categories listed, you’ll most likely be better off taking the standard deduction. The main reason to itemize would be if your total Schedule A deductions (charity + mortgage interest + large state/local taxes up to the SALT cap + medical over threshold, etc.) exceed the standard deduction.
- NOTE: the rent you pay and the home-office portion of rent are not Schedule A itemized deductions — they are business expenses deductible on Schedule C (if you are self-employed). That reduces your AGI regardless of whether you itemize.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the web design income and business expenses including the home-office portion.
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as the deductible part of SE tax and student loan interest (if eligible).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (for your $2,000 charitable gift and any other itemizable items).
- Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend income exceeds $1,500 or there are other reporting triggers.
- Form 8995 / 8995-A — possibly, if you want to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) from your Schedule C net income — eligibility depends on income and other factors.
- Form 1040-ES — if you need to make estimated quarterly tax payments for your self-employment income (likely recommended if no sufficient withholding).
- Retain supporting forms you’ll receive: 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC (if clients issued), 1099-INT (Discover), 1099-DIV / 1099-B (TD Ameritrade), 1098-E (student loan interest if paid in 2024).

Illinois state return
- IL-1040 (Illinois individual income tax return). Illinois starts from your federal AGI with specific additions/subtractions — include your federal-reported interest and business income as required. (If you want, I can list common Illinois additions/subtractions based on your details.)

How to treat the home office in practice
- Because your web design income appears to be self-employment, claim the home office on Schedule C.
- Use the actual-expense method unless your only expenses are minor — given your rent, actual method (business share of rent + utilities) yields a much bigger deduction than the simplified method.
- Keep records that show exclusive and regular business use (floorplan, photos, contemporaneous records of usage), rent/utility bills, receipts.

Other practical points & tax impacts
- Net self-employment income will be subject to self-employment tax (~15.3% on net earnings after the 7.65% reduction factor) and income tax. However the self-employment tax is partially deductible (half) on Form 1040.
- If many of your $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are actually business expenses for the web design activity, put them on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, those are generally NOT deductible for federal returns (suspended under current law).
