Below is a practical, step‑by‑step summary of how to treat each item you listed, whether itemizing makes sense, and which IRS/state forms you’ll likely need. I’ve flagged places where I need a quick clarification from you to be precise.

1) How to report each income item
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($82,350)
  - Report on Form 1040 using the W‑2 (Box 1). Withholding on the W‑2 will be applied against your tax liability.

- Self‑employment (web design) $8,900 (6 clients)
  - Report this on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see home office below and any other business costs).
  - Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 and is also used to compute self‑employment tax on Schedule SE.

- Interest income: $890 (Discover savings) and $195 (TD Ameritrade)
  - Report interest (and any taxable dividends) on Form 1040. If total interest and ordinary dividends > $1,500 you must file Schedule B; your amounts total $1,085 so Schedule B is not required but you still report the amounts.

2) Home office: how to treat it (two options)
You said the home office is 155 sq ft exclusive, total living area 750 sq ft, rent $2,050/month.

- Simplified method (easy)
  - IRS allows $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. Your deduction = 155 × $5 = $775.
  - Claim on Schedule C (no Form 8829). No depreciation or detailed allocation required.

- Actual expense method (likely larger in your case)
  - Business percentage = 155 / 750 = 0.2067 (≈20.67%).
  - Rent deduction (business portion) = $2,050 × 12 × 0.2067 ≈ $5,084 for the year.
  - Because you rent, you won’t claim depreciation for the building, but you can claim the business portion of rent, utilities, internet (business portion), renters insurance, etc.
  - To use the actual method you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
- Which to pick: Based on rent alone the actual method (≈$5,084) gives a bigger deduction than simplified ($775). Use actual if you can/are willing to document the expenses; use simplified if you want less recordkeeping.

Important tax effect: Home office expenses reduce your Schedule C net profit, which (a) reduces your income subject to income tax, (b) reduces self‑employment tax, and (c) may reduce your Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction base.

3) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net Schedule C profit (computed on Schedule SE). Half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
- You may also be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995 or 8995‑A) for your web design business, depending on taxable income and net business profit. With relatively small net profit, you’ll likely be eligible for some QBI deduction — we can estimate if you want.

4) Student‑loan interest: what you can deduct
- Only interest paid during the tax year is potentially deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (maximum $2,500 per return, subject to income phaseouts).
- You wrote $4,400 (Navient, 2016–2019) — I need clarification: was $4,400 the total you paid in 2024, or is that interest paid in prior years? Only interest actually paid in the tax year you’re filing for (2024) can be claimed on the 2024 return.
- Phaseouts apply based on modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). For most married filing jointly taxpayers with the income levels you’ve shown (W‑2 + self‑employment around ~$92k before adjustments) you will likely be under the phaseout and could claim up to the $2,500 maximum (but only for interest paid in 2024).
- The student loan interest deduction is claimed on Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) of Form 1040.

5) Charitable contribution $2,000
- If you itemize, you can deduct the $2,000 cash donation (you said you have a receipt — keep it).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this $2,000 on Schedule A.
- Note: for 2024 the standard deduction amount is higher than in prior years; see next section about itemizing vs standard.

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are unreimbursed employee business expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018 through 2025 under current law (TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions).
- If those $890 are business expenses for your self‑employment (web design) — e.g., software, hosting, supplies used for clients — they are deductible on Schedule C. Please confirm whether they relate to the web design work or to W‑2 employment duties.

7) Itemize vs. Standard deduction — which to take
- I don’t want to state the standard deduction number incorrectly; however, the decision is: compare your total allowable itemized deductions (Schedule A) to the standard deduction for married filing jointly for the 2024 tax year.
- From the items you listed that belong on Schedule A you only have $2,000 (charitable). You would also be able to include state and local income/property taxes (SALT), mortgage interest, medical expenses (subject to a threshold), etc. But you didn’t list those amounts.
- Preliminary conclusion: with only $2,000 in confirmed Schedule A deductions, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction (unless you have significant other deductible items you haven’t listed — e.g., mortgage interest and/or large property/state taxes).
- If you want, give me any mortgage interest, property tax, or state tax totals and I’ll compute the comparison for you.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal and state)
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main form)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for web design income/expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and deductible half
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual home office method
  - Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction if applicable
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest deduction and other adjustments
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (e.g., if your itemized total > standard deduction)
  - Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend totals exceed $1,500 (your current totals don’t)
