Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether itemizing will likely help, and which federal/state forms you’ll use. I’m not a CPA or tax attorney — for final filing or anything complex I recommend confirming with a tax pro or the IRS/IL Dept. of Revenue. A few clarifying questions are below (I’ll need answers to give final numbers).

1) Quick summary of what you can/don’t appear to be able to deduct
- Deductible (federal) items you’ve listed:
  - Self‑employment business expenses for your web design work (report on Schedule C). That includes the home‑office portion of your rent (see calculation below) and any ordinary & necessary business costs (software, supplies, internet portion if used for business, advertising, etc.).
  - Self‑employment tax deduction: you can deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
  - Charitable contribution: $2,000 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible if you itemize, and you have the receipt (good).
  - Interest and brokerage distributions: $890 interest from Discover and $195 from TD Ameritrade are taxable income (reported on 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B as applicable) — not deductions, but must be reported.

- Items that generally are NOT deductible (based on the info you gave):
  - The $890 “unreimbursed expenses” — since 2018 the miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses subject to the 2% floor are suspended through 2025 for most employees. If those expenses were outlays for your W‑2 job, they’re not deductible on federal return. If instead those $890 are business expenses for your web design work, they belong on Schedule C (they would be deductible there).
  - Student loan interest: only interest actually paid in the tax year can be deducted (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts); you cannot deduct interest from prior years now. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from your servicer showing interest paid in the year. If you truly paid $4,400 in student loan interest in 2024, only up to $2,500 (and subject to MAGI limits) is deductible as an adjustment to income. If the $4,400 is aggregate interest paid in prior years (2016–2019), it’s not deductible on your 2024 return.

2) Home‑office treatment and quick math
- You qualify for the home‑office deduction because you said the 155 sq ft is used exclusively for business.
- Two methods: actual expenses (pro rata share of rent, utilities, etc.) or the simplified method ($5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft).
- Your numbers (rent $2,050/month = $24,600/year):
  - Percentage of home used: 155 / 750 = 0.206666... ≈ 20.67%
  - Actual method — rent portion deductible = 24,600 × 155/750 = $5,084 (this would be entered as a Schedule C expense; you can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., in addition to rent).
  - Simplified method — 155 sq ft × $5 = $775.
- Given rent alone, the actual method ($5,084) gives a much larger deduction than the simplified method ($775). Use the actual method if you want to include your share of utilities, insurance, and if you can substantiate the figures. If you own the home, there are extra depreciation considerations; since you rent, depreciation isn’t relevant.
- Home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (thus reducing income tax and self‑employment tax).

3) Self‑employment tax & reporting
- Your web design income ($8,900) is self‑employment income. You’ll report it on Schedule C (Form 1040). Business expenses (including home office) reduce your net profit.
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income; calculate on Schedule SE. You get to deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- If you expect to owe taxes beyond withholding, make/continue estimated quarterly tax payments using Form 1040‑ES.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction — recommendation
- For 2024, the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is large (you’ll want to confirm the exact dollar on the IRS site; for tax‑year 2024 it’s around $29k).
- Known itemizable amount you provided: $2,000 charitable gift. You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes, or a large amount of state/local taxes.
- Conclusion: it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized deductions as reported here, so you will probably take the standard deduction. You should itemize only if the sum of your itemizable deductions (charitable gifts + state and local taxes up to $10,000 SALT cap + mortgage interest + significant medical expenses above the threshold, etc.) exceeds the standard deduction.
- Action: gather amounts for property tax, state income tax paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, and any other charitable gifts. I can re‑check and compute precisely once you provide them.

5) Forms you’ll need (federal + state)
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return).
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 and business expenses (home office portion, other business costs).
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculate SE tax.
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you use the actual method to calculate the home‑office deduction. If you use the simplified method, you don’t file Form 8829; instead include the simplified deduction directly on Schedule C.
  - Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for the student loan interest deduction (if eligible) and the half SE tax deduction flows here.
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize instead of taking the standard deduction.
  - Forms you should receive/attach info from: W‑2 (Riverside Hospital), 1099‑INT (Discover), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (TD Ameritrade if applicable), any 1099‑NEC for self‑employment income (if you received), and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement, if interest paid in the year).
- State:
  - Illinois Form IL‑1040 for your IL return. Report W‑2 wages, interest, and self‑employment income as required by Illinois rules (Illinois taxes interest). Check state rules for deductions/credits.

6) Documentation to keep
- Receipts for charitable contributions (your $2,000 receipt).
- Records for all business income and expenses (invoices, client payment records, bank deposits).
- Proof of rent paid (lease, canceled checks or bank statements) and utility bills if you claim utilities as part of home‑office actual expenses.
- Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing student loan interest paid in the year (if you paid interest in 2024).
- W‑2, 1099s.

7) Clarifying questions I need to give precise guidance / compute amounts
- Did you or your spouse have any other income (spouse W‑2, unemployment, retirement, etc.)? Married filing jointly means combined MAGI matters for phaseouts.
- Did you actually pay $4,400 of student loan interest in 2024, or is $4,400 the total interest you paid in years 2016–2019? (Only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible.)
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” related to the web design business (so they belong on Schedule C) or are they unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 job (not deductible)?
- Do you have mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state income tax payments you want to include for itemizing?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC, 1099‑K, or other forms for your web design clients?

8) Offer
If you tell me:
- whether the $4,400 student interest was paid in 2024 (and whether you have a 1098‑E),
- whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business or employee,
- whether your spouse has income, and
- any mortgage interest / property tax / state tax amounts,
I can run a quick draft calculation showing:
- tentative taxable income,
- whether itemizing or standard is better,
- estimated self‑employment tax, and
- which exact lines/forms to complete.

Would you like me to run that draft calculation? If so, answer the clarifying questions above (or upload the additional numbers you have).

