Short answer up front
- You should almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) rather than itemizing — the charitable gift you listed ($2,000) by itself is far less than the 2024 MFJ standard deduction.
- Your web‑design income is self‑employment income and will be reported on Schedule C; you’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- You can claim a home‑office deduction for the exclusive 155 sq ft office. Because your rent is fairly large, the regular (actual) method (allocate rent/utilities) will likely give a bigger home‑office deduction than the simplified method, but the actual method requires Form 8829 and is limited to your net business income.
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for student‑loan interest adjustment), Form 8829 (only if you use the actual home‑office method), and Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely). Report interest from Discover/TD Ameritrade on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if total taxable interest/dividends > $1,500). Also file IL‑1040 for Illinois state tax.

Details and calculations (estimates)
1) Income you must report
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040)
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900 — report on Schedule C (gross receipts).
- Interest/dividends: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085 — report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if > $1,500).

2) Business expenses and unreimbursed expenses
- If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are expenses you incurred for the web‑design business (software, hosting, supplies, travel for clients, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce self‑employment net income.
- If the $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) for most taxpayers.

3) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; rental: $2,050/month, 750 sq ft total)
- Percentage of home used for business = 155 / 750 = 31/150 ≈ 20.667%.
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Home‑office portion of rent (actual/regular method) = 20.6667% × $24,600 = $5,084 (approx).
- Simplified method = $5/sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 (max 300 sq ft).
- Which method to use: simplified is easier; actual will likely give a larger deduction here because of your high rent. Actual method requires Form 8829 and you must allocate and substantiate shared expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). The home‑office deduction cannot create or increase a business loss beyond limits — if your home‑office deduction would push your Schedule C to a loss, some of that deduction can be limited and carried forward.

Illustrative comparison (if $890 are business expenses)
- Using actual method (home office = $5,084):
  - Schedule C net profit ≈ 8,900 − 890 − 5,084 = $2,926.
  - Approx. self‑employment tax = 15.3% × 92.35% × 2,926 ≈ $414; half is deductible (~$207).
- Using simplified method (home office = $775):
  - Schedule C net profit ≈ 8,900 − 890 − 775 = $7,235.
  - Approx. self‑employment tax ≈ $1,024; half deductible (~$512).

4) Student loan interest $4,400
- Only student loan interest actually paid in the tax year is eligible as an above‑the‑line deduction (maximum $2,500 in most cases). If the $4,400 refers to interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019), you cannot deduct past years’ interest on your 2024 return — only interest you paid in 2024 can be deducted.
- The student loan interest deduction phases out at higher incomes (for MFJ there is a phaseout range — check the 2024 MAGI limits). With the approximate income shown here, you will likely be under the MFJ phaseout thresholds, but confirm your exact MAGI.

5) Charitable gift $2,000
- Cash gift to qualified charity: deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). Because your itemizable deductions shown are small (only $2,000 listed), the standard deduction (2024 MFJ — $29,200) will almost certainly be larger, so you will take the standard deduction and not deduct the charitable gift on Schedule A. (Keep your receipt for records; you can carry out other planning if you have more deductible items.)

6) Recommendation re: itemize vs standard
- Based on the numbers you gave, standard deduction is the better choice for 2024 (standard deduction for married filing jointly in 2024: $29,200). Your listed itemizable items ($2,000 charity) are far below that.
- The home‑office deduction is a business deduction and does not hinge on itemizing — it reduces your Schedule C net and therefore AGI, regardless of whether you itemize.

7) Forms you will likely prepare and where things go
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report the $8,900 business income and business expenses (including home‑office deduction).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net self‑employment income.
- Form 8829 — only if you use the regular (actual) home‑office method to compute the business portion of rent/utilities/home expenses. If you use simplified method, you do not file Form 8829.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest deduction goes here).
- Schedule B — only necessary if your total interest and dividend income > $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts.
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely based on current numbers).
- IL‑1040 — Illinois state individual return (report W‑2, interest, business income; IL has its own rules for deductions/credits).
- Keep receipts: charitable donation receipt, documentation for student loan interest (Form 1098‑E if provided), proof of business expenses, documentation showing exclusive use of home office (floor plan, photos, proof of square footage), records for rent and utilities.

Other practical notes
- If you use the actual method for the home office, save copies of lease/rent receipts and utility bills and be prepared to show the exclusive and regular business use of that 155 sq ft.
- Make sure the $890 unreimbursed expenses are classified properly (business vs employee). If they’re business expenses, include them on Schedule C. If they’re employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they’re not deductible unless you have a special situation (e.g., certain reimbursed expenses for performing artists, reservists, etc.).
- If part of the TD Ameritrade amount was a 1099‑B capital gain or dividends, those are reported differently — tell me what Form 1099 you received.
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe estimated tax or want to increase W‑2 withholding to cover the added income and SE tax.

Next steps / clarifying questions I need
1. Were the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses of your web‑design business? If yes, what are they (software, hosting, equipment)?
2. Was the $4,400 student‑loan interest paid in 2024, or are those payments from earlier years (2016–2019)? Did you receive a Form 1098‑E for any student‑loan interest paid in 2024?
3. What exactly is the $195 from TD Ameritrade — interest, dividends, or capital gains (did you receive a 1099‑B, 1099‑DIV, or 1099‑INT)?
4. Do you want me to run more precise calculations (AGI, taxable income, estimated tax) using either the actual or simplified home‑office method, once you confirm the above?

Disclaimer
I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent. This is general guidance based on the numbers you shared. For filing and to ensure you follow all IRS rules (especially for home‑office substantiation and student‑loan interest phaseouts), I recommend confirming with a tax professional or your preparer. If you want, I can draft a summary email you can send to your tax preparer with these numbers and questions.

