Thanks — this is enough to give practical, accurate guidance and a short checklist of next steps. I’ll summarize what you can/don’t can deduct, how to treat the home office, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, which federal/state forms you’ll need, and a few recordkeeping / next-step items. I have a couple of quick clarification questions at the end that will change some specifics.

Summary of your situation (what I used)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly, federal and Illinois resident (Chicago, IL).  
- W-2 wages: $82,350 (Riverside Hospital).  
- Self-employment (web design) gross receipts: $8,900 (6 clients).  
- Interest/dividend income: $890 (Discover savings) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085.  
- Home: 750 sq ft; home office = 155 sq ft, used exclusively for business. Rent = $2,050/month.  
- Other amounts: $4,400 reported student-loan interest (Navient — note: see clarification below), $2,000 charitable cash gift (Doctors Without Borders, you have receipt), $890 “unreimbursed expenses” (need clarification whether business-related).

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment/business expenses (Schedule C): You must report the $8,900 gross on Schedule C. Any ordinary and necessary business expenses directly related to the web‑design business (software, hosting, domain fees, supplies, sub‑contractors, business portion of phone/internet, etc.) are deductible on Schedule C. If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses (rather than unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your business net income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 hospital job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act).
- Home office deduction (only if you are self‑employed / the space is for your business): Because you run the web‑design work yourself, you can claim a home‑office deduction for the exclusive 155 sq ft space. Two ways to calculate (pick the method that gives the larger deduction):
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (no Form 8829 needed; report on Schedule C).
  - Regular method: business-use percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.6667% ≈ 20.67%. You can allocate deductible rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. Example using only rent: annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 → rent portion allocable to office = $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,086. You would also allocate a portion of utilities, internet, and other shared home expenses to increase the deduction. Use Form 8829 to compute regular-method amounts. Because your rent alone gives a larger deduction than the simplified method, the regular method will likely be better — but include utilities and other shared expenses to maximize the benefit.
- Self‑employment tax deduction: Half of your self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1). Important limits:
  - The maximum allowed deduction in a tax year is $2,500 (even if you paid $4,400).
  - Eligibility phases out at higher MAGI thresholds and is disallowed if married filing separately. Also, the deduction is for interest you actually paid in the tax year. (See Clarification question below about the dates/years of the interest you cited.)
- Charitable contributions: Cash gifts to qualified public charities (Doctors Without Borders) are deductible if you itemize (Schedule A); with a receipt you can substantiate it. If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct this (except certain limited above-the-line charity provisions that applied only in special years — not applicable generally for 2024).
- Interest and dividends: The $1,085 interest/dividend total is taxable and reported on your Form 1040 (no Schedule B needed unless total interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or other special circumstances).

What you likely cannot deduct
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses tied to your W‑2 hospital job are not deductible for 2018–2025 (unless you qualify for an exception such as certain performing-artist rules).
- Student loan interest paid in prior years (e.g., 2016–2019) cannot be deducted on your 2024 return — only interest you paid in the tax year is deductible. (See clarification request below.)

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly): $29,200 (IRS inflation-adjusted number for the 2024 tax year).  
- From the items you listed, the only certain itemized deduction is the $2,000 charitable gift. You likely would also have state and local taxes (SALT) and possibly property tax or mortgage interest — you didn’t list property tax or mortgage interest. Even with SALT up to the $10,000 cap, and charitable $2,000, it’s very likely your itemized total will be well under $29,200. Therefore you will most likely take the standard deduction. If you do have other large deductible items (substantial mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, or large state/local taxes up to the cap), re-check — otherwise standard deduction is almost certainly better.

How the home office interacts with itemizing
- The home office deduction for a self‑employed person is taken on Schedule C and is NOT part of Schedule A itemized deductions. So taking the standard deduction on Schedule A does not stop you from claiming a home-office deduction on Schedule C if you are self‑employed.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to report additional income (e.g., taxable interest if needed, but interest > schedule? small) and adjustments (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax).  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 self‑employment gross receipts and business expenses. Home‑office deduction also reported here (or via Form 8829).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax on net Schedule C profit.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the regular method for the home office; not needed for the simplified method.  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize. (Given the numbers you provided, you will likely not file Schedule A.)  
- Form 1040-ES — use this for quarterly estimated tax payments if you expect to owe significant tax because of self‑employment income (and insufficient withholding). If you will owe $1,000+ when you file, you should consider quarterly payments to avoid penalties.

Illinois state forms
- IL-1040 — Illinois individual income tax return. Illinois starts from federal AGI (with some state-specific additions/subtractions). Illinois has its own rules and rates; you’ll report your federal AGI and follow IL guidance. (If you owe additional tax because of self‑employment income, you’ll pay IL tax on that as well.)

Other practical tax points
- Self‑employment tax: Net self‑employment earnings are subject to Social Security and Medicare (SE tax ≈ 15.3% on net earnings up to the Social Security wage base for the Social Security portion). You can deduct half the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Estimated tax: Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated tax (Form 1040‑ES). If you do not have enough withholding across the year, you could owe penalties.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts/records for business expenses, the 1099s (if you received any), bank 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV forms, the charity receipt, and documentation for student loan interest (Form 1098‑E if available) and home expenses (rent receipts, utility bills).
- If you expect your AGI to be under the student-loan phaseout threshold (likely in your case given the numbers you gave), your $4,400 reported interest paid will be limited to $2,500 allowed maximum for the deduction — but confirm the payments were actually made in the tax year you’re filing for.

Quick numerical examples (illustrative)
- Home office: simplified = $775. Regular (rent only): annual rent $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,086. Regular method appears better based on rent alone; add utilities and you get more. Use Form 8829 for exact numbers.
- Student loan interest: you listed $4,400. Maximum deductible = $2,500 (if you meet the income rules). If that $4,400 was paid in prior years (2016–2019) it is not deductible for 2024.
- Itemize vs standard: standard = $29,200 (MFJ). Your known itemized piece (charity $2,000) is far below that; unless you have other large itemizable items (mortgage interest, large property taxes, huge medical expenses), take standard deduction.

Clarifying questions (these affect exact treatment)
1. Is the $8,900 web‑design amount gross receipts for 2024 and are you operating as a sole proprietor (no separate corporation)? Also: do you have ordinary business expenses beyond the $890 (hosting, software, contractor pay, supplies)? Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business-related or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?  
2. Did you actually pay $4,400 of student‑loan interest in the 2024 tax year, or is that total interest paid across earlier years (2016–2019)? (Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return.)  
3. Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or large state/local taxes withheld or paid that would materially add to itemized deductions? (This affects whether you should itemize.)  

Recommended next steps
- If you want, I can: (a) calculate a draft Schedule C and approximate tax estimate if you provide business expenses, or (b) prepare a checklist of documents and exact line-by-line guidance for each form you’ll need.  
- If you want definitive answers on phase‑outs and exact tax owed, consider sharing a few more numbers (business expense detail, exact amount of state tax paid/property tax, confirmation of student‑loan payment year) or consult a CPA/tax preparer — especially because self‑employment and home‑office interactions can get complex.

If you’d like, tell me the answers to the three clarifying questions and I’ll run through the numbers (net Schedule C profit, approximate self‑employment tax, whether the regular home‑office method or simplified yields a larger deduction, and a recommendation whether to itemize vs take the standard deduction).

