Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk through this and give clear next steps. I’ll flag a few questions where I need clarification to be precise.

Summary of what you told me (used below)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (federal) — Chicago, IL for state filing.
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $82,350
- Self-employment (web design, 6 clients): $8,900
- Interest: $890 (Discover savings)
- Brokerage (TD Ameritrade): $195
- Rent: $2,050/month for a 750 sq ft home (so $24,600/year)
- Home office: 155 sq ft used exclusively for business
- Charitable gifts: $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders (you have receipt)
- Student loan interest: $4,400 (Navient, 2016–2019) — need clarification (see below)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $890 — need clarification whether these are business expenses for the web design or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job

Quick top-level conclusions
- You should report the W-2, interest, and brokerage amounts on Form 1040. Self-employment income is reported on Schedule C; that also lets you claim business-related deductions (including a home-office deduction and other business expenses).
- The home-office deduction is available only for self-employed (Schedule C) — it is not available for W-2 employees. Because you rent and your office is 155/750 ≈ 20.67% of the home, the actual-method home-office deduction for rent alone would be about $5,084 for the year (24,600 × 155/750). The simplified method would give $5 × 155 = $775. The actual method is likely more beneficial here if there are no other disqualifying facts.
- The $2,000 charitable gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If your total itemizable deductions are less than the standard deduction, do not itemize.
- The “student loan interest” deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (Schedule 1) up to $2,500 for interest paid in the tax year — but you can only deduct interest paid in the tax year you are filing for (2024). If the $4,400 was interest paid in 2016–2019, those amounts cannot be deducted on your 2024 return. I need to know whether any or all of that $4,400 was interest actually paid in 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (for a W-2 job) are not deductible for federal returns for tax years through at least 2025 (TCJA). If the $890 are business expenses tied to your self-employment work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Estimated totals and effects (illustrative)
- Total gross income (before adjustments): 82,350 + 8,900 + 890 + 195 = $92,335.
- If you take the actual home-office deduction (rent portion only) and have no other business expenses:
  - Home-office deduction (actual, rent portion): ≈ $5,084.
  - Net self-employment income ≈ 8,900 − 5,084 = $3,816.
  - That net SE income is subject to self-employment tax (and then half of that SE tax is deductible as an adjustment). You’d also get a potential QBI (20%) deduction on qualified business income (subject to rules), which could reduce taxable income further.
- If you instead use the simplified home-office method your home-office deduction = $775, so net SE income = 8,900 − 775 = $8,125.
- Which is better depends on full actual expenses. Based on rent alone, the actual method (≈$5,084) is much better than the simplified $775.

Itemize vs Standard deduction
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is significantly larger than in prior years; given the items you listed only $2,000 in charitable giving (plus any SALT, mortgage interest, medical, etc. you may have) you will most likely be better off taking the standard deduction for federal taxes.
- Important: home-office deduction is not part of itemized deductions — it reduces your business income (Schedule C) whether or not you itemize.
- Student loan interest (if paid in 2024 and within the phase-out limits) is an above-the-line adjustment — also not an itemized deduction — so you can take it even if you take the standard deduction (subject to eligibility and phaseout).
- Bottom line: unless you have other large itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state and local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses over the floor, or more charitable giving), you’ll likely take the standard deduction.

How to treat each item (practical filing guidance)
- W-2 wages: Report on Form 1040 (attach W-2 info).
- Self-employment (web design):
  - Report gross receipts $8,900 on Schedule C.
  - Deduct business expenses on Schedule C (home office, supplies, software, internet portion used for business, client expenses, mileage where applicable). If your $890 is business expense for web design, include it here.
  - Home-office options:
    - Actual method: use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute allowable portion of rent, utilities, etc. (your rent portion alone ≈ $5,084).
    - Simplified: claim $5 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
  - Self-employment tax: compute on Schedule SE (you’ll owe Social Security and Medicare on net SE income). Half of SE tax is an adjustment on Schedule 1.
  - Potential QBI (Section 199A): you may be eligible for a deduction up to 20% of qualified business income — usually beneficial, but subject to income and other rules.
- Interest and dividends (Discover $890, TD Ameritrade $195): report these on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099-INT and 1099-DIV or 1099-B statements from brokerage for 2024).
- Student loan interest: deductible up to $2,500 as an adjustment on Schedule 1, line for student loan interest — only if interest was paid in the 2024 tax year and you meet the MAGI limits. If the $4,400 was paid in earlier years, it is not deductible on your 2024 return.
- Charitable donation ($2,000): report on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this on your federal return (some special temporary rules applied in prior years, but generally not for 2024).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for federal if they are for your W-2 employment. If they are business expenses for your web design, include them on Schedule C.

Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (with any schedules)
  - Schedule 1 (Adjustments to income — student loan interest, half SE tax, etc.)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your web design income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you choose the actual home-office method
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize
  - Form 8995 or 8995-A (for QBI deduction) if applicable (Form 8995 is the simpler worksheet used if your income is under the thresholds)
  - 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC if you received any from clients — you must report the income whether or not you received 1099s
  - 1099-INT, 1099-DIV (for the bank and brokerage)
- Illinois state:
  - IL-1040 — Illinois return. Illinois has different rules and a flat income tax; check IL instructions for deductibility of student loan interest or charitable contributions. (You’ll also include federal AGI adjustments per IL rules.)

Recordkeeping you should keep
- Rent receipts or canceled checks showing monthly rent paid ($2,050 × 12)
- Measurement and any photos of the home office, proof it is used exclusively and regularly for business
- Receipts for the $2,000 charitable contribution (acknowledgement from Doctors Without Borders)
- 1099s, bank/broker statements, W-2
- Proof of student loan interest paid in the tax year (Form 1098-E for the year you paid interest) — if you don’t have a 2024 Form 1098-E showing interest paid in 2024, the $4,400 likely cannot be deducted for 2024

Questions I need to answer precisely and next steps I recommend
1. Was any portion of the $4,400 student loan interest paid in 2024 (the tax year you are filing for)? Or is that total interest paid during 2016–2019? If it was not paid in 2024, you cannot deduct it on your 2024 return.
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses related to the web design business (i.e., business expenses you paid out of pocket) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job? If they are business expenses, add them to Schedule C.
3. Do you have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income taxes withheld, large medical expenses, or other charitable gifts)? That affects the itemize vs standard decision.
4. Did any clients issue 1099-NEC to you? (You must still report the $8,900 even if no 1099s were issued.)
5. Would you like me to prepare an estimated calculation (with approximate tax and self-employment tax) based on assuming the home office actual method vs simplified method? I can run both scenarios to show which yields the larger tax benefit.

Final recommendation (concise)
- Most likely: take the standard deduction for federal (unless you have larger itemized items not mentioned). Still claim Schedule C for the web-design income and take the home-office deduction (actual method looks better here) using Form 8829, file Schedule SE for self-employment tax, claim student loan interest on Schedule 1 only if it was paid in 2024 and you meet income limits, and report your bank and brokerage interest on Form 1040.
- File Illinois IL-1040 as required, and check state rules for deductions.
- Keep receipts and documentation for all business expenses, home-office allocation, and the charitable donation.
- If you want, I can calculate estimated federal tax and SE tax under both home-office methods — tell me the answers to questions 1–4 above and I’ll do the math and list the exact forms and line numbers to use.

