- The bank/broker amounts ($890 Discover, $195 TD Ameritrade) are taxable interest/dividends and must be reported on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if the total interest/dividends or other triggers require it). Expect 1099-INT / 1099-B / 1099-DIV from those payers.
- Student-loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible and the maximum annual deduction is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). You mentioned $4,400 from 2016–2019 — that cannot be deducted on your 2024 return. If you paid interest in 2024, you can potentially deduct up to $2,500 (you’ll need Form 1098-E from the lender).
- Charitable gift: $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize. Because your likely itemizable deductions (as stated) are small, you will almost certainly be better off taking the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (standard deduction is $29,200 for 2024); if you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct that $2,000 gift on your 2024 return.
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040; Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business); Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax); Form 8829 (if using the regular home-office method); Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student-loan interest if applicable) — and any 1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-NEC / 1098-E you receive. Possibly Schedule B only if interest/dividend totals exceed the threshold or other triggers.

