- Home office percent = 155 / 750 = 20.67%
- Regular home-office deduction (your rent): 24,600/year × 20.6667% ≈ $5,086.67
  - Business expenses total = $5,086.67 + $890 = $5,976.67
  - Net self-employment income ≈ 8,900 − 5,976.67 = $2,923
  - Self-employment tax ≈ $413 (Schedule SE); half (~$206.50) is an adjustment to income
  - Total income before adjustments ≈ W-2 (82,350) + SE net (2,923) + interest (1,085) = ≈ $86,358
  - AGI after half SE tax ≈ $86,152 (before any student-loan-interest adjustment)
  - If you qualify for full $2,500 student-loan-interest deduction, AGI ≈ $83,652
  - Standard deduction (MFJ 2024) = $29,200 → taxable income ≈ $54,452 (with full student-interest), or ≈ $56,952 (without student-interest)
- Simplified home-office (for comparison): 155 × $5 = $775 → yields a much higher net SE income and higher SE tax — so regular method is much better here.

Important notes & limits
- Home-office deduction requires the space be used exclusively and regularly for business. You stated it is exclusive — keep records/photos and a floor plan.
- The regular home-office method requires Form 8829. If you rent, you can use the prorated share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (depreciation applies to owners only).
- Home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business. In your case the regular-method deduction still leaves positive net profit so it should be allowable.
- Student-loan interest deduction only covers interest paid in the tax year and is capped at $2,500 and phases out at higher MAGI (verify your MAGI against IRS phaseout limits — married filing jointly phaseout thresholds are higher than single). You’ll need Form 1098‑E to substantiate.
- Charitable contributions require receipts for itemizing. No above-the-line charitable deduction applies for most taxpayers in 2024 (so if you take the standard deduction you can’t deduct the $2,000 gift).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for someone who is an employee) are not deductible for 2018–2025. Your $890 is deductible only if they are business expenses of your self-employment (Schedule C). If they were unreimbursed expenses from a W-2 job, they are not deductible.

Forms checklist
- Form 1040
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report web design income and business expenses
- Form 8829 — expenses for business use of your home (regular method)
- Schedule SE — compute self-employment tax
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest, half SE tax flows to adjustments)
- 1099-NEC(s) if clients issued them (also report income even if no 1099)
- 1099-INT (Discover), 1099-DIV / 1099-B (TD Ameritrade) as applicable; Schedule B only if required by thresholds
- Form 1098-E if you paid student loan interest in 2024
- Keep receipts for the $2,000 charitable gift in case you itemize in a year you choose to itemize

