What you can deduct (federal) — short version
- Self‑employment business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. That includes ordinary & necessary expenses for your web‑design work (the $890 if those are business expenses), supplies, software, domain hosting, etc.
- Home‑office deduction: available only for self‑employed business use of home (not for W‑2 unreimbursed employee expenses). Two methods (explained below).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings; you can deduct half of that as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is an adjustment to income (on Schedule 1) if you actually paid that interest during the tax year and you meet the income limits. (You cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on the current year return.)
- Charitable cash gift: $2,000 is an itemized deduction on Schedule A (you must have receipt). If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct this.
- Interest/dividends/1099s: report interest and broker income on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if interest + dividends > $1,500 or other reasons). All income must be reported even if you didn’t receive a 1099.

Important clarifying question about the student loan interest
- You listed $4,400 (Navient, 2016–2019). Only interest actually paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible now, and the maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). If those $4,400 are prior‑year interest, you can’t deduct them on 2024 returns. Please confirm the $4,400 is interest paid in 2024 or clarify the years.

Home‑office: two methods and a comparison (your numbers)
- Home office percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.67% (≈20.7%)
- Rent (annual) = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600

1) Simplified method
- Flat $5 per square foot × 155 sq ft = $775 (maximum 300 sq ft).
- No Form 8829. Easier recordkeeping.

2) Regular method (actual expenses)
- Deduct business portion of actual home costs. If you only have rent, the deductible portion of rent = $24,600 × 20.67% ≈ $5,086.
- If you also have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., 20.67% of those are also deductible.
- To claim the regular method you generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the deduction that flows to Schedule C.
- Limitation: the deductible home‑office expenses cannot create a business loss beyond other Schedule C income — excess home office expenses may be carried forward.

Direct numeric comparison (based on the information you gave)
- Business gross: $8,900
- Other business expenses (you said $890) assumed deductible on Schedule C.

If simplified method:
- Home office = $775 + other expenses $890 → total = $1,665
- Net self‑employment income = $8,900 − $1,665 = $7,235

If regular method (rent only):
- Home office = $5,086 + other $890 → total = $5,976
- Net self‑employment income = $8,900 − $5,976 = $2,924

Self‑employment tax (approximate)
- SE tax base = 92.35% of net self‑employment income
- Simplified estimate: SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × $7,235) ≈ $1,022
- Regular estimate: SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × $2,924) ≈ $413
- You may then deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.

Which home‑office method is likely best for you?
- With rent of $24,600 and a 20.7% business use, the regular method gives you a substantially larger deduction ($≈5,086 vs $775). Based on the numbers you shared, the regular method looks more favorable.
- A caveat: the regular method requires Form 8829 and supporting records (proof of rent, utilities, etc.). Also the home‑office portion cannot create or increase a Schedule C loss beyond limits (but in your case the deduction stays within the business income, so it’s OK).
- If you want simplicity and minimal recordkeeping, simplified is easier, but in your case it’s likely less tax‑advantageous.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) = $29,200.
- Your known itemizable deduction is $2,000 (charitable). You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes, or state/local taxes paid. Because the SALT cap is $10,000, many taxpayers find they still take the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large mortgage interest, property taxes, state income taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, etc.), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- The home‑office deduction does not affect the decision to itemize — it reduces Schedule C business income (thus reduces AGI) whether or not you itemize.

Other items you may qualify for
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (section 199A): up to 20% of qualified business income from your web‑design business may be deductible on Form 8995 or 8995‑A (subject to limits based on taxable income and the nature of the business). With small net QBI you may be eligible for a 20% deduction — your preparer or tax software can calculate this.
- Student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) — confirm year and MAGI phase-out rules.

Forms you will generally need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments): reporting adjustments like student loan interest deduction and the deductible half of self‑employment tax
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the regular home‑office method
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for QBI deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable contribution of $2,000 would be claimed here)
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend rules trigger it (generally if interest + dividends > $1,500)
- Keep Forms W‑2, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if you received them), and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement if you have it)

Illinois state return
- File IL‑1040 (Illinois return). Illinois starts with federal AGI, then applies state additions/subtractions; you’ll report your federal AGI and taxable income per IL rules and pay the Illinois flat tax (verify current rate). State rules for deductions differ from federal, so check IL guidance or ask your preparer.

Other practical points
- If your web‑design work is self‑employment, track all 1099‑NEC amounts and all expenses. You must report all income even if no 1099 issued.
- If you expect to owe significant tax because of self‑employment (SE tax + income tax), consider making quarterly estimated tax payments using Form 1040‑ES to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Keep receipts for the charitable gift ($2,000 receipt) and for all business and home‑office related expenses (rent receipts, utility bills, insurance, repairs).
- If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” were employee expenses (for wages) rather than business expenses, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 for most employees. If they were business expenses for your self‑employed work, include them on Schedule C.

Next steps I can help with
