I can walk you through what’s likely deductible, whether you should itemize, how to handle the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll need — plus a few questions so I can be more precise.

Quick bottom line
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 (because the only clear itemizable amount you listed is $2,000 in charitable gifts).  
- Your web design income (self‑employment) should be reported on Schedule C; you’ll owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) and can claim a home‑office deduction for the business.  
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500 limit and subject to income limits) if you actually paid that interest in 2024.  
- You’ll need Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments), and either Form 8829 (actual home office) or use the simplified home‑office method on Schedule C. File an Illinois IL‑1040 for state taxes.

What you can (probably) deduct
- Salary from Riverside Hospital (W‑2 $82,350): report on Form 1040; no deduction for salary itself.
- Self‑employment (web design) $8,900: report on Schedule C (profit/loss). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against that income (supplies, advertising, part of internet if used for business, etc.). This reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security/Medicare on net self‑employment income): you’ll calculate this on Schedule SE. One‑half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Interest income $890 (Discover savings) and $195 (TD Ameritrade): report on Form 1040. If they are interest/dividend amounts, they’re reported on 1040 (Schedule B only if total interest/dividends > $1,500). If the $195 is capital gains or dividends, treat accordingly (1099‑DIV / 1099‑B).
- Home‑office deduction (if you are self‑employed and the space is used exclusively and regularly for the business): yes, you can claim a deduction. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (max 300 sq ft). Easy, no Form 8829.
  - Actual expenses method: allocate actual rent and other allowable home expenses by the business percentage of the home. Your business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.6667% (≈20.67%). Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion allocated to the home office = 24,600 × 20.6667% = $5,084. You can also allocate part of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc. (use Form 8829 to compute). Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business for the year (excess may carry forward if using actual method).
- Charitable gift $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders: deductible if you itemize and have a receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you can’t also deduct this on your federal return (no general above‑the‑line deduction for charitable gifts in typical years).
- Student loan interest $4,400: only interest paid in the tax year is eligible, and the maximum deduction is $2,500 (as an adjustment to income) subject to income phaseouts. Given your reported income (W‑2 + self‑employment + interest), you are likely under the MAGI phaseout threshold for full or partial deduction, but I need confirmation of the actual amount of 2024 interest you paid. (If the $4,400 is not all paid in 2024, you can only deduct what was paid in 2024.)

Items that are generally NOT deductible (federal)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (post‑TCJA, 2018–2025): if the $890 is unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, they are not deductible on your federal return. If that $890 are business expenses for your self‑employment work, then yes, they belong on Schedule C.
- Personal living expenses (other than allowed home‑office allocation on Schedule C if self‑employed).

Which option — itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly is typically much larger than $2,000 in charitable giving. Based solely on the items you listed (charity $2,000, no mortgage interest listed, no property tax / state tax amounts provided), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for 2024.  
- If you have additional deductible items not listed (large state and local taxes or mortgage interest, big unreimbursed medical expenses, etc.), then itemizing might be better. If you want, list property taxes, state income taxes paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other charitable gifts and I’ll compare numerically.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest deduction and the deduction for one‑half of SE tax (and other adjustments)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report your web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax and the deductible half
- Either Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual expenses method for home‑office, or use the simplified method on Schedule C (no 8829)
- Schedule B only if total interest/dividends > $1,500 (your amounts are below that threshold)
- Keep and attach supporting 1099s: 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for contract income (if issued), 1099‑INT (bank interest), 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B for brokerage amounts. You may not “attach” these to the return but you must report them and keep them with your records.
State (Illinois)
- IL‑1040 (Illinois individual return). Illinois tax is based on your federal AGI with state rules; you’ll report the federal amounts and any state adjustments. (If you live in Chicago, there is no city income tax to file separately, but you still file IL‑1040.)
Other practical points
- Quarterly estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments for 2024 (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties. If you didn’t make estimated payments during the year and you owe tax, you could be subject to penalty.
- Records to keep: receipts for business expenses and the home office (photos/plans showing exclusive use), Form 1098‑E or lender statement for student loan interest (Navient usually issues 1098‑E showing interest paid in the year), receipts or a bank/credit card record for the $2,000 charitable gift, 1099s from clients/banks/brokerages.
- Home office method choice: if you want the biggest immediate deduction and have adequate net profit, the actual method (rent allocation + utilities etc.) looks more favorable for your numbers (actual: ~$5,084 vs simplified $775), but actual requires Form 8829 and careful recordkeeping. If you prefer simplicity and low audit risk, simplified is easier.

Questions I need to be more precise
1. Is the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” related to your W‑2 hospital job (employee expenses) or to the web‑design business (business expenses)? If business, confirm what they are so they can go on Schedule C.  
2. Did you actually pay $4,400 in student loan interest during 2024? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.) Do you have a 2024 Form 1098‑E from Navient showing interest paid?  
3. What is the $195 from TD Ameritrade (interest, dividends, or capital gains/proceeds)? Do you have a 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B?  
4. Do you have any mortgage interest, property taxes, or state income taxes paid (SALT) that weren’t listed? That could change the itemize vs standard decision.  
5. Do you want me to estimate your federal and Illinois tax liability numerically (rough estimate) based on current numbers and assuming the actual home office method?

If you want, I can:
- Estimate taxes (federal + Illinois) using the assumptions above and show a comparison: (A) standard deduction with simplified home office, vs (B) standard deduction with actual home office, vs (C) itemizing (if you provide SALT/mortgage amounts).  
- Or draft a checklist of documents and line items you’ll need to prepare your return or give to a preparer.

I’m not a CPA — for definitive tax filing or complex situations (especially if your student loan interest payments or other items span multiple years or there are unusual brokerage items), I recommend confirming with a tax professional or using reputable tax software.

