- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), and Schedule A only if you itemize. Also Illinois Form IL‑1040 for state tax.

Detailed explanation and quick calculations

1) Income to report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $82,350 — report on Form 1040 (W-2 attached).
- Self‑employment (web design) income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (profit or loss).
- Interest from Discover savings: $890 — report as taxable interest (Form 1040).
- TD Ameritrade $195 — report (interest/dividend) on Form 1040; you should receive 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV.

2) Business expenses and “unreimbursed expenses”
- If the $890 are costs related to your web design business (software, subscriptions, supplies, travel for clients, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If the $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses (expenses while you were an employee for someone else), those are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under current law.

3) Home‑office deduction (you are self‑employed — so you can take it)
- Qualification: must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business. You said 155 sq ft exclusive, so that sounds like it qualifies.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
     - 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction.
     - No Form 8829 needed.
  b) Actual expense method: allocates portions of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, depreciation) by business percentage.
     - Business % = 155 / 750 = 20.6667% (≈20.67%).
     - Rent: $2,050/month × 12 = $24,600/year → home‑office portion of rent = $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,086.
     - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase the deduction. Use Form 8829 to compute this.
- You must choose either simplified or actual for the year. Based on rent alone, the actual method (≈$5,086) yields a much larger deduction than simplified ($775).

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- The net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax. You pay SE tax on 92.35% of net profit at a combined rate of 15.3% (Social Security + Medicare); half of the SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Example (two scenarios) — assuming $8,900 gross SE and $890 business expenses (so pre‑home office profit = $8,010):
  - Actual home‑office method:
    - Home office deduction ≈ $5,086 → Schedule C net profit ≈ $2,924.
    - Net earnings subject to SE = 2,924 × 0.9235 ≈ 2,700.4.
    - SE tax ≈ 2,700.4 × 15.3% ≈ $413.76 → half deductible ≈ $206.88.
  - Simplified method:
    - Home office deduction = $775 → Schedule C net profit ≈ $7,235.
    - Net earnings = 7,235 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,683.7.
    - SE tax ≈ 6,683.7 × 15.3% ≈ $1,021.80 → half deductible ≈ $510.90.
- Note: SE tax is calculated on Schedule SE; half is reported on Schedule 1 as an adjustment to income.

5) Student loan interest ($4,400 paid)
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line) if you qualify. Eligibility is subject to a MAGI phaseout for joint filers — if your MAGI is below the phaseout range you can take the full $2,500; if above, partially reduced; if very high, you may get none.
- With the incomes you reported (W‑2 and modest SE income), your MAGI is likely well under phaseout thresholds, so you should be able to deduct the full $2,500. (Confirm with exact MAGI rules for 2024 or check with your preparer.)

6) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. You have a receipt — good. If you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct this.

7) Standard deduction vs itemize (what’s likely best for you)
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction (tax year 2024) is higher than it was in prior years — you should confirm the exact IRS published number when you file. With the items you listed, your only clear Schedule A item is $2,000 charitable contribution (plus any state/local tax or mortgage interest you didn’t mention). That is almost certainly less than the standard deduction. 
- Based on the data you provided, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction rather than itemize. The exception would be if you have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other large itemizable amounts that push Schedule A above the standard amount.

8) Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest deduction, half self‑employment tax)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self‑employment income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual home‑office method
  - Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize
- State:
  - Illinois Form IL‑1040 (state return). (Chicago has no separate city income tax to file.)
- Keep 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, W‑2, and receipts.

9) Practical checklist / next steps
- Collect your W‑2, any 1099s (clients may have issued 1099‑NEC), 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV, rent receipts or lease, receipts for donations and business expenses, and a log/photos showing exclusive use of the home office.
- Choose home‑office method: actual (Form 8829) vs simplified. Based on rent alone, actual looks better for you, but check utilities and other home costs too.
- Compute Schedule C both ways (actual vs simplified) — pick the one that gives the larger tax benefit.
- Confirm student loan interest phaseout using your computed MAGI (or ask me to run the numbers if you want me to calculate with any additional items).
- If you expect to owe > $1,000 in tax not covered by withholding, consider estimated tax payments (quarterly) to avoid penalties. With your small net self‑employment profit under the actual home-office method, your extra tax may be small, but calculate carefully.

10) Caveats and recommendation
- Tax law has detailed rules (e.g., what counts as business expense, limitations for the home office, phaseout calculations). I gave estimates above using the numbers you provided — final results depend on exact items, other income, and the precise IRS rules for the tax year.
- I recommend either using tax‑preparation software (it will compute Schedule C/SE and compare methods automatically) or consulting a CPA/tax preparer for filing — especially to prepare Form 8829 correctly and to confirm the student‑loan AMT/MAGI phaseout for 2024.

