Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize, and which federal (and basic Illinois) forms you’ll need. I’ll also point out a few items I need you to clarify to be definitive.

Quick summary / bottom line
- You must report W-2 wages ($82,350) and all interest ($890 + $195) on your Form 1040.
- Your web‑design income ($8,900) is self‑employment income: report it on Schedule C; pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on the net profit.
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive of a 750 sq ft home; rent $2,050/month) likely qualifies — the actual‑expense method will give a much larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Charitable gift ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize. With the items you listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the 2024 married‑filing‑jointly standard deduction (2024 MFJ standard deduction = $29,200), unless you have other significant deductible items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large medical expenses, etc.).
- Student loan interest you listed ($4,400 from 2016–2019) is not automatically deductible now — the deduction only applies to interest you actually paid in the tax year and is limited to $2,500/year (and phased out by income). I need to know whether any of that $4,400 was interest you paid in 2024 and whether you received 1098‑E for 2024.

Details and how each item is treated

1) W-2 wages
- Reported on Form 1040 (the W-2 will show federal/state withholding). No special action beyond normal 1040 reporting.

2) Interest (Discover savings $890; TD Ameritrade $195)
- Taxable interest; report on Form 1040.
- Schedule B is only required if your taxable interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts. Your total interest $1,085 is below $1,500 so Schedule B is not required, but you still report the amounts on Form 1040.

3) Self‑employment income (web design, $8,900)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (hosting, software, supplies, client costs, part of the home office, etc.).
- If those $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your web design work, they belong on Schedule C and reduce your net profit. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are generally not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended under TCJA).
- Self‑employment tax: use Schedule SE to compute SE tax (roughly 15.3% on net earnings from self‑employment). You may deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040.

4) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; home = 750 sq ft; rent = $2,050/mo)
- Because you use the space exclusively and regularly for your web‑design business, you likely qualify for the home office deduction (only available for the self‑employed or for certain business owners; not available for employees under TCJA rules).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot, up to 300 sq ft.
     - 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction.
  b) Actual‑expense method: prorate actual home expenses by business use percentage.
     - Business use percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.6667% (≈ 20.67%).
     - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion deductible = $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,085.60.
     - You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs attributable to the business area, etc. That will increase the deduction beyond the rent share.
- Recommendation: With only rent provided, the actual method (≈ $5,086) is much larger than the simplified method ($775). Use actual method unless your recordkeeping is inadequate. For actual method you’ll document rent and utilities; use Form 8829 (or the appropriate place on the Schedule C instructions) to compute and report the deduction. The deduction reduces Schedule C net profit (and therefore reduces both income tax and SE tax).

5) Charitable deduction ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Deductible on your federal return only if you itemize. Keep the receipt (you have it) and a bank record/acknowledgment if needed. For cash gifts of $250 or more you need a written acknowledgment from the charity.
- Charitable gifts do not affect Illinois state tax the same way as federal (see below).

6) Student loan interest ($4,400; Navient, 2016–2019)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500 per year) only for interest paid in the tax year. Interest paid in earlier years is not deductible now. You need Form 1098‑E for interest paid in the tax year to claim this.
- Also subject to income phaseouts (depends on your modified adjusted gross income and filing status). If you didn’t pay student loan interest in 2024, you cannot claim that past interest.

7) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890)
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses for a W‑2 job: not deductible for most taxpayers for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended).
- If they are business expenses for your self‑employment (web design), put them on Schedule C.

8) Will you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- The only clear itemized amount you listed is $2,000 charitable. You didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, or large medical expenses. So with the information provided you will almost certainly be better off taking the 2024 MFJ standard deduction ($29,200).
- Note: even if you take the standard deduction for itemizing purposes, you still must file Schedule C and Schedule SE for self‑employment income.

9) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
