- Is the $4,400 in student-loan interest paid in tax year 2024, or is that interest from 2016–2019 (older years)? Only interest actually paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return. Do you have a 1098‑E for 2024 from Navient?
- Are the $890 of “unreimbursed expenses” for your web‑design work (self‑employment) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job? (After 2017 TCJA, unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers through 2025.)
- Do you have any other potential itemizable deductions (state income tax paid or withheld, real estate tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, large charitable gifts beyond the $2,000)? Also confirm you rent (you said $2,050/month) rather than own.

Assuming the facts you gave and making a few reasonable assumptions, here’s how things generally work and an estimate of outcomes.

1) Which items are deductible and where they go
- W‑2 wages ($82,350): report on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Self‑employment income from web design ($8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., the $890 if those are business expenses).
- Bank and brokerage interest ($890 Discover + $195 TD Ameritrade = $1,085): report as interest income (1099‑INT or 1099‑B/1099‑INT as applicable) on Schedule 1 / Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is an above‑the‑line deduction (entered on Schedule 1) if you actually paid interest in 2024 and your MAGI is below the phaseout limits. You must have the lender’s 1098‑E showing interest paid. If the $4,400 is not 2024 interest, it’s not deductible now.
- Charitable cash gift ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). No general above‑the‑line deduction for 2024 (except limited temporary rules that don’t apply here). Keep your receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890): generally not deductible for 2018–2025 unless you’re in a few specific categories (active military reservist, certain government employees, etc.). If those $890 were business expenses for your web design clients, they belong on Schedule C instead.
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusively used; you pay $2,050/month rent): deductible on Schedule C if you qualify (self‑employed, exclusive and regular use for business, principal place of business). Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet = 155 × $5 = $775 (no Form 8829).
  - Regular (actual) method: deduct your business share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, maintenance, etc., using the business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.666…%. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600; rent portion ≈ $24,600 × 20.666% ≈ $5,087 (plus a prorated share of utilities if not included). Because you rent, you do not claim depreciation on the dwelling; you would use Form 8829 to compute and attach the actual home‑office deduction to Schedule C. Important: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed the gross income from that business (excess may be carried forward).
- Self‑employment tax: net self‑employment income (Schedule C profit) is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You pay about 15.3% on 92.35% of your net SE earnings. Half of your self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1), which reduces AGI.

2) Rough comparison: simplified vs actual home‑office for your numbers
(Assumptions: the $890 are business expenses for web design; you rent; no other business expenses. If the $890 are not business expenses, ignore that line below.)

- Simplified home‑office method:
  - Business income: $8,900
  - Home‑office deduction: $775
  - Other business expenses: $890
  - Net Schedule C profit ≈ 8,900 − 775 − 890 = $7,235
  - SE tax (approx): 0.9235 × 7,235 ≈ 6,685 taxable earnings × 15.3% ≈ $1,022 SE tax
  - Half SE tax deduction ≈ $511

- Actual (rent portion) method:
  - Home‑office rent portion ≈ $5,087 (plus any utility share)
  - Net Schedule C profit ≈ 8,900 − 5,087 − 890 = $2,923
  - SE tax (approx): 0.9235 × 2,923 ≈ 2,699 × 15.3% ≈ $412 SE tax
  - Half SE tax deduction ≈ $206

So actual method will almost certainly give you a larger business deduction (because your rent portion alone exceeds the simplified calculation), reducing taxable income a lot more. Note: the home office deduction is limited to net business income — it can’t create a business loss beyond other business deductions. With the actual method your Schedule C profit is still positive in the example; if it were negative, the excess home‑office portion is carried forward.

3) Itemize vs standard deduction
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly (tax year 2024) is higher than it was in prior years (please confirm current official published amount when you prepare your return or I can check for you). Given only the $2,000 charitable gift and no large mortgage interest or big state/local taxes beyond the SALT cap, you will most likely be better off taking the standard deduction. Example: with only $2,000 in charitable contributions and no large deductible state tax or mortgage interest, itemized deductions will almost certainly be less than the MFJ standard deduction. If you have substantial state income taxes withheld, property taxes, mortgage interest, or unusually large medical expenses, itemizing could become beneficial. I can run the math if you tell me state taxes paid and mortgage interest/property tax paid in 2024.

4) Forms you will typically need to file
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — interest income, student loan interest adjustment, etc.)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your web‑design income and business expenses, including home office deduction (or simplified amount)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home office; if you use the simplified method, you do not file 8829
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if you have student-loan interest paid in 2024
- 1099‑INT / 1099‑B / 1099‑DIV (interest/dividends/brokerage statements) as applicable
- Your W‑2 from Riverside Hospital

Also consider:
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (Qualified Business Income deduction) — you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C subject to limits; it’s worth checking.
- If you received any 1099‑NEC for the web design income, include it on Schedule C (but absence of a 1099 does not mean the income is not taxable).

5) Next steps and recommended actions
- Confirm whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses (if so, include them on Schedule C). If they are employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible for 2024.
- Confirm whether the $4,400 student loan interest was actually paid in 2024 and whether you have 1098‑E for 2024. If you did pay interest in 2024, the deduction max is $2,500 (subject to phaseout by MAGI).
- Gather documentation: receipts for charitable donation, 1098‑E, 1099‑INT, 1099‑B, W‑2, records showing exclusive use of the home office and square footage, rent receipts or canceled checks showing rent paid, receipts for utilities if you claim a prorated utility share.
- Decide whether to use simplified or actual home‑office method. Based on your rent, the actual (regular) method will likely give a larger deduction.
- If you want, I can take the exact amounts you confirm (student loan interest in 2024, whether $890 is business expenses, state taxes paid, any mortgage interest) and produce:
  - a) a line‑by‑line estimate of AGI, taxable income and approximate tax,
  - b) a recommendation (itemize vs standard) with numbers, and
  - c) a checklist of forms/1099s to attach.

If you want me to run the numeric comparison (standard vs itemize, simplified vs actual home office) with the most accurate standard deduction and phaseout thresholds for 2024, confirm the clarifying points above and I’ll compute the estimates and list exactly what you’ll sign/attach.

