Short answer up front
- You’ll report your Riverside W-2 on Form 1040, and your web‑design work on Schedule C (Form 1040). Self‑employment income is subject to income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE).
- You can deduct business expenses on Schedule C (including a home‑office deduction). You then deduct half of the SE tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). Charitable gifts (Doctors Without Borders $2,000) are deductible only if you itemize.
- With the items you listed, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for MFJ unless you have significant additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large medical expenses). I’ll show numbers below so you can see why.
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments), Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) if you claim the QBI deduction, and Schedule A only if you itemize. For IL: IL‑1040 for your state return.

Important: I’m not your CPA/tax preparer. Use this as a practical guide and consider having a tax pro review your return or using tax software to confirm calculations and phaseouts.

Detailed breakdown and rough math (estimates)

1) Gross receipts and interest
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment (web design) gross receipts: $8,900
- Interest: $890 (Discover savings) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085
Total gross before business expenses: $92,335

2) Business expenses (Schedule C)
- You listed $890 unreimbursed expenses. If those are business expenses for your web‑design work, they go on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer, they are NOT deductible (suspended under current law).
- Home office: two methods — simplified or actual. You must use the home office only for business and it must be exclusive and regular (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; good).

Home‑office math:
- Space % = 155 / 750 = 20.666…% (~20.67%).
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (easy, no rent/utility records needed).
- Actual method (rent allocation): rent $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 annual rent × 20.666% = $5,086 allocable to the home office (plus proportional utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you want those included — you didn’t list utilities so I used rent only).

Which is better in your case?
- Simplified gives $775.
- Actual (rent allocation only) gives about $5,086 — substantially larger. If you’re a renter and can document rent and any utilities/insurance, the actual method will likely give a bigger deduction. Use actual only if you keep records.

3) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
You compute net profit on Schedule C (gross receipts minus business expenses including the home‑office deduction and the $890 business expenses). Example scenarios:

A) If you take the simplified home office ($775):
- Net SE income = 8,900 − 890 − 775 = $7,235
- SE tax base = 92.35% of net SE income ≈ $6,682
- SE tax ≈ $6,682 × 15.3% ≈ $1,022
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $511 (above‑the‑line)

B) If you use the actual home office (rent allocation ≈ $5,086):
- Net SE income = 8,900 − 890 − 5,086 ≈ $2,924
- SE tax base ≈ $2,924 × 92.35% ≈ $2,700
- SE tax ≈ $2,700 × 15.3% ≈ $413
- Deductible half ≈ $207

4) Student loan interest
- You listed $4,400 of student loan interest. The student loan interest deduction caps at $2,500 per year (and phases out at higher MAGI for MFJ). Using the AGI estimates above (after subtracting the half‑SE tax), your MAGI will be well below the MFJ phaseout threshold, so you should be able to claim the full $2,500 (not the full $4,400). You need the 1098‑E (or other documentation) from Navient to support the deduction.

5) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. Most likely your total itemized deductions (charity + any state/local taxes + mortgage interest + medical over the threshold) will be less than the standard deduction for MFJ (see next point), so you will probably take the standard deduction and not deduct the $2,000 on your federal return. Keep the receipt (bank record/acknowledgement) for substantiation.

6) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- For married filing jointly (tax year 2024), the standard deduction will likely be far larger than the $2,000 charity you listed. Unless you have significant mortgage interest, large state & local taxes (up to $10,000 SALT cap), or other big itemizable items, take the standard deduction. If you want, I can compute a precise comparison once you provide mortgage interest, property taxes, state tax paid, and any large medical expenses.

7) QBI (section 199A)
- With net self‑employment income, you may be entitled to a Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction of up to 20% of qualified business income. With small net business income like yours, the QBI amount will be modest (e.g., ~20% of the Schedule C net). You’ll report this on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) as part of the 1040 process. This reduces taxable income (not AGI).

8) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — for the $8,900 and expenses/home office.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Schedule 1 — to report adjustments (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax is carried into adjustments as well).
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if claiming QBI deduction.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize.
- Keep 1099s, W‑2, and 1098‑E documentation.

State (Illinois)
- IL‑1040 for Illinois. Illinois taxes income at the state level (you’ll start with your Federal AGI and make state modifications). Illinois does not allow an itemized deduction like federal — it uses the federal AGI as a starting point.

Other practical things to check / actions
- Confirm whether the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses (Schedule C) or employee unreimbursed expenses (not deductible). If they are for the web design business, include them on Schedule C.
- Confirm that the $2,050/month is rent (not mortgage). If mortgage interest paid, you may have sizable Schedule A deductions available.
- Get 1099‑NEC or 1099‑K (if any) from clients — even if you don’t receive a 1099, you still report the income.
- Get 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) and 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV from banks/brokers for the interest.
- Keep rent receipts, utility bills, and any home‑office documentation (sketch of office, square footage) if you use the actual method.
- Consider maxing a traditional IRA or SEP IRA (if you qualify) to reduce AGI further and lower taxable income — especially useful if the student loan phaseout is a consideration.

Would you like me to:
- Recalculate the numbers using the actual 2024 standard deduction amount and exact IRS phaseout thresholds (I can fetch standard deduction/phaseout amounts if you want), OR
- Prepare a worksheet that lists the exact lines/forms to fill in with the numbers you provided, OR
- Help you gather which 1099s/records to collect before you prepare or hand off to a tax preparer?

Tell me which and I’ll prepare the next step.

