I can walk you through how to report everything and give estimates so you can decide (or take to a tax pro). I’m not a CPA — consider this general guidance and confirm with a tax preparer for filing. A few quick clarifying questions up front (answers below will change the results a bit):
- Are the $4,400 in student‑loan interest payments amounts you actually paid in tax year 2024, or are those payments from years 2016–2019 (i.e., not paid in 2024)? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible for that year.
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web‑design work (i.e., things you paid for as a self‑employed contractor), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.)
- Do you rent (you said $2,050/month, so I assume rent) or do you own the home? (That affects depreciation vs. rent allocation.)
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC from clients, 1099‑INT from Discover or 1099‑DIV/1099‑B from TD Ameritrade, or a Form 1098‑E from Navient?

Summary of how to treat each item and the likely outcome

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($82,350): Report on Form 1040 (W‑2 wages).
- Self‑employment (web design) $8,900 (6 clients): Report gross on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see below).
- Discover savings interest $890 and TD Ameritrade $195: Report interest/dividends on Form 1040. If total interest or dividends > $1,500 (or certain other triggers) you’d also use Schedule B, otherwise you report the amounts directly. Expect 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV from those institutions.
- Total gross income (before deductions and SE adjustments) based on numbers you gave: 82,350 + 8,900 + 890 + 195 = $92,335.

2) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Net profit from your Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute self‑employment tax on Schedule SE. You may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Also remember that you may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction — typically up to 20% of qualified business income from Schedule C — subject to limitations. With the income levels you’ve shown, you’ll likely be able to claim QBI; the exact amount depends on final taxable income and business net profit.

3) Home office deduction (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; total living space 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
You have two options — actual method or simplified method:

- Actual (regular) method:
  - Business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.666…% ≈ 20.67%.
  - If you rent and only listed rent as household cost ($2,050 × 12 = $24,600/year), the portion allocable to the home office = 24,600 × 0.2067 ≈ $5,086.
  - You may also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (if partially business), etc., to increase the deduction if applicable.
  - That home office amount is reported on Schedule C as a business expense (it reduces Schedule C net profit).
  - Limitation: the home‑office deduction cannot create or increase a Schedule C loss for the business (i.e., it is limited to the business’s net profit). Unused amounts may be carried forward in certain cases.

- Simplified method:
  - IRS simplified rate is $5 per sq ft (up to 300 sq ft) — for 155 sq ft that is 155 × $5 = $775.
  - Simpler paper trail, but often smaller than the actual allocation when rent is high relative to business income.

Which is better for you (estimates using your numbers)
- If the $890 is a Schedule C business expense (e.g., supplies for the web design work), then:
  - Simplified: Business expenses = $890 + $775 = $1,665 → Schedule C net = 8,900 − 1,665 = $7,235.
  - Actual (rent only used here): Business expenses = $890 + $5,086 = $5,976 → Schedule C net = 8,900 − 5,976 = $2,924.
- Using the actual method and your rent amount gives a much larger home office deduction (≈ $5,086) than the simplified method ($775). So actual method is likely more beneficial here — but it requires documenting rent and how you calculated the allocation, and possibly allocating/including portions of utilities if applicable.
- Self‑employment tax (rough estimate): SE tax ≈ 15.3% of 92.35% of Schedule C net. Example:
  - If net = $2,924 (actual method) → SE tax ≈ $413; half SE deductible ≈ $207.
  - If net = $7,235 (simplified) → SE tax ≈ $1,023; half SE deductible ≈ $511.

4) Student loan interest ($4,400)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible, and the maximum deduction is $2,500 (an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1) subject to income phaseouts.
- If the $4,400 were payments made in previous years (2016–2019), those are not deductible now. If $4,400 was paid in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseout). Given your gross income (~$92k before adjustments), you are likely under the phaseout thresholds for married filing jointly (but we should confirm MAGI).

5) Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Charitable cash gifts are deductible on Schedule A (itemized deductions) if you itemize.
- If you take the standard deduction, you do not deduct the charitable gifts on the federal return (special temporary rules for 2020–2021 aside, for 2018–2025 the standard rule is you either itemize or take the standard).
- Compare total itemized deductions to the standard deduction for married filing jointly: with only $2,000 charitable reported and no other itemized items provided (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income taxes, major medical, etc.), it’s almost certain the standard deduction will be larger. So you’d typically take the standard deduction unless you have significant other deductible items (SALT, mortgage interest, large medical costs, etc.).

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If those $890 are expenses for your self‑employment business, include them on Schedule C (they reduce net profit).
- If instead they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for federal tax purposes for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA), unless you have a specific exception (e.g., certain reservists, qualified performing artists, etc.).

7) Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (individual return).
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — e.g., student loan interest adjustment, other adjustments.
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — reporting the web‑design income and business expenses including home office.
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the half‑SE deduction.
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize.
  - Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend totals exceed the threshold or other triggers.
  - Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (often Form 8995 is sufficient for small taxpayers).
  - Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — keep this for your records; your lender may issue one if interest > $600.
  - 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC — attach/report amounts on the return as required.
- State:
  - Illinois Form IL‑1040. (Illinois taxes interest and most income; state rules differ in some details — check IL instructions or ask a tax pro about state specifics.)

8) Whether to itemize or take standard deduction
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly is generally substantially larger than $2,000 in charitable gifts. With only the $2,000 charitable contribution and no reported mortgage interest, no reported property or large SALT payments, it is almost certainly better to take the standard deduction.
- Exceptions: if you have large state and local taxes (up to $10,000 SALT cap), mortgage interest, or other significant itemized amounts that you didn’t list, then you might beat the standard deduction. If you want, give me estimated property taxes, state withholding for 2024, mortgage interest paid, and any large medical expenses and I’ll compare numerically.

9) Practical recordkeeping & next steps
- Keep receipts, 1099s, and the charitable receipt.
- For home office documentation: floor plan showing exclusive use, calculation of sq ft, proof of rent payments and any utilities you want to allocate.
- For student loan interest: Form 1098‑E from Navient or your records showing how much interest you paid in 2024.
- If you expect to have similar self‑employment income next year and you expect tax due, consider paying estimated quarterly taxes to avoid underpayment penalties.

10) Recommendations
- Based on what you’ve given, my preliminary conclusions:
  - Report W‑2 and interest on Form 1040.
  - Report web design income on Schedule C and claim home office using the actual method (because the rent allocation gives a substantially larger deduction than the simplified method given your rent and office size). Also include the $890 as a Schedule C business expense if it’s business‑related.
  - Claim half of SE tax on Schedule 1.
  - Student loan interest: you can deduct up to $2,500 only if that interest was paid in 2024 (and subject to MAGI limits). Clarify the timing.
  - Charitable $2,000 — likely won’t beat the standard deduction, so you’ll probably take the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable expenses not listed.
  - File Illinois Form IL‑1040 as well.

If you want, I can:
