Thanks — I can walk you through what’s likely deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which IRS/state forms you’ll need. I’ll also list a few clarifying questions at the end because a couple of items need confirming to be precise.

Summary first (short):
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction (MFJ 2024 = $29,200) based on the information you gave.
- Your self-employment activity (web design) is reported on Schedule C; the home-office portion of your rent is a deductible business expense and — with the numbers you gave — the actual-expense method for the home office appears more beneficial than the simplified option.
- You can claim up to $2,500 of student-loan interest as an above-the-line deduction (subject to income phaseouts).
- Your $2,000 charitable gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C and Schedule SE, Form 8829 only if you use the actual home-office method, and Schedule 1 for the student-loan interest adjustment. For Illinois, you’ll file IL-1040.

Detailed breakdown and calculations (using the figures you gave)
1) Income
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Self-employment (web design): $8,900
- Interest: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085
- Total gross income = $82,350 + $8,900 + $1,085 = $92,335

2) Home office (self-employment)
- You said the office is 155 sq ft exclusive in a 750 sq ft rental.
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Business-use share = 155 / 750 = 0.2066667.
- Rent portion for home office (actual method) = $24,600 × 0.2066667 = $5,084.
- Simplified-method deduction = $5 × 155 = $775.

Recommendation: the actual method (deducting $5,084 of rent allocated to the office) gives a far larger deduction than the simplified method ($775), so actual method looks better given the numbers. If you have utility, renter’s insurance, or other household expenses that are allocable to the business, those can increase the deduction further under the actual method. To use the actual method you’ll compute the detailed allocation on Form 8829 (if required) and report the result on Schedule C.

3) Other business expenses
- You listed $890 “unreimbursed expenses.” If these are business expenses related to your web-design work (supplies, software, client travel, hosting, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and reduce net self-employment income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from a job where you were an employee (not self-employed), those are generally NOT deductible on your federal return (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized employee deductions through 2025).

4) Self-employment tax and adjustment to income
- Example (assuming the $890 is a business expense and you use the actual home-office rent portion):
  - Schedule C net profit = $8,900 − $5,084 − $890 = $2,926.
  - Net earnings subject to SE tax = $2,926 × 0.9235 ≈ $2,702.
  - Self-employment tax ≈ $2,702 × 15.3% ≈ $413.
  - Half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income ≈ $206. (Entered on Schedule 1.)

5) Student-loan interest
- You reported $4,400 student-loan interest (Navient). The student-loan interest deduction limit is $2,500 per year (above-the-line), and it is subject to MAGI phaseouts. Only the interest actually paid during the tax year is potentially deductible; the maximum you can claim on your 2024 return is $2,500 (if your MAGI is below the phaseout range). You’ll normally receive a Form 1098-E from the servicer showing interest paid (or you can use your records). If your MAGI is high, the deduction may be reduced or eliminated — we can calculate that if you want to estimate MAGI.

6) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. Because your likely itemized total (from data provided) looks smaller than the MFJ standard deduction ($29,200), itemizing probably won’t help you — unless you have additional deductible items (significant state & local taxes up to $10,000, large medical expenses above threshold, mortgage interest, etc.).

7) Itemize vs. standard (straightforward comparison)
- Standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly, 2024): $29,200.
- Likely itemizable amounts you listed: $2,000 (charity). Student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment, not an itemized deduction. Home-office and business expenses reduce your business profit on Schedule C (not on Schedule A).
- Conclusion: Unless you have additional itemizable deductions not listed (large SALT up to cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.), you should take the standard deduction.

8) Likely approximate bottom-line (example)
- Gross income: $92,335
- Subtract: half of SE tax (~$207) and student loan interest (up to $2,500) as above-the-line adjustments → estimated AGI ≈ $89,628 (if full $2,500 allowed).
- Subtract standard deduction $29,200 → taxable income ≈ $60,428.
(This is an approximation to show scale; exact numbers depend on which expenses are business vs employee and your final allowable student interest deduction.)

Forms you will need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction goes here (and other adjustments)
  - Schedule C — report web-design income and business expenses (home-office deduction and other business expenses go here)
  - Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax
  - Form 8829 — only if you use the actual-expense method to compute business-use-of-home; not needed for simplified method
  - Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (charitable donation reported here)
  - Forms 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received them from clients) and 1099-INT (from Discover/TD) — use them to fill in income lines
  - Form 1098-E from Navient (student loan interest statement) if available
- Illinois:
  - IL-1040 — Illinois individual income tax return (Illinois typically starts with your federal AGI with certain additions/subtractions). Charitable deductions and other items are handled differently by the state; verify state-specific rules.
- Keep records/receipts: receipts for charitable contributions (acknowledgement), records of home-office measurement and allocation, proof of rent and utilities, business receipts and mileage logs (if applicable), Forms 1099 and 1098-E.

Practical next steps / checklist for you
- Confirm whether the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your web-design clients (if yes: include on Schedule C; if no and they’re employee unreimbursed expenses — they are generally not deductible).
- Confirm how much student loan interest you actually paid in the 2024 tax year and whether you have Form 1098-E.
- Gather W-2(s), any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for self-employment income, 1099-INTs for interest, receipts for donations, and rent/utility bills to support the home-office allocation.
- Decide whether to use the actual home-office method (Form 8829) or the simplified method ($5/sq ft). Based on the numbers you gave, the actual method (rent allocation ≈ $5,084) looks better than simplified ($775).
- If you expect to owe more tax (self-employment tax + income tax not covered by withholding), consider estimated tax payments in future years or increasing W-2 withholding.

A couple of important caveats
- Student loan interest deduction is limited by income (MAGI phaseouts) — if your AGI/MAGI is above the phaseout range you may only get a partial or no deduction. If you want, give me any retirement contributions, spousal income, or other adjustments and I can estimate MAGI and whether you phase out.
- I’m providing general information. For definitive filing, complex situations (business losses, domicile/state issues, multiple deductions), or to maximize tax strategies, consult a CPA or tax preparer.

If you’d like, I can:
- Recompute exact numbers if you confirm whether the $890 is a business expense and state the exact student-loan interest paid in 2024.
- Draft an email to a tax preparer with the info and attachments (I can prepare it if you want).
- Walk through filling the forms step-by-step.

