Quick summary (bottom-line)
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 married filing jointly unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local tax, medical expenses, etc.). The $2,000 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize.
- Your web‑design income ($8,900) is self‑employment income and is reported on Schedule C; you’ll owe self‑employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE) and you can deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040.
- Home‑office: As a self‑employed person you can take either the simplified method ($5/sq ft, up to 300 sq ft) or the regular method (your share of actual home expenses using percentage of home devoted to the office). With the numbers you gave, the regular method (allocating rent) will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest: only interest you paid in tax year 2024 can be deducted (up to $2,500 and subject to AGI limits). Interest paid in prior years (2016–2019) cannot be deducted on your 2024 return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension), so your $890 of unreimbursed W‑2 expenses is generally not deductible unless they relate to your self‑employment work and can be claimed on Schedule C.
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, and possibly Form 8829 (if using the regular home‑office calculation), and other forms listed below.

Details and calculations

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: report on Form 1040 (attach W‑2).
- Web‑design $8,900: report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Report gross receipts even if no 1099(s) were issued. On Schedule C you can claim ordinary and necessary business expenses (internet, supplies, software, home office, etc.). These reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Interest from Discover Bank $890: report as interest income on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if total interest/dividends > $1,500).
- TD Ameritrade $195: determine whether this is interest, dividends, or capital gain. Your 1099 from TD Ameritrade will tell you (1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, and/or 1099‑B). Report accordingly.

2) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). SE tax is computed on Schedule SE (roughly 15.3% of net earnings, after the 0.9235 adjustment).
- You may deduct one‑half of SE tax on Form 1040 as an “above‑the‑line” adjustment.
- Example (approximate) using only the numbers you gave and assuming no other business expenses:
  - If you use the regular home‑office allocation (see next section) the Schedule C net will be smaller (which lowers both income tax and SE tax).
  - If net self‑employment income is modest, SE tax will be modest. (I can calculate exact SE tax if you want once we settle on the home‑office method and any other business expenses.)

3) Home‑office deduction (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; total home 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
- Business use percentage = 155 / 750 = 0.206666… ≈ 20.67%.
- Regular (actual-expense) method: you take 20.67% of qualifying home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). Based on rent alone:
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
  - Rent portion allocable to office ≈ $24,600 × 20.67% ≈ $5,082.
  - If you have utility bills or internet that you want to apportion, those add to the deduction.
  - To claim this you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach to Schedule C.
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775. No Form 8829 needed; you report the amount on Schedule C.
- With the rent numbers you gave, the regular method will almost certainly give a larger deduction (~$5,082 vs $775) unless you have other constraints.
- Note: The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit. If you have a net loss from Schedule C due to a large home‑office deduction, special rules apply (and passive loss/at‑risk rules). Usually with $8,900 gross, a $5k deduction still leaves positive net self‑employment income, but if other expenses apply we should check.

4) Student loan interest ($4,400, Navient, 2016–2019)
- Only student loan interest paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible on your 2024 return, up to $2,500 and subject to income phaseouts for married filing jointly. Interest you paid in earlier years is not deductible now.
- If you did pay student loan interest in 2024, you should receive Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid in 2024. That is the amount used for the deduction. The deduction is taken as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (not an itemized deduction).
- If you truly paid $0 of student loan interest in 2024, you cannot claim the $4,400 now.

5) Charitable contribution $2,000 (Doctors Without Borders)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt/acknowledgement.
- If you take the standard deduction (which for most MFJ filers will be higher than $2,000), you will not separately deduct that $2,000.
- If you have other itemizable deductions (state tax, real estate tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses >7.5% AGI) that, when combined with $2,000, exceed the standard deduction, then itemizing would make sense.

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to W‑2 employment, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- If they are business expenses for your web‑design self‑employment (e.g., software, supplies, business travel), they should be reported on Schedule C instead.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- You should compare total itemized deductions (Schedule A) to the standard deduction for 2024 married filing jointly. You provided only $2,000 in charitable donations and no mortgage interest or state/local taxes numbers; so based on what you stated, it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger.
