Summary (short)
- Income: report W‑2 wages, interest/dividends/1099 amounts, and self‑employment income on Schedule C.
- Home office: if you qualify, you can deduct a portion of rent/utilities for the office. Regular method (Form 8829) will likely give a bigger deduction than the simplified method, but the home‑office deduction can’t create or increase a net business loss (excess carries forward).
- Student loan interest: deductible up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income only for interest you actually paid in the tax year and subject to income phaseouts — older years’ interest is not deductible now.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 jobs) are generally NOT deductible for tax years after 2017 (suspended through 2025). If they were business expenses for your web design, they belong on Schedule C.
- Charitable gift of $2,000 is deductible only if you itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.
- Likely you will take the standard deduction for MFJ (almost always larger unless you have significant mortgage interest, property tax, or big SALT up to the $10,000 limit).

Treatment of each item (details)

1) Income
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($82,350): report on Form 1040 (from your W‑2).
- Self‑employment (web design) $8,900: report gross receipts on Schedule C. Subtract ordinary and necessary business expenses (including your share of home expenses for the home office) to get net profit/loss.
- Interest from Discover $890: report as taxable interest on Form 1040 (if > $1,500 you’d also use Schedule B, but you’re below that threshold).
- $195 from TD Ameritrade: identify whether that is interest, dividends, or capital gain distributions (check your 1099s); report accordingly (1099‑DIV/1099‑B etc).

2) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; home is 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
- Qualification: must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc). For a self‑employed web designer this normally qualifies.
- Methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × square feet (max 300 sq ft). For 155 sq ft → $775 deduction. No Form 8829 required.
  - Regular: prorate actual home expenses by office % (155/750 = 20.67%). Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 → home office portion of rent ≈ $24,600 × 20.67% ≈ $5,083. You can also include your prorated share of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), and other allowable expenses. Use Form 8829 to calculate and report.
- Limitation: the home office deduction claimed under the regular method cannot exceed the gross income from the business (before the home‑office deduction); any disallowed amount carries forward to the next year.
- Recommendation: regular method will likely produce a much larger deduction here, but check whether it would cause a net loss for the business. If your business net before home‑office expenses is small, you may be limited; if you prefer simplicity, use the simplified method.

3) Student loan interest ($4,400; Navient, 2016–2019)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year (2024) can be deducted on the 2024 return (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts).
- If your $4,400 refers to past years’ interest paid 2016–2019, that is not deductible on your 2024 return. If you did pay $X in 2024 you should have Form 1098‑E from Navient showing the amount for 2024.
- Student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) — it reduces AGI (you do not have to itemize).
- There is an income phaseout for this deduction for married filing jointly — if your MAGI is above the phaseout threshold you may not be eligible or partially eligible.

4) Charitable donation $2,000
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep your receipt (you said you have it).
- If you take the standard deduction, you do not deduct this on the federal return.

5) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If they are employee business expenses (unreimbursed for a W‑2 job): generally not deductible under current law (suspended through 2025) for most taxpayers.
- If they are ordinary and necessary expenses for your web design business, report them on Schedule C as business expenses.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- For 2024 married filing jointly the standard deduction is approximately $29,200 (confirm with current IRS table when you file). Given the items you listed, the only clear itemized amount is $2,000 charitable; you didn’t list mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state taxes that would get you above the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: most likely you will take the standard deduction, unless you have substantial additional deductible items (large property tax or mortgage interest, or SALT taxes up to $10,000 and other deductible medical/property items) that push your itemized total above the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — for things like student loan interest if applicable
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your web design income and related expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — for Social Security/Medicare on net self‑employment earnings
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method to compute home office deduction
  - Form 4562 only if you claim depreciation (for owned homes). For renters usually not needed.
  - Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable gifts, SALT, mortgage interest)
  - Schedule B — only if you have more than $1,500 of interest/dividends (you do not in the amounts you gave)
  - Form 8995 / 8995‑A — if you claim the Qualified Business Income deduction (QBI) for self‑employment income (you may be eligible for up to 20% of QBI depending on taxable income and rules)
- State:
  - Illinois individual return (IL‑1040). Illinois has its own rules; state tax is generally deductible up to the federal SALT limit if you itemize (but again, you’re likely taking the federal standard deduction).

Quick illustrative calculation (rough — depends on clarifications)
Assumptions:
- Home office regular method used, and unreimbursed $890 are business expenses.
- Student loan interest not deductible (not paid in 2024).
