Below I’ll walk through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether you should itemize vs take the standard deduction, which forms you’ll likely need for federal (and a note about Illinois), plus a few follow-up questions so I can refine numbers.

Quick summary / recommendation
- Likely take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200) — your itemizable amounts as listed (charity $2,000 plus anything else you haven’t mentioned) are almost certainly less than that.
- Report the $8,900 web-design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for that work, deduct them on Schedule C. If those $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return (suspended by current law).
- For the home office, the regular method (use Form 8829) will almost certainly give you a bigger deduction than the simplified method because your rent is high relative to your office square footage. The home office deduction is taken on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in 2024 can be deducted on your 2024 return, and the federal limit is generally $2,500 (subject to income phase-outs). Prior-year interest (2016–2019) is not deductible on the 2024 return.
- Keep all receipts and your Forms 1099 / 1098 documents (1099‑INT from banks, 1099‑NEC if any clients issued them, 1098‑E for student loan interest if applicable).

Detailed steps, calculations, and forms

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 — reported on Form 1040 (attach W‑2 info).
- Self‑employment/web design: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). Any business expenses (supplies, software, the $890 if they are business expenses, etc.) reduce Schedule C net profit.
- Interest: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085 — report on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INTs). Schedule B is only required if total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other special situations, so you likely won’t need Schedule B.

2) Home office treatment (you gave: 155 sq ft exclusive; total home 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
- Office percentage = 155 / 750 = 0.206666... = 20.6667% (exact fraction 31/150).
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Regular-method home office share of rent = $24,600 × (155/750) = $5,084.
- Simplified method = $5 × 155 sq ft = $775.
- Recommendation: Use the regular method and file Form 8829 with Schedule C (because $5,084 > $775). The regular method lets you prorate rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. against the business.
- Note the home-office deduction cannot exceed the business’s net profit from that business in the year. If the home-office share plus other Schedule C expenses would create a net loss, excess home-office deduction becomes a suspended business loss to carry forward (check with a preparer if that happens).

Example (illustrative with assumptions)
- Assume $890 are business expenses (eligible on Schedule C):
  - Gross self-employment: $8,900
  - Less business expenses: $890
  - Less home office (regular): $5,084
  - Schedule C net profit = $8,900 − $890 − $5,084 = $2,926
- Self‑employment tax (approx): Net earnings × 0.9235 × 15.3% → roughly $414 (use Schedule SE to compute exactly).
- Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI) — approx $207 in this illustrative example.
- Student loan interest deduction (if paid in 2024 and not phased out) — up to $2,500 (above‑the‑line). Verify with Form 1098‑E from Navient for 2024 payments.
- Your total AGI will be W‑2 + net Schedule C + interest, less above‑the‑line adjustments (half SE tax, student loan interest if allowed).

3) Student loan interest ($4,400 you mentioned)
- Only student‑loan interest paid during the 2024 calendar year is deductible on your 2024 return. The maximum deduction is generally $2,500 (subject to income phaseout). If the $4,400 represents payments made in earlier years (2016–2019), those are not deductible in 2024.
- You’ll need Form 1098‑E from Navient for 2024 interest paid to claim any deduction.

4) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Charitable gifts to qualified organizations are deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). With only $2,000 in charity and no other major itemized amounts provided, the standard deduction ($29,200 for MFJ in 2024) will likely be larger — so taking the standard deduction is probably better.
- Keep the receipt for Doctors Without Borders for your records. If you do itemize, you’ll include this on Schedule A.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- Clarify: if these are business expenses for your web‑design clients, deduct on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee business expenses connected to a W‑2 job, those are not deductible on the federal return under current law (2018–2025), except in limited categories.

6) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments like student loan interest or other adjustments).
- Schedule C — profit/loss from your web design business.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if using the regular method). If you choose the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829; you just report the simplified home office deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 1099‑INT(s) — report bank & broker interest (Discover, TD Ameritrade).
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if you paid interest in 2024).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (seems unlikely given the numbers you provided).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 199A deduction (you may be able to take up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C subject to rules and income limits — with small net profit this may be small but you may still check). Your tax preparer or software will check eligibility.

7) Illinois state (IL)
- Illinois uses Form IL‑1040. Illinois starts with federal AGI with limited additions/subtractions. Charitable deductions and most federal adjustments don’t change much of the Illinois calculation, but check IL instructions. Illinois has a flat income tax rate (check current rate) and personal exemptions. If you want, I can give a short IL checklist once federal AGI is finalized.

8) Recordkeeping & other items to collect
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑K (if any) or records of client payments for the $8,900 (even if no 1099 issued).
- 1099‑INT(s) from Discover and TD Ameritrade.
- 1098‑E for student loan interest (for 2024 payments only).
- Receipts for the $2,000 charitable gift.
- Documentation backing the home-office calculation (square footage, lease agreement showing rent, utility bills).
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses (the $890 if business-related).
- If you paid estimated taxes during the year, keep those records.

9) Estimated tax / potential tax due
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and possibly income tax beyond what is withheld on your W‑2. If you expect similar self‑employment income in following years, consider making quarterly estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.

10) Next steps I recommend
- Confirm whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are for the web design business or for your W‑2 job.
- Confirm whether any of the $4,400 student loan interest was paid in 2024 (and if you received a 2024 Form 1098‑E).
- Tell me if you have other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, etc.).
- If you want, I can run the numbers more precisely with your confirmation (compute Schedule C net profit, SE tax, AGI, taxable income using the regular home office method vs simplified method, and show whether itemizing beats the standard deduction).

Important legal note
- I’m providing general information to help you prepare. This is not a substitute for personalized advice from a tax professional or CPA who can review your full situation and current 2024 tax law details (including phaseouts and Illinois rules). If you want, I can prepare a detailed calculation draft you can bring to a tax preparer.

