- Half of your self-employment tax: deductible as an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1 (calculated on Schedule SE).
- Charitable donation: $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders is deductible if you itemize (and you have the receipt). You cannot deduct it if you take the standard deduction.
- Student-loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible (up to $2,500 maximum, subject to income phaseouts and other rules). If the $4,400 was not paid in 2024 (but instead paid in earlier years), you cannot deduct it now. If it was paid in 2024, you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 (phaseouts apply).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally NOT deductible for most employees for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). If those $890 are employee expenses (i.e., not self-employment), they’re not deductible on your federal return now.

3) Home-office treatment (yours: 155 sq ft exclusive use; home = 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
You qualify for a home-office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business (or used for clients, storage, etc., under the rules). Because you have self-employment income, the home-office deduction applies to your Schedule C business.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft: 155 × $5 = $775 deduction.
- Regular (actual-expense) method: allocate actual home expenses by business-use percentage.
  - Business-use percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%.
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
  - Rent portion allocable to the office = 24,600 × 20.67% ≈ $5,086.
  - You can also allocate a share of utilities and renters insurance, etc., which would increase the deduction.
  - If you use the regular method you generally must file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute and report the deduction (the result is reported on Schedule C). The simplified method does not use Form 8829.

Which is better here?
- With your numbers the regular method (≈ $5,086 just from rent allocation) is substantially larger than the simplified $775, so the regular method will almost certainly yield a larger deduction. That said, if you don’t have good records of utilities or other allocable expenses you can still include the rent portion shown above.
- Important: the home-office deduction is limited to net profit from the business. If your business has little or no net profit after expenses, some home-office amounts may be limited and could be carried forward (Form 8829 rules apply).

4) Itemize vs Standard Deduction
- The home-office deduction is a business deduction (Schedule C) and reduces your self-employment income / AGI; it is not part of itemized deductions on Schedule A.
- Itemized deductions (Schedule A) include charitable gifts, state/local taxes (SALT, limited to $10,000), mortgage interest, medical (above AGI threshold), etc.
- From the items you listed, your only obvious itemized deduction is the $2,000 charitable gift (unless you have other state taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t list). That alone is very likely far less than the standard deduction for married filing jointly.
- Conclusion: With the information given, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on Schedule A (i.e., not itemize). The home-office and other business deductions still reduce taxable income via Schedule C and above-the-line adjustments regardless.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main tax return.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for reporting interest, dividends, and some adjustments.
- Schedule C — to report the $8,900 web-design income and your business expenses (including a line for business-use-of-home).
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method to calculate home-office expenses (recommended here because it’s larger). Results from Form 8829 transfer to Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax on your net Schedule C profit.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not for your situation).
- Schedule B — if you have more than $1,500 in interest and ordinary dividends or certain foreign accounts (probably not needed for the amounts you listed, but check total interest/dividends).
- Form 8949 and Schedule D — if you sold securities during the year and have capital gains/losses (TD Ameritrade will issue 1099-B if there were sales).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) from your Schedule C net income; you may be eligible, and the form depends on income level/complexity.
- State return: Illinois Form IL-1040 for your state income tax filing.

6) Other items to note / potential planning
- Self-employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax on your net Schedule C income (computed on Schedule SE); you may also get an above-the-line deduction for half of that SE tax.
- QBI (20% deduction): small business owners often qualify for the 20% pass-through deduction on qualified business income; it’s computed from your net Schedule C income and may reduce taxable income. It has rules and income thresholds — worth checking when preparing the return.
- 1099s: even if clients did not issue you 1099-NEC because amounts were under $600, you still must report the income. Gather any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, 1099-INT (Discover), and brokerage 1099s from TD Ameritrade.
- Keep receipts: for the $2,000 charitable donation, your bank/charity receipt is good substantiation. Keep rent records, utility bills, and records of the exclusive office area to substantiate the home-office claim.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self-employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated payments next year if you expect similar self-employment income.

7) Example quick calculation (illustrative)
- Home-office allocation (regular method): ≈ $5,086 (rent portion).
- Net self-employment income approx = $8,900 − $5,086 = $3,814 (before any other business expenses).
- Self-employment tax on that net income would be relatively small; half of that SE tax is an adjustment to income.
- Even with the home-office deduction applied, your itemizable amounts (charity $2,000, plus any SALT up to your actual amounts) are likely less than the standard deduction for married filing jointly, so standard deduction is probably better.

8) Recommended next steps
