Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk through this and tell you what’s (likely) deductible, whether itemizing makes sense, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll need. Before I finalize numbers, two quick clarifying questions:

1. The $4,400 in student-loan interest — was that interest you actually paid in 2024 (the tax year you’re filing)? The student-loan interest deduction only applies to interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for.  
2. The $890 in “unreimbursed expenses” — were those business expenses you paid as a self-employed web designer (receipts, supplies, software, etc.), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers under current law.)

If you confirm those, I’ll use the correct treatment. Below I explain the rules, show the math with assumptions, and list the forms you’ll need.

Summary of how items are treated
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital $82,350): Report on Form 1040 from your W-2. No special deduction here.
- Self-employment income (web design $8,900): Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including the business portion of your rent for a home office and any business supplies). Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 and is subject to income tax. You also pay self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self-employment earnings, reported on Schedule SE.
- Interest income (Discover $890, TD Ameritrade $195): Report as interest on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099-INT/1099-B). Taxable unless investment-specific rules apply.
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; rent $2,050/month for 750 sq ft): If you are self-employed and use the space exclusively and regularly for business, you can take a home-office deduction. Two methods:
  - Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by the business-use percentage (155 / 750 = 20.667%). With rent $2,050/month (annual rent = $24,600), the business share of rent = $24,600 × 0.206667 ≈ $5,086. You would also allocate a business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute and report this, and transfer the result to Schedule C.
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775. Simpler to claim but usually smaller than the regular-method deduction here. Given your rent, the regular method looks much more favorable.
- Student-loan interest ($4,400, Navient 2016–2019): Only interest actually paid in the tax year you’re filing can be deducted, and the maximum annual adjustment (above-the-line) is $2,500 (phase-outs apply at higher incomes). Also you need documentation (Form 1098-E from the servicer) showing interest paid in the tax year. If these interest payments were only for prior years and were not paid in 2024, they are not deductible on the 2024 return.
- Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): Charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize. There is no general above-the-line charitable deduction for 2024 (the temporary special provisions from some prior years aren’t in effect now). Keep the receipt and bank/CC record.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890): If these are self-employed business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job, they are not deductible for most taxpayers under current law (TCJA suspension).

Rough math (with assumptions)
Assumptions used in this example:
- The $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the web-design work.
- The $4,400 student-loan interest is NOT interest paid in 2024 (see note above). I’ll show the alternate scenario below if it was paid in 2024 and eligible.

Step A — compute net self-employment profit (Schedule C)
- Gross self-employment receipts: $8,900
- Less business share of rent (regular home-office allocation): ≈ $5,086
- Less other business expenses (assumed the $890): $890
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $8,900 − $5,086 − $890 = $2,924

Step B — total income before adjustments
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Net self-employment income: $2,924
- Interest income: $890
- TD Ameritrade income: $195
Total income = 82,350 + 2,924 + 890 + 195 = $86,359

Step C — adjustments to income
- Self-employment tax: SE tax is computed on net self-employment earnings (net profit × 0.9235). Roughly:
  - Net earnings subject to SE ≈ 2,924 × 0.9235 ≈ $2,701
  - SE tax ≈ $2,701 × 15.3% ≈ $413
  - Deductible half of SE tax (above-the-line) ≈ $206.50 (reported on Schedule 1, reduces AGI)
- Student-loan interest: if you did pay qualifying student-loan interest in 2024, you may deduct up to $2,500 (subject to AGI phase-out). If you did not pay interest in 2024, no deduction.

So with only the half-SE deduction:
- AGI ≈ $86,359 − $206.50 = $86,152.50

If you also are eligible to deduct student-loan interest in 2024 (up to $2,500), AGI would be about $86,152.50 − $2,500 = $83,652.50.

Itemize vs. Standard
- For 2024 (married filing jointly), the standard deduction is substantially larger than a typical single charitable gift of $2,000. (For planning purposes, the 2024 standard deduction for MFJ is roughly $29,200 — verify the exact IRS figure when you file.)
- Your itemizable items that you’ve told me:
  - Charitable contributions: $2,000
  - State and local taxes (SALT): you didn’t give state income tax or property tax amounts; SALT is capped at $10,000 for MFJ.
  - Mortgage interest/property tax: not provided.
Given only $2,000 of charitable donations and no other major itemized expenses reported, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. Even with student-loan interest (which is an adjustment to income, not an itemized deduction), the standard deduction will likely exceed the total of your itemizable amounts.

Home-office recommendation
- Use the regular method (Form 8829) if you can document rent and utilities and you want the larger deduction — the regular method gives ≈ $5,086 of business rent deduction in the numbers above. The simplified method would only give $775 (155 × $5). The regular method also lets you allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., so it is likely better in your case.
- Keep documentation: floor plan/sq ft measurement, lease and rent receipts, utility bills, and contemporaneous records showing exclusive, regular business use.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student-loan interest and the deductible half of SE tax flows here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self-employment gross receipts and business expenses, including the home-office deduction amount derived from Form 8829 or the simplified method
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method to compute the home-office deduction
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute/ report SE tax and the deductible portion
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (based on total itemizable amounts). Otherwise you won’t file Schedule A.
- Attach copies or statements (keep with your records): W-2 from Riverside Hospital, 1099-INT from Discover, 1099s from TD Ameritrade, any 1098-E for student loan interest (if applicable), and Form 1099-NEC/1099-MISC if you received one for web-design income.
State (Illinois)
- IL-1040: Illinois uses your federal adjusted gross income as the starting point for the state return. You will file IL-1040 and follow Illinois instructions for any state-specific deductions or credits. (If you live in Chicago, there are no separate city income taxes that change filing—Chicago uses state return. But check local requirements for any business licenses or local taxes if you’re self-employed.)

Other practical items and recommendations
- Documentation: keep receipts for the home-office calculation, charitable receipts, business expenses, and any 1098-E showing student-loan interest paid in the tax year.
- Quarterly estimated taxes: with self-employed income, you may owe estimated quarterly payments for income tax + SE tax. If your self-employment income will continue, plan to make quarterly estimated taxes to avoid penalties.
- If your $4,400 of student interest is actually interest paid in the current tax year and you have a 1098-E, you can deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income phase-out). Tell me whether that interest was paid in 2024 and I’ll re-run the numbers.
- If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are employee expenses (not self-employed), they are not deductible in most cases. If they are business expenses for the web-design work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.

Would you like me to:
- Recalculate AGI and taxable income using the student-loan interest as a 2024 deduction (assuming it was paid in 2024)?  
- Prepare a checklist of the documents you should gather to file (W-2, 1099s, receipts, 1098-E, etc.)?  
