I can help — here’s a clear, practical rundown of what you can (likely) deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, what IRS forms you’ll use, and the follow-up questions I need to give exact numbers. I’m not a CPA — if you want definitive tax filing or to optimize complex items (QBI, phaseouts), consider a tax professional.

Important clarifying questions (please answer these so I can refine calculations)
- Was the $4,400 of student loan interest paid during tax year 2024 (i.e., actually paid in 2024), or is that interest accrued over 2016–2019? (Only interest paid in the tax year may be deductible.)
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web‑design clients (self‑employment expenses)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job?
- Do you have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, property tax paid, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, casualty losses)?
- Did you receive any 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC for the $8,900 web‑design income, or was all paid without 1099s?

What you can (likely) deduct and how to treat each item

1) W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Report on Form 1040 (W‑2 box with federal income tax withheld, etc.).

2) Self‑employment (web design) income: $8,900
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., software, hosting, supplies).
- If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, include them on Schedule C. If they’re employee unreimbursed expenses, they are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) unless they meet special categories.

3) Self‑employment tax
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax. Calculate SE tax on Schedule SE (15.3% on net earnings — actually applied to ~92.35% of net earnings).
- You may deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (so it reduces your AGI).

4) Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive use; total home 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/month
- This qualifies only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your trade or business and you’re self‑employed (Schedule C). W‑2 employees generally cannot claim home office deductions.
- Business-use percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.67% (approx).
- Two methods:
  a) Regular method (you allocate actual home expenses): Rent annual = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Office portion = 24,600 × 20.667% ≈ $5,085. You can also allocate portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase the deduction (you’ll need records).
  b) Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775.
- Use Form 8829 to figure the deduction with the regular method (filed with Schedule C). If you use the regular method, total home office deduction cannot exceed business income (i.e., it’s limited by your net profit from that business).
- The regular method here looks substantially larger than the simplified method, but it’s limited by net profit.

5) Interest & dividends: $890 (Discover interest) + $195 (TD Ameritrade)
- Taxable interest/dividends. Report on Form 1040, and Schedule B only if total taxable interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (your total here is $1,085, so Schedule B not required).

6) Student loan interest: $4,400 (Navient; years 2016–2019 — clarify)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (Form 1040 Schedule 1) up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts and loan qualification rules).
- You should receive Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer showing interest paid in the year. If the $4,400 wasn’t paid in 2024, you cannot deduct it for 2024.
- The deduction phases out at higher MAGI levels for married filing jointly — it may be reduced or eliminated based on your AGI.

7) Charity: $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders (you have a receipt)
- Charitable cash contributions are deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Keep receipt.
- For 2024/2025, there were special above-the-line caps for cash gifts in some years, but generally cash gifts are deductible up to limits based on AGI (usually very high thresholds).
- If you claim the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct these charitable gifts (unless there are special provisions that apply).

8) Unreimbursed expenses: $890
- If these are personal employee unreimbursed expenses (from a W‑2 job), they are not deductible in 2018–2025 (suspended).
- If they are business expenses for your web design work, include on Schedule C.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly is almost always larger than $2,000 in charitable gifts alone. (If you don’t have other large itemizable amounts—mortgage interest, significant state/local taxes up to $10,000 cap, big medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI—then the standard deduction will usually be larger.)
- Based on the items you gave (charity $2,000 only), you will very likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantial mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state taxes that make Schedule A exceed the standard deduction.
- If you want, tell me how much you paid in mortgage interest and property taxes and state income tax in 2024, and I’ll compare a quick itemized total to the standard deduction.

Quick example (illustrative) showing home office effect and SE tax
- Business gross: $8,900
- Business expenses (example): $890 (if business-related)
- Home office (regular method): ≈ $5,085 (155/750 × annual rent $24,600)
- Net profit before SE tax = 8,900 − 890 − 5,085 = $2,925
- Net earnings subject to SE tax ≈ 2,925 × 0.9235 = $2,703
- SE tax ≈ 2,703 × 15.3% ≈ $414 (Schedule SE). Half of this (~$207) is deductible on Schedule 1 (above the line).
- Home office deduction lowers your Schedule C net income; that reduces income tax and SE tax. Note: home office deduction cannot create a net loss beyond other business income limits (losses can offset other income in many cases, but there are rules).
- The regular method home office deduction in this example (≈$5,085) is far larger than the simplified method ($775).

Other items you may be able to use
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: as a self‑employed person you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income (Form 8995 or 8995‑A) — thresholds and limitations apply and depend on taxable income and the type of business. Web design usually qualifies but phaseouts can apply.
- Retirement contributions (SEP‑IRA, Solo 401(k), or traditional IRA) can reduce taxable income and are worth considering if you want to lower your tax bill.

Forms you will likely need for 2024 filing (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income — e.g., student loan interest deduction, half SE tax).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web‑design income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for home office deduction; NOT required if you use the simplified method.
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — you’ll need the issuer’s report; use it to compute the student loan interest deduction.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received any for client payments) and 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV for interest/dividends.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if applicable.
- Illinois state return: IL‑1040 (for state income tax).

Records to gather (important)
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- Any 1099‑NEC or records for the $8,900 web‑design income.
- 1099‑INT (Discover), 1099‑DIV (TD Ameritrade).
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills for the home (for home office allocation), and detailed records of home office square footage and exclusive use.
- Receipts for the $890 in expenses (if business-related).
- Receipt for charitable gift $2,000.
- 1098‑E or loan statements showing interest paid in 2024 (Navient).
- Records of state/local taxes paid, property tax statements, mortgage interest (Form 1098) if any.

Next steps I can take for you
- If you answer the clarifying questions above, I can:
  - Estimate federal taxable income and approximate tax owed or refund (including SE tax).
  - Compare itemizing vs. standard deduction using your full list of itemizable items (mortgage interest, property taxes, state tax paid).
  - Prepare a checklist of exact forms and amounts for your filer or tax preparer.

If you want a quick estimate now, tell me:
- Was the $4,400 student loan interest paid in 2024 (or is that total interest from prior years)?
- Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses for your web design business?
- Any mortgage interest, property taxes, or state income tax paid in 2024 (amounts)?
- Any 1099s received for the web design work?

