1) What you can (likely) deduct
- W-2 wages from Riverside Hospital ($82,350): reported on Form 1040 (W-2 income).
- Web design income ($8,900, 6 clients): treated as self-employment income — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). All ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the web design work reduce this income.
  - Unreimbursed expenses ($890): if these are expenses you incurred for the web design business (software, hosting, supplies, supplies, client travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If instead they are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a job where you received a W-2), those are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under current law.
- Interest income: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085. Report on Form 1040 as interest income. (You only need Schedule B if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500.)
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; you rent 750 sq ft at $2,050/month):
  - Two methods: simplified or actual. Compare:
    - Simplified: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
    - Actual: proportionate share of actual home costs. Rent annual = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Office share = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.667%. Rent portion deductible ≈ $24,600 × 0.20667 ≈ $5,088 (plus a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have those costs). Because you rent and have no mortgage depreciation to worry about, actual method is likely to give you a larger deduction given the values you provided. The home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net business income.
  - To use the actual method with a Schedule C you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). For simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829 — you just claim the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest ($4,400 listed): the student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (on Schedule 1) with a maximum of $2,500 in the tax year and is subject to MAGI phaseouts. Important: the deductible amount is interest paid during the tax year on a qualified student loan. You cannot claim interest paid in prior years on a past-year return now; only the interest you actually paid in the tax year can be deducted (and the IRS/your loan servicer typically issues Form 1098-E when you paid $600+ in interest). Clarify below whether the $4,400 is interest paid in tax year 2024 or something else.
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible if you itemize (Schedule A) and you have proof/receipt (you do). If you take the standard deduction, this $2,000 will not give additional tax benefit (there is no universal above-the-line deduction for cash gifts for 2024).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: as noted, not deductible now unless they’re business expenses for a self-employed activity.

2) Itemize vs. standard deduction (recommendation)
- For 2024 married filing jointly, the standard deduction is likely to be substantially larger than the $2,000 charitable contribution alone (so with the items you listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction).
