Below is a clear summary of how each item is treated, followed by concrete next steps and the forms you’ll use.

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: report on Form 1040 (W‑2 wages).
- Web design income ($8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). This is self‑employment income and will also be subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Interest: $890 from Discover (reported on 1099‑INT) and $195 from TD Ameritrade (1099‑DIV or 1099‑INT depending on type) — report on Form 1040 (and  Schedule B if required).

2) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; home = 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/month)
You have a bona fide home office for a self‑employed person if it is used regularly and exclusively for the business. You can choose either:
- Simplified method: $5 per sq. ft. up to 300 sq. ft. → 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction (reported on Schedule C; no Form 8829).
- Actual method: prorate actual home expenses by the business percentage (155/750 = 20.6667%).
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion attributable to the office ≈ $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,086.
  - You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., by the same percentage and deduct the business portion on Schedule C. To use the actual method you will generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with your Schedule C.
Which is better here: the actual method almost certainly gives a larger deduction because 20.7% of your rent ($~5,086) >> simplified $775. So the actual method looks advantageous unless you prefer the simplicity of the $5/sqft method. Keep receipts and records for rent, utilities, etc.

3) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- You must pay self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). Rough example (assuming only the home office as a business expense):
  - Net business income ≈ $8,900 − $5,086 = $3,814.
  - Self‑employment tax is ~15.3% on 92.35% of net income: SE tax ≈ $539 (estimate).  
  - Half of the SE tax (~$269) is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- The business expenses (including the home‑office portion) reduce your Schedule C net and therefore reduce both income tax and SE tax.

4) Student‑loan interest ($4,400)
- Only interest paid in the tax year (e.g., 2024) can be deducted, up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). This deduction is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), not an itemized deduction.
- If the $4,400 was paid in prior years (2016–2019), it is not deductible on your 2024 return. If you did pay $4,400 in 2024, you can deduct up to $2,500 (or a reduced amount if your MAGI exceeds the phaseout thresholds). I’ll need to know whether the payments occurred in 2024.

5) Charitable deduction ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders)
- This is an itemized deduction on Schedule A (if you itemize). Doctors Without Borders is a qualified charity (keep the receipt).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct the $2,000 on Schedule A.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job: under current federal law (TCJA through at least 2025) miscellaneous employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible on Schedule A for most taxpayers.
- If these expenses are ordinary and necessary costs of your web‑design business, report them on Schedule C as business expenses (they reduce Schedule C net income and SE tax).

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- You should compare total itemizable deductions (Schedule A) to the standard deduction for married filing jointly. Itemizable items would include: charitable contributions ($2,000), mortgage interest (if any), medical expenses (only the portion exceeding 7.5% of AGI), and state & local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 cap. Unreimbursed employee expenses are generally not deductible.
- Given what you’ve listed so far (only $2,000 charitable and no mortgage/property taxes or large SALT or deductible medical expenses noted), it’s likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized deductions. In that case you would take the standard deduction and not file Schedule A.
- If you have mortgage interest, significant property taxes, or paid substantial state income tax, then itemizing could make sense. Tell me those amounts and I’ll run the comparison.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for up to a 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A) on qualified business income from your web‑design business, subject to limits based on taxable income and the nature of the business. With your income level, you probably qualify for at least some QBI deduction. This is computed on Form 8995 (or Form 8995‑A for more complex situations).

9) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments (student loan interest if eligible, half of self‑employment tax shown on Schedule SE in certain spots)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and business expenses (including home office)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — if required by amounts / multiple interest sources
- Keep (and attach if required) 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, W‑2, 1099‑NEC, etc., as appropriate.
- If you expect tax due during the year, use Form 1040‑ES for estimated tax payments.

10) Recordkeeping and documents to gather
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099‑INT (Discover) and 1099s from TD Ameritrade
- Records of web‑design income (invoices, 1099‑NEC, bank deposits)
- Receipts and calculations for home office (floor plan, square footage, rent, utilities, insurance, repairs)
- Receipts for the $2,000 charitable donation (written acknowledgement)
- Documentation of student loan interest paid in 2024 (Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer)
- Records supporting any other deductions or credits

11) Next steps / recommendations
