I can walk through how your 2024 federal tax return would generally be handled and what to watch for — but I’m not a CPA or tax attorney, so consider this guidance and consult a tax pro for filing-level accuracy, especially for state rules (Illinois) and any complex items.

Summary / headline recommendations
- Report all income: W-2 wages ($82,350), self-employment income (web design), and interest/dividend income from Discover and TD Ameritrade.
- File Schedule C (Form 1040) for the web‑design business and Schedule SE for self‑employment tax.
- For home office, you can choose simplified ($5/sq ft) or actual method (pro rata share of rent/utilities). Based on the numbers you gave, the actual method (about $5,086/year allocated from rent) will likely give a bigger deduction than the simplified ($775) — provided you have enough net profit from the business to absorb it.
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). If the $4,400 you cited is interest paid in 2016–2019, that does not create a 2024 deduction; if you actually paid student‑loan interest in 2024, we need that year’s amount.
- Charitable $2,000 is deductible only if you itemize. With only $2,000 of charitable contributions (and no other itemizable amounts listed), the standard deduction for married filing jointly will almost certainly be larger — so you will likely take the standard deduction.
- State: you will also file an Illinois return (IL‑1040). State rules differ — consider a tax preparer for state-specific items.

Details and calculations from your numbers
1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $82,350 — reported on Form 1040 (attach W-2).
- Self-employment (web design): $8,900 gross — report on Schedule C. Subtract allowable business expenses (supplies, software, your $890 if those are business expenses, etc.) to get net profit.
- Interest/dividends: $890 (Discover) and $195 (TD Ameritrade) — report as interest/dividend income on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV).
Total gross income before adjustments ≈ $92,335 (82,350 + 8,900 + 890 + 195).

2) Business expenses and home office
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890): If these are business expenses for your web‑design work (software, supplies, client expenses), report them on Schedule C and they reduce your net profit. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, those are not deductible under current federal law.
- Home office:
  - You said the office is 155 sq ft exclusively, home is 750 sq ft; business percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%.
  - Rent you pay: $2,050/month → $24,600/year. Allocated portion = 24,600 * 20.67% ≈ $5,086.
  - Two ways to claim:
    - Simplified: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (no Form 8829 required).
    - Actual method: use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to allocate rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, etc.; here just the rent allocation is ≈ $5,086 (plus your share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if applicable). The home‑office deduction is claimed on Schedule C as a business expense.
  - Important limits: actual home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business. If your Schedule C net profit (before the home office deduction) is low, the deductible amount may be limited and excess may carry forward in some cases (Form 8829 rules).
  - Because your gross self‑employment income is relatively small ($8,900) and if you have $890 in other business expenses, a $5,086 home office allocation would substantially reduce net profit (which is allowed, but you cannot create a business loss that’s disallowed as a hobby rule — generally business loss is allowed and reduces your overall taxable income, but watch for hobby/activity considerations if losses persist).
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe SE tax (~15.3% on net earnings) calculated on Schedule SE. Half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces taxable income).

3) Student loan interest ($4,400)
- Only interest paid in the calendar tax year (2024) is deductible on your 2024 return, up to $2,500 (above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts for married filing jointly.
- You mentioned $4,400 and years 2016–2019 — if that amount was paid in past years, it is not deductible in 2024. If you actually paid some amount in 2024, tell me the 2024 interest paid and I can estimate the deductible amount.
- Phaseouts: the deduction phases out at higher MAGI levels for MFJ. Based on your income (~$92k before adjustments), you will likely be under phaseout thresholds, but I’ll confirm if you provide exact AGI or further details.

4) Charitable donation ($2,000)
- Cash donation of $2,000 with receipt is deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. With only $2,000 of charitable giving (and no mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, or other itemized items provided), the standard deduction for married filing jointly will almost certainly exceed your itemized total — so you’d take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable expenses not listed.

5) Whether to itemize or take standard deduction
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly (2024) is typically much larger than $2,000. Given the items you listed (charity $2,000, no mortgage interest or large medical/state taxes mentioned), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- The main reason to itemize would be if you have significant other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, high state/local taxes up to SALT limits, big medical expenses above threshold, or larger charitable gifts).

6) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — used for adjustments like student loan interest and for reporting business income flow-through from Schedule C.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web‑design income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you don’t need Form 8829.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (sounds unlikely based on provided items).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) if you are eligible. With a small Schedule C net profit you may qualify; this form computes the QBI deduction.
- Keep and attach (not file) any 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV you receive (you report the amounts on 1040).
- Illinois: IL‑1040 (state return) and any Illinois schedules required. (State-specific deductions/exemptions vary.)

7) Practical next steps and questions for you
To be precise and run the numbers I need a few clarifications:
- Is the $4,400 student‑loan interest amount the total you paid in 2024, or is that an aggregate of amounts paid in 2016–2019? (Only 2024 interest payments matter for the 2024 return.)
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web design work, or are they unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job? (If the latter, they are not deductible on the federal return under current law.)
- Is the $2,050/month rent for the whole dwelling (i.e., you rent), or is that mortgage payment? (Either way, with the actual method you normally allocate rent or mortgage interest appropriately; but if mortgage interest applies you may have additional Schedule A itemizing considerations.)
- Do you have any other itemizable items not listed (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income taxes paid, large medical expenses)?
- Do you want me to estimate the taxable income and self-employment tax for 2024 using the numbers you gave and assuming the home office actual method vs simplified?

8) Other notes & reminders
- Keep receipts and records for charitable contributions, student loan interest (Form 1098‑E if you received one), business expenses, and home‑office calculations.
- If you expect to owe additional tax because of self‑employment tax or underwithholding, consider estimated tax payments or adjusting W‑2 withholding to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Illinois state rules: you must file IL‑1040; some items are treated differently at the state level. Consider a tax preparer familiar with Illinois law.

If you want, I can:
- Run an estimated worksheet comparing (a) standard deduction vs itemizing and (b) simplified vs actual home office method, using assumed values and your answers to the questions above.
- Draft an email to a CPA with the facts and ask for a formal review (I can create the draft for you to send).

