Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions where details matter. I’m not a CPA — for anything final or complex (amending prior years, large or unusual items, or minimizing audit risk) consider a tax professional or the IRS instructions.

Summary answer (short)
- Most likely you should take the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (standard deduction is larger than your likely itemized deductions given the data you provided).
- Your self‑employment work (web design) must be reported on Schedule C; you can deduct a home‑office share of your rent and other business expenses against that income. Using the actual‑expense method for your home office will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method, based on the numbers you gave.
- Student loan interest is deductible only for interest paid in the tax year (and max $2,500 per year with income phaseouts) — interest paid in 2016–2019 cannot be deducted on your 2024 return (unless you are amending those prior returns).
- Expect to file Form 1040 plus Schedule C (business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and possibly Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office method). If you itemize you’d use Schedule A — but likely you’ll not itemize.

Details

1) Income you must report
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $82,350 (reported on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment/web design: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). All income must be reported even if no 1099s were issued.
- Interest income: $890 (Discover) + $195 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,085 — report on Form 1040 interest line. (If interest total were > $1,500 you’d also file Schedule B, but at $1,085 Schedule B is not required.)

2) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% on net self‑employment earnings; half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).
- You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (software, supplies, advertising, business mileage if any, business portion of phone, etc.). Make sure the $890 of “unreimbursed expenses” is business expenses from your self‑employment. If those $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended).

3) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; home is 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
You have two methods:

a) Simplified method
- Deduction = $5.00 × square feet used for business (max 300 sq ft).
- For 155 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775 deduction on Schedule C.

b) Actual expense method (usually better when rent is significant)
- Compute business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.666...% (≈ 20.67%).
- Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). With rent only: annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600; business share = $24,600 × 20.666% ≈ $5,086.67.
- You may also include a business share of utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable expenses.
- If you use actual expenses, you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
Recommendation: Based on the rent figure you provided, the actual‑expense method will almost certainly produce a larger deduction (~$5,087 from rent alone) than the simplified method ($775). Use actual method if you have documentation for rent/utilities; use simplified only if you prefer minimal recordkeeping.

Note: The home office deduction requires the space be used regularly and exclusively for business (you said exclusive), and used as principal place of business or for meeting clients.

4) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders — deductible as an itemized deduction on Schedule A (cash charitable contributions documented by receipt). Not deductible on Schedule C (unless it’s a business expense, which charitable gifts are not).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this charitable gift on Schedule A.

5) Student loan interest ($4,400 / Navient, 2016–2019)
- Only student loan interest paid in the tax year (2024) may be deducted on Form 1040 (adjustment to income) up to $2,500 (and subject to MAGI phaseouts).
- Interest from prior years (2016–2019) cannot be claimed on your 2024 return. If you mean you paid $4,400 in interest during 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income limits). If those earlier years’ interest was not deducted at the time and you believe you have a claim, that would require amending those earlier returns — consult a tax pro.

Income limits: There is a phaseout for the student loan interest deduction based on modified adjusted gross income; for MFJ there is a phaseout range (check the exact 2024 thresholds or ask your preparer). If your MAGI is above the phaseout range you may not be able to take it.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly for 2024 is (inflation‑adjusted) substantially higher than prior years (about $29,200 for MFJ — confirm current IRS table or your preparer). With only a $2,000 charitable gift and no mortgage interest, large SALT (state+local tax) or big medical expenses shown, your itemized deductions likely won’t exceed the standard deduction.
- Therefore, you will most likely take the standard deduction for 2024 rather than itemizing.
- Note: the home office deduction is separate (it’s a business deduction on Schedule C) and does not affect your choice between standard/itemized deductions for personal deductions.

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- If your Schedule C net income qualifies, you may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A), calculated on Form 8995 or 8995‑A. With your combined W‑2 and business income, you’ll very likely be under the phaseout thresholds for more complicated limitations, so you may qualify for up to 20% of qualified business income — but QBI can be limited by W‑2 wages, qualified property rules, and taxable income. Because your business income is relatively small, the QBI deduction may be modest but is worth calculating.

8) Estimated taxes and withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and additional income tax. If your employer withholding doesn’t cover the tax due, you should make estimated quarterly payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties. If you expect a small tax due, you can adjust payroll withholding instead.

9) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $8,900 web‑design income and associated business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and half‑SE deduction.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using actual home‑office expense method. If using simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — to calculate the QBI deduction (199A), if applicable.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (likely not in your case).
- Schedule B — only if interest income > $1,500 or other conditions.
- Attach copies of W‑2(s), and include 1099‑INTs / 1099‑NEC if provided.
- Illinois state return: IL‑1040 (file IL return). State rules differ for some deductions — check Illinois instructions or your preparer.

10) Recordkeeping / items to gather
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099‑INTs from banks/brokers (Discover, TD Ameritrade).
- Records of the six clients’ payments (invoices, bank deposits). 1099‑NEC if issued by any clients.
- Receipts for the $890 business expenses (or documentation of what the unreimbursed expenses are).
- Home records: floor plan square footage, rent receipts/lease, utility bills — to support the home office percentage and actual expenses.
- Charitable receipt for $2,000.
- Student loan interest statements (Form 1098‑E) if any for 2024; documentation showing interest payments in earlier years if you plan to amend earlier returns.

11) Questions / items I need to confirm to be more precise
- Was the $4,400 of student loan interest paid in 2024 or during 2016–2019? (Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return.)
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web design clients? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from the W‑2 job? If they are business expenses, they go on Schedule C. If they are employee unreimbursed expenses for the W‑2 job, they’re not deductible in 2018–2025.
- Do you have any significant state income tax or property tax or mortgage interest? (Those could increase itemized deductions and change the take‑standard vs itemize decision.)
- Do you want me to run a quick illustrative tax calculation with the actual numbers to show taxable income and approximate tax owed under both home office methods (actual vs simplified)?

If you’d like, I can:
- Do a short, illustrative tax calculation showing (a) actual home‑office method vs simplified and (b) whether itemizing beats the standard deduction given any additional info you provide; or
- Draft an email summarizing these items to a CPA with the documents you have to ask for professional help; or
- Prepare a checklist of documents to send to a preparer.

