- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web‑design work (independent contractor) or unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are generally not deductible 2018–2025.)

If you confirm those two points I can refine numbers. Meanwhile, here’s guidance based on the information you gave.

1) Which items are deductible (and how)
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital ($82,350): included in gross income; withholding/credits apply normally.
- Self‑employment income (web design) $8,900: report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see next bullets).
- Interest income: $890 (Discover savings) and $195 (TD Ameritrade) are taxable interest/dividend income and go on Form 1040 (reported from 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV).
- Student loan interest ($4,400): Only interest paid in the tax year (and reported on Form 1098‑E) can be claimed. The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (up to $2,500 limit, subject to income phaseouts). If the $4,400 was paid in prior years (2016–2019) it cannot be deducted on your 2024 return.
- Charitable contribution $2,000: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep your receipt — for cash contributions you should have documentation.
- Unreimbursed expenses $890: If these are business expenses for your self‑employment work, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer, they are generally NOT deductible (suspended under TCJA through 2025), except in rare categories.
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive space; home = 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo): this can be deductible for your self‑employment business if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business. Two methods:
  - Actual method (Form 8829): business‑use percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion allocable to the home office ≈ $24,600 × 0.2067 ≈ $5,088. You can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc., by that percentage. The allocated home office expense is a Schedule C deduction but is limited to your net business income (excess is suspended/treated as carryforward).
  - Simplified method: $5 × square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft → $775 deduction (no Form 8829 required). This is reported on Schedule C as well.
  - Which to choose? The actual method here (~$5,088) is much larger than the simplified ($775). However the home office deduction cannot create or increase a business loss — it’s limited to net profit from that business. With the numbers you provided, if the home office deduction is taken as actual and you also deduct $890 of other business expenses, net profit = 8,900 − 5,088 − 890 = $2,922 (positive), so actual method is allowable and gives a larger deduction. If you use simplified: net profit = 8,900 − 775 − 890 = $7,235.
  - Note: Home office deductions reduce self‑employment (SE) tax and income tax because they reduce Schedule C net profit.

2) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) the standard deduction is substantially larger than common small itemized totals. Based on what you listed, the only itemized amount you have is the $2,000 charitable gift (and possibly state/local taxes or mortgage interest if you have them — you didn’t list those). Unless you have additional itemizable amounts (significant mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large medical expenses, or more charitable giving), your itemized total will be much less than the standard deduction.
- So in almost all cases with the data given, you should take the standard deduction. (If you do have mortgage interest, property tax, or other itemizable amounts you didn’t list, add them up and compare to the standard deduction for 2024.)

3) Self‑employment tax and related items
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- If you expect to owe significant tax beyond withholding, you may need to make estimated tax payments quarterly to avoid penalties.

4) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (Married Filing Jointly)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for some adjustments (e.g., student loan interest if eligible) and other income lines
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report web design income and business expenses (including simplified home office or net after Form 8829)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the actual home‑office method
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (e.g., to deduct the $2,000 charitable gift). Otherwise you won’t use it.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if clients issued them), W‑2 from Riverside, 1099‑INT (Discover), 1099‑DIV / 1099‑INT from TD Ameritrade or broker statements — include those as supporting documents.
