1) What you can deduct (based on usual IRS rules)
- Self-employment business expenses: If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your web design work (supplies, software, internet, client travel, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against the $8,900 business income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are generally NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) unless you have a very specific exception.
- Home office: Deductible only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business. You said it’s exclusive — that meets the basic test. Two options (details and comparison below).
- Interest and dividends: Taxable income on Form 1040. You’ll get 1099-INT and 1099-DIV; no Schedule B is needed unless interest + dividends > $1,500 or other Schedule B triggers.
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction (adjustment to income) is limited to $2,500 maximum in the tax year and is subject to MAGI phaseouts at higher incomes. Based on the wage+income amounts you provided, you’re likely under the phaseout and could take up to $2,500 — but you cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on this year’s return. (See clarifying question below.)
- Charitable contributions: Deductible only if you itemize (on Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, the $2,000 gift will NOT be deductible on your federal return.
- Self-employment tax: You’ll owe self-employment tax on net self-employment earnings; half of that SE tax is an above-the-line deduction (Schedule 1).

2) Home office — simplified vs. actual method (quick math)
- Office fraction = 155 / 750 = 0.2066667 ≈ 20.67%.
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.

Simplified method
- Rate: $5 per sq. ft up to 300 sq ft.
- Deduction = 155 × $5 = $775.

Actual (regular) method — using rent only (you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you pay them)
- Deduction = 20.6667% × $24,600 ≈ $5,078.

Effect on net self-employment income (approx.)
- Business income: $8,900
- Other business expenses you listed: $890 (I assume these are business expenses)
  - Using simplified: deductible expenses = $890 + $775 = $1,665 → net profit ≈ $7,235
  - Using actual: deductible expenses = $890 + $5,078 = $5,968 → net profit ≈ $2,932

Self-employment tax (approx = 15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit)
- Simplified: net*0.9235 ≈ $6,683 → SE tax ≈ $1,022; half SE tax ≈ $511 deduction
- Actual: net*0.9235 ≈ $2,707 → SE tax ≈ $414; half SE tax ≈ $207 deduction

Conclusion: With the numbers you gave, the actual method (allocating rent) gives a much larger home-office deduction and reduces both income tax and SE tax more than the simplified method. If you only provided rent as a home expense, actual method still looks clearly better here. If you own the home (depreciation rules) or have other shared household expenses, the actual method requires Form 8829 and a proper allocation; the simplified method is easier (no Form 8829) but gives a smaller deduction.

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- You reported $2,000 in charitable gifts. Based only on the items you listed, your likely itemized deductions are far less than the standard deduction for married filing jointly. So you will almost certainly be better taking the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state and local taxes up to the SALT cap, large medical expenses above the AGI threshold, significant other charitable gifts, etc.).
- Practical rule: If your total Schedule A items exceed the standard deduction amount for MFJ (standard deduction typically substantially larger than $2,000), itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.
- Based on the numbers you gave (no mortgage interest listed, only $2,000 charity), I expect the standard deduction will be the better choice.

4) Forms you will need / where these items are reported
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 to Form 1040 — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction up to $2,500; also the deductible half of SE tax flows here)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web design income ($8,900) and business expenses (the $890 and the home office deduction if using simplified or actual affects Schedule C)
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for home-office deduction
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of QBI) if eligible (with your income level you’ll likely file Form 8995)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim the $2,000 charitable gift)
- 1099-INT (Discover) and 1099-DIV (TD Ameritrade) information included on Form 1040; Schedule B only if total interest + dividends > $1,500 or other triggers
- Form 1098-E — lender statement for student loan interest (Navient) — use this to substantiate the student loan interest deduction if you claim it
- Keep any receipts/invoices for business expenses and the $2,000 charity receipt.

5) Other items to consider
- QBI deduction: As a self-employed person you may be eligible for up to 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income. With the small net business profit in the actual-method scenario, the QBI benefit will be modest but helpful. You’ll use Form 8995 (simple) unless you cross the threshold or have complicated items.
- Estimated taxes: If you expect similar self-employment income in future years, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties.
- State taxes: Illinois has its own rules. Most of the federal items above affect your Illinois return but check IL specifics (e.g., student loan interest is handled differently at state level).
- Documentation: Keep Form 1099s, Form 1098-E, receipts for business expenses, the charity receipt, and documentation for home office (a simple floorplan showing exclusive area and photos are wise).

6) Two clarifying questions
1. The student loan interest: you wrote “$4,400 student loan interest (Navient, 2016–2019)”. Do you mean (A) you paid $4,400 in student loan interest during tax year 2024, or (B) the total interest you paid in earlier years 2016–2019? The student loan interest deduction applies to interest paid in the tax year only, and the maximum deduction in a year is $2,500 (subject to income limits).
