I’m not a CPA, but here’s a clear, practical summary of how your 2024 return will generally work and what you should check/collect. If you want firm answers or to file, I recommend confirming with a tax preparer or CPA.

Key assumptions / ask first
- Is the $8,900 web-design income self‑employment income (you invoice clients / receive 1099‑NEC or you’re an independent contractor), or was it paid as wages to you as an employee? (This changes where expenses/home office are reported.)
- Is the $4,400 “student loan interest” amount interest you actually paid in tax year 2024, or is it total interest paid over 2016–2019? (Only interest paid in the tax year may be deductible; you should have a Form 1098‑E from the servicer for interest paid in the year.)
- Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or large state/local tax payments withheld/paid (SALT), or other deductible medical expenses? Those change the itemize vs standard decision.

What you can deduct (based on the facts you provided)
1. Wages and interest reporting
- W‑2 wages ($82,350) are ordinary wage income (Form 1040, from your W‑2).
- Interest from Discover savings ($890) and TD Ameritrade ($195) is taxable interest income and must be reported on Form 1040. (If combined interest > $1,500 you’d use Schedule B; your total is $1,085 so Schedule B is not required.)

2. Self‑employment / web design income (if self‑employed)
- Report the $8,900 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Business expenses (including the $890 unreimbursed expenses if they are business expenses) are deducted on Schedule C, reducing your net self‑employment income.
- Net Schedule C income is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE). You can also deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- If the web design activity qualifies, you may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 1040, via Form 8995/8995-A) depending on taxable income and type of income.

3. Home‑office (only if you are self‑employed)
- You may take a home‑office deduction only if the space (155 sq ft) is used exclusively and regularly for the business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft = $775 deduction. Simple to use and no Form 8829.
  - Actual (regular) method: allocate a business percentage of actual home expenses. Your business % = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%. With rent $2,050/mo = $24,600/year; business portion of rent alone ≈ $5,088. (You would also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) To use this you complete Form 8829 and attach it to Schedule C.
- If you are an employee (not self‑employed) and receive no reimbursement from your employer, the home‑office deduction is NOT allowed (employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible under current law for 2018–2025).

4. Student loan interest
- You can deduct student loan interest paid during the tax year (up to $2,500 per return, subject to income phaseouts) as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1. You need the Form 1098‑E from Navient showing interest paid in the tax year. If the $4,400 is interest paid in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 on your 2024 return (and phaseouts may reduce or eliminate the deduction depending on MAGI). If the $4,400 covers prior years, it is not deductible now.

5. Charitable contribution
- Your $2,000 cash donation to Doctors Without Borders is deductible if you itemize; you have the receipt, which is required for contributions of $250+.
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct that charitable contribution on Schedule A.

6. Unreimbursed expenses
- If they’re business expenses tied to the web‑design work (self‑employment), deduct them on Schedule C.
- If they’re unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 job), they are not deductible under current law (through 2025).

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is approximately $29,200. (Confirm exact IRS figure when preparing your return.)
- Your known potential itemized amounts from the data you gave: charitable $2,000 (plus any state/local taxes/property taxes and mortgage interest if applicable). Absent substantial mortgage interest or property/SALT taxes, it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total.
- Quick rule: If your total itemizable deductions (Schedule A) exceed the standard deduction, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction. Based on the numbers you provided, standard deduction will likely be better unless you have sizable mortgage interest, property taxes, or state/local taxes near the SALT cap ($10,000).

Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (to report adjustments to income such as student loan interest and the deductible half of SE tax).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — if web design is self‑employment.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — if self‑employed.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for home office; not needed for the simplified method.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (for charitable contributions, state/local taxes, mortgage interest, medical above threshold, etc.).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends > $1,500 (not required given your stated interest).
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — used to support student loan interest deduction (Navient should provide this for interest paid in the tax year).
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC if you received any for contract income — keep them for Schedule C.
- 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / brokerage statements (TD Ameritrade) for interest or dividends.

Other practical notes
- Keep receipts and documentation: W‑2, 1099s, 1098‑E, charity receipt, rent records and utility bills for home office allocation, invoices/receipts for business expenses, and any records of state tax/property tax payments.
- If self‑employed, be aware of estimated tax payments for 2024 if you expect a tax liability not covered by withholding.
- Half of your SE tax reduces your adjusted gross income (reported on Schedule 1).
- If you choose the home office actual method, be careful to only include the business portion of shared expenses and follow Form 8829 instructions.

Suggested next steps (I can help)
- Confirm: (a) whether the web design income is self‑employment; (b) whether the $4,400 student loan interest was paid in tax year 2024; (c) whether you have mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state/local tax payments for 2024.
- If you confirm, I can run a simple comparison: (a) standard deduction vs itemized (using any SALT/mortgage numbers you provide) and (b) compute an estimate of tax and self‑employment tax, and show the difference between using the simplified vs actual home‑office method.

Would you like me to:
- run the “itemize vs standard” comparison if you provide any state/property/mortgage tax numbers, or
- estimate your federal tax and self‑employment tax using the numbers you gave (I’ll need to know whether the $4,400 is 2024 interest and whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses)?

Which of these would be most helpful?

