- Net self-employment income = gross self‑employment receipts ($8,900) minus Schedule C business expenses (home office portion if you use actual method + any other business expenses you have — e.g., the $890 if it’s business).
- If net earnings from self‑employment are $400 or more, you owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) reported on Schedule SE (roughly 15.3% of net SE earnings before the 92.35% adjustment).
- You may deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (so it reduces your AGI).
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income — eligibility and amount depend on taxable income and the nature of the business. With modest self-employment income, QBI may apply, but we’d need to compute taxable income to check phaseouts.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Whether to itemize depends on whether your total itemizable deductions (Schedule A: medical over threshold if any, state and local taxes paid up to $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, charitable gifts, large miscellaneous items if allowed, property tax, etc.) exceed the standard deduction for married filing jointly.
