What you can (generally) deduct and how they are reported
1) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusively; home = 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
- You have an eligible home office for a self‑employed business (exclusive use + principal place of business).
- Two ways to claim it:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft.
     - 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction (reported directly on Schedule C).
  b) Actual (regular) method: business % × allowable home expenses.
     - Business percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%
     - Rent per year = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600
     - Rent portion deductible = $24,600 × 20.6667 ≈ $5,088 (plus you could allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., if you provide amounts).
- Which to use? Based strictly on rent alone, the actual method yields ≈ $5,088 > $775, so actual method gives a larger deduction if you don’t have other offsetting limits. Note: the home‑office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business — it’s limited by your net profit from the business (Schedule C). In your case, if the business has no other expenses, 8,900 − 5,088 = $3,812 net business income (so no immediate problem). The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income, which lowers income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Form(s): If you use the actual method you prepare Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer the result to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, no Form 8829 — just claim it on Schedule C.

2) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
