Short answer up front
- Likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large property taxes, >$25k–$30k of SALT, large medical expenses). With only the $2,000 charitable gift shown (and no mortgage interest or large SALT listed) your itemized total is well under the standard deduction.
- Your web‑design income is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C; that lets you claim a home‑office deduction (regular or simplified) against that business income and also causes self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Student loan interest is only deductible for interest paid in the tax year (and is limited to $2,500 and phases out at higher incomes). From what you wrote about Navient interest for 2016–2019, that won’t be deductible on your 2024 return unless you actually paid interest in 2024.
- Charitable donations are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the regular home‑office method), Schedule A (only if you itemize), Schedule 1 (for certain adjustments like the deductible half of self‑employment tax or student‑loan interest), plus the Illinois IL‑1040.

Detailed breakdown and steps

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 wages ($82,350): reported on Form 1040. Make sure you have your W‑2.
- Self‑employment (web design) $8,900: report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). Include any business expenses (you mentioned $890 — see question below about whether those are business expenses). If net profit is >0 you will also file Schedule SE and pay self‑employment tax (Social Security/Medicare on net earnings).
- Interest/dividends: $890 from Discover (bank interest) and $195 from TD Ameritrade — report as interest/dividend on Form 1040. If total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 you would also use Schedule B; your total is $1,085 so Schedule B is not required.

2) Home‑office deduction (you can use only for the self‑employment business)
- You qualify to take a home‑office deduction only for the portion of the home used regularly and exclusively for your business. You said 155 sq ft exclusive out of 750 sq ft.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft: 155 × $5 = $775 deduction.
  b) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.667% (≈20.67%). Apply that percentage to rent and other allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, possibly some repairs). Example using rent only: rent $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 × 20.667% ≈ $5,085. That is larger than the simplified method in your case.
- Which to choose: regular method will likely give a bigger deduction given your rent, but you should include other eligible expenses and keep documentation (copies of lease/rent receipts, utility bills). The regular method requires Form 8829 to be completed and the result flows to Schedule C. The simplified method is easier and requires no Form 8829 (you still claim it on Schedule C).
- Effect: the home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (which reduces income tax and self‑employment tax). You also can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

3) Student loan interest ($4,400 from Navient for 2016–2019 — need clarification)
- Only student loan interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible (up to $2,500). The deduction is an adjustment to income (you can claim it whether or not you itemize), subject to income phaseouts and other eligibility rules (e.g., you cannot claim it if married filing separately).
- If you truly paid $4,400 in 2024 you can only claim up to $2,500 (and the deduction phases out at higher incomes). If those payments were made in 2016–2019 (as written), they do not create a 2024 deduction. Ask Navient for the Form 1098‑E for the year(s) showing interest paid in the tax year you are filing for.
- I can help run the phaseout if you confirm your total modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) and whether your spouse has income.

4) Charitable deduction ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Charitable cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Receipts are required (you said you have the receipt—good).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this $2,000 on the federal return.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible at the federal level under current law (TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions through 2025).
- If those $890 relate to your self‑employment (web design) business (supplies, software, client costs), include them as Schedule C expenses — that reduces your net self‑employment income.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction is substantially larger than a small set of itemized amounts. With the items you listed:
  - Charitable $2,000
  - (Possible SALT up to $10,000 if you have property tax/state income tax — you didn’t list amounts)
  - No mortgage interest listed
  => It’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger. If you have significant property taxes or large unreimbursed medical expenses (over 7.5% of AGI), or large SALT up to $10k and mortgage interest, then itemizing could make sense.
- If you want, provide property tax amounts and any mortgage interest or state income tax paid and I can run a side‑by‑side itemize vs standard comparison.

7) Other items to consider
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): you may be eligible for a 20% deduction on qualified business income from your web design business, subject to rules and taxable income thresholds. Because your business income is relatively small, you likely can claim QBI of up to 20% of qualified business income (calculated after allowable business deductions). This is claimed using Form 8995 (or 8995‑A). I can estimate this if you provide net Schedule C profit.
- Self‑employment tax: expect to owe self‑employment tax on net Schedule C earnings (use Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment on the Form 1040.
- Estimated tax/withholding: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes or underwithholding penalties if not enough tax is withheld from your W‑2. You can increase withholding on W‑2 to cover the extra tax if that’s easier.

8) Forms you will likely use (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — for some adjustments including half of SE tax and student loan interest if applicable
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you claim the QBI deduction
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable, SALT up to cap, etc.)
- Keep/collect: W‑2, 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (if any), 1099‑INT (bank), 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (brokerage), Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) if applicable.

9) Records and receipts to gather now
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
- Any 1099s (clients, bank interest, brokerage)
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills for home office allocation
- Receipts for the $2,000 donation
- Documentation for the $890 expenses (explain whether business or employee)
- Documentation of any student loan interest paid in 2024 (Form 1098‑E from Navient)
- Any property tax statements, mortgage interest statements (Form 1098), and state tax paid

Questions I need to finalize exact numbers
1. Did you (or your spouse) actually pay any student loan interest during 2024? If so, how much was paid in 2024 and do you have a 2024 Form 1098‑E from Navient?
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web‑design clients or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
3. Does your spouse have income to report (you said married filing jointly but didn’t list spouse income)?
4. Do you pay property taxes or have mortgage interest? If so, how much for 2024? Also how much state income tax was withheld or paid?
5. Do you want me to run a sample calculation comparing: (a) standard deduction vs itemized, and (b) regular home‑office vs simplified method using the numbers you give?

If you want, provide the answers above and I’ll run the math (estimate taxable income, self‑employment tax, likely federal tax owed, and show exactly which filing method and forms are best). If you prefer, I can also draft a checklist/email you can send to your tax preparer with all of these details. Note: I’m not a CPA — for complex or high‑stakes issues (especially about prior‑year student loan interest or state specifics) consider confirming with a tax professional.

