I can walk through what’s deductible, how to treat each item, what forms you’ll need, and whether itemizing is likely to help. I’m not a CPA — consider this a detailed, practical overview and please check with a tax professional for filing.

Summary of the amounts you gave
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $82,350
- Self-employment (web design): $8,900 (6 clients)
- Interest: Discover savings $890 + TD Ameritrade $195 = $1,085 (taxable)
- Rent: $2,050/month for a 750 sq ft home → annual rent $24,600
- Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive use
- Charitable gifts: $2,000 to Doctors Without Borders (you have a receipt)
- Student loan interest: $4,400 (you wrote “Navient, 2016–2019” — see questions below)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $890 (need to know whether these are employee expenses or business expenses)

What you can and cannot deduct (federal)
- Self-employment income and related expenses
  - You must report the $8,900 on Schedule C (Form 1040) as business income.
  - Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your web design work are deductible on Schedule C. If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses (paid as a self-employed person and not reimbursed by an employer), include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 work, they are not deductible for most taxpayers under current law (suspended for 2018–2025).
- Home office
  - Because you say the 155 sq ft is used exclusively for business, you likely qualify for the home-office deduction for the self-employed.
  - Two methods:
    - Actual-expense method (Form 8829): compute percentage = 155 / 750 = 0.206667 (≈20.67%). Then apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, maintenance). With only rent provided, that’s about 20.667% × $24,600 = ~$5,081 deductible as a business expense on Schedule C (plus any allocable utilities if you have them).
    - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 155 = $775 (max 300 sq ft). Because your rent portion alone (~$5,081) is larger, the actual-expense method will likely be better unless other home expenses are negligible.
  - If you use the actual method, prepare Form 8829 to allocate expenses and calculate any carryover.
- Self-employment tax
  - Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment (SE) tax; SE tax is computed on Schedule SE. You can also deduct half of your SE tax as an “above-the-line” adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Interest income
  - The $1,085 of taxable interest must be reported on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). You need the 1099-INTs from the banks/broker.
  - Schedule B is only required if interest/dividend > $1,500 or other specific situations — you’re under that threshold, but you still report the interest.
- Student loan interest
  - The student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1 if you’re eligible.
  - Important: only interest you actually paid in the tax year is deductible. Your note “2016–2019” is ambiguous — you can’t deduct interest paid in prior tax years on the 2024 return. Also, the deduction phases out at higher income levels and is not allowed if married filing separately. With a combined MAGI in the range you reported (~$90k), you would generally be eligible for the deduction up to $2,500, assuming the interest was paid in 2024 and you meet the income limits. Confirm the year(s) the $4,400 was paid and get Form 1098-E from Navient (shows interest paid in the year).
- Charitable contributions
  - Your $2,000 cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible if you itemize; it does not affect the standard deduction calculation directly. You must have receipts (you said you do).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses
  - For most employees (W-2), unreimbursed work expenses are not deductible for federal returns through 2025 (suspended by TCJA). If these $890 expenses were incurred as part of your self-employed web design business, include them on Schedule C instead.
- State taxes (Illinois)
  - You’ll file an Illinois return (IL-1040). Illinois tax rules differ; state income and property taxes and how to deduct them on IL forms depend on IL rules. Illinois also has the SALT considerations for federal itemizing (subject to federal $10k cap if relevant).

Itemize vs. Standard deduction (federal)
- For 2024 the IRS standard deduction (married filing jointly) is $29,200. (Confirm on IRS site or with a preparer if you want absolute certainty.)
- Your known itemized deductions from your list: charitable $2,000. You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes, or large medical expenses.
- Likely conclusion: With only $2,000 in charitable contributions and no large state/local taxes or mortgage interest listed, your itemized total will be far less than the standard deduction. So in almost all realistic cases based on what you provided, you should take the standard deduction.
- Note: the home-office deduction is a business deduction (Schedule C), not an itemized deduction. It reduces your AGI (via reducing Schedule C net profit and the above-the-line half-SE tax adjustment) and so is available whether or not you itemize.

Forms you will likely need
- Federal
  - Form 1040 (main)
  - Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — interest, adjustments like student loan interest, half of SE tax if applicable
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $8,900 and business expenses (home office portion, any supplies, etc.)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using actual-expense method for home office
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — computes SE tax and half SE tax deduction
  - Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC: if you received any 1099s from clients include them; reconcile client payments with your records
  - 1099-INTs for bank/broker interest (report those amounts)
  - Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) — to substantiate student loan interest deduction
  - Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (doesn't look likely here)
- Illinois
  - IL-1040 and any required schedules/forms for credits or additions/subtractions (report the same income and follow IL instructions)
  - Keep records of Illinois withholding on your W-2 and any estimated tax payments

Illustrative rough calculation (example) — use only to see magnitude (not a final tax)
Assumptions: your $890 unreimbursed are NOT business expenses, and you qualify for up to $2,500 student loan interest deduction (paid during the year).
- Total income: W-2 $82,350 + self-employment $8,900 + interest $1,085 = $92,335
- Home-office actual rent portion as business expense: ~20.667% × $24,600 ≈ $5,081
- Schedule C net profit: $8,900 − $5,081 ≈ $3,819
- SE tax (approx): 0.9235 × $3,819 × 15.3% ≈ ~$540; half of that ≈ $270 deductible above the line
- Student loan interest deduction (if eligible) up to $2,500
- Above-the-line adjustments total ≈ $2,770 ($2,500 + $270)
- Estimated AGI ≈ $92,335 − $2,770 = $89,565
- Standard deduction (2024 MFJ) ≈ $29,200 → estimated taxable income ≈ $60,365
This is illustrative only — exact SE tax, credits, and final taxable income depend on exact amounts and other items (withholding, pre-tax retirement contributions, additional deductions, dependents, etc.).

