Thanks — here’s a clear, practical breakdown of what you can and cannot deduct, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, what forms you’ll need, and a few follow-up questions so I can make a precise calculation.

Quick summary (straight answer)
- Likely better to take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200) unless you have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable amounts you haven’t listed.
- Your web-design income is self-employment income — report it on Schedule C and deduct ordinary business expenses there (including a home-office deduction).
- The $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are deductible only if they are business expenses for the web‑design work (deduct on Schedule C). Unreimbursed employee expenses (if you were an employee) are not deductible for most taxpayers through 2025.
- Student loan interest: the deduction is limited to $2,500 (above-the-line) and phases out at higher MAGI; with the income you reported you’re likely eligible, but I need to confirm the $4,400 amount is interest paid in the tax year (only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible).
- Charitable contribution $2,000 is deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction you cannot also deduct that charitable gift (unless you want to itemize and total itemized deductions exceed $29,200).
- Home office: you can claim it on Schedule C. Two methods: simplified ($5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft) = 155 x $5 = $775; or actual method (pro rata share of rent, utilities, etc.). Given your rent ($2,050/mo), the actual-method rent portion alone is ~ $5,084 (155/750 × $24,600), so the actual method will likely give a larger deduction — but actual method has limits if it creates/larger business loss; see details below.

Details and reasoning

1) Which deductions you can take (based on what you provided)
- Self‑employment income (web design $8,900): report on Schedule C. Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (software, hosting, supplies, part or all of the $890 if those are business expenses, advertising, professional fees, etc.).
- Home office: deductible only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You said 155 sq ft exclusive; that qualifies. Two options:
  - Simplified method: 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 (no Form 8829).
  - Actual method: prorate allowable home costs by business-use percentage (155/750 = 20.667%). Example using rent only: $24,600 × 20.667% ≈ $5,084. Plus prorated share of utilities, renters/homeowner insurance, repairs, etc., if you have them. To use actual method you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach the result to Schedule C. The home-office deduction is limited by the net profit from that business; excess actual-method home-office deduction may carry forward.
- Student loan interest ($4,400): the student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1). The maximum deductible amount for most taxpayers is $2,500 in the tax year; the total amount you paid beyond that is not deductible in the current year. There is an income phaseout — for MFJ the deduction phases out at higher MAGI. With the incomes you listed, you are likely within the phase-in range (but I need your exact MAGI to be sure).
- Charitable contributions ($2,000): deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize.
- Interest from Discover ($890) and TD Ameritrade ($195): include as interest/dividend income. You will receive 1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B; include on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if interest/dividend > $1,500 or other reporting conditions).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890): if these are employee expenses (you were an employee and not self-employed) they generally are not deductible 2018–2025. If they are business expenses for your self-employment work, deduct them on Schedule C.

2) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2024): $29,200.
- Itemizing makes sense only if your total itemized deductions (State & Local Taxes up to $10,000 cap, mortgage interest, large charitable gifts, large medical expenses above threshold, etc.) exceed $29,200.
- From the items you gave: charitable $2,000 (only helpful if itemizing) + student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (not an itemized deduction) + no SALT/mortgage/property tax numbers provided → it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total. So recommendation: take the standard deduction and still claim Schedule C business deductions (including home office) which reduce your adjusted gross income (AGI).

3) How home office interacts with your other income
- The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C income (and therefore reduces income tax and self-employment tax).
- If you use the actual method and the home-office expenses exceed your net profit from that business, the home-office deduction may be limited and any disallowed amount may carry forward (check Form 8829 instructions).
- Example (illustrative with numbers you provided):
  - Gross self-employment income: $8,900
  - If the $890 is a business expense: 8,900 − 890 = 8,010
  - Simplified home office (775) → net profit ≈ $7,235
  - Actual-method home-office rent share (~$5,084) → net profit ≈ $2,926
  - Self-employment tax is ~15.3% on ~92.35% of net self-employment income; half of that SE tax is an adjustment to income. So using the actual method reduces SE tax and taxable income more than the simplified method in this case.
- Because the actual-method deduction (driven by your rent) looks substantially larger than the simplified-method amount, the actual method will probably be more beneficial — but confirm other home expenses and whether any limits apply.

4) Forms you will need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the web-design income and business expenses (including home-office deduction entry).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method to calculate the home-office deduction. If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute and report SE tax.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction (and deduction for one-half of SE tax flows here).
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — only if interest/dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply.
- If you have capital transactions from TD Ameritrade (sales), you may need Schedule D and Form 8949.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (for your $2,000 charitable gift and SALT/mortgage interest if applicable).
- You should collect all 1099s (1099-NEC or 1099-MISC if issued to you for the web design work, 1099‑INT from Discover, 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B from TD Ameritrade).

5) Important limits and notes
- Student loan interest deduction limit: maximum $2,500 in the year; phases out for higher MAGI. Only interest paid in the tax year counts — interest payments from prior years do not.
- SALT deduction is capped at $10,000 total for state and local taxes.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 jobs are not deductible for most taxpayers 2018–2025.
- Home-office deduction requires regular and exclusive business use; shared or occasional use does not qualify.
- Self-employed income may require estimated tax payments during the year to avoid penalties — if you haven’t made estimated payments you may owe underpayment penalties.

Next steps / what I need to give a precise computation
