- You should have a Form 1098‑E from Navient showing interest paid for the year. Keep your receipts.

3) Home office deduction (important — applies only to self‑employment)
- Because you earned self‑employment income (web design), you can claim a home office deduction for the exclusive 155 sq ft office.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft (IRS simplified rate) for up to 300 sq ft → 155 * $5 = $775. (This is easy to use.)
  b) Actual expense method: allocate the business percentage of actual home expenses. Your business % = 155 / 750 = 20.67% (roughly 20.7%). That lets you deduct 20.67% of rent and qualifying utilities/insurance that you pay. Example: annual rent $24,600 * 0.2067 ≈ $5,086 (plus your portion of utilities if applicable) — substantially larger than the simplified amount.
- Because your rent is high, the actual‑expenses method will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- How to claim it: report your web design income and expenses on Schedule C. If you use the actual method, use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the allowable home office deduction. The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit, which reduces self‑employment tax and taxable income.
- Important: the home office deduction is only available for self‑employed business use (Schedule C). If you are an employee doing side work solely as an employee (not self‑employed / independent contractor), you cannot claim it (unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025).

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are business expenses related to your web design self‑employment (supplies, software, business travel, client costs), report them on Schedule C — they’re deductible as business expenses.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (expenses incurred while working as an employee at Riverside Hospital and not reimbursed), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

5) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Your net self‑employment profit (Schedule C) will be subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You’ll calculate that on Schedule SE. You also get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
