What you can and cannot deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest:
  - The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment that can reduce your AGI (you do not have to itemize to take it).
  - The maximum deduction is $2,500 in the tax year (phaseouts apply by MAGI for married filing jointly). Based on the total income above, you are likely below the MFJ phaseout range and would be eligible for the full $2,500 — but confirm exact MAGI phaseout thresholds for 2024 or check with a preparer.
  - IMPORTANT: You can only deduct interest actually paid in 2024. You mentioned $4,400 (Navient, 2016–2019) — if that $4,400 was paid during 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500 this year; if the $4,400 refers to earlier years’ payments, those are not available now. Use Form 1098‑E from your lender to confirm the amount paid in 2024.

- Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders):
  - Deductible if you itemize (cash contribution to a qualified charity).
  - If you take the standard deduction, you do not get a separate deduction for this contribution.

- Unreimbursed expenses ($890):
  - If these were employee unreimbursed work expenses for your W‑2 job: currently (post-2017 tax law through 2025) these miscellaneous employee unreimbursed expenses are NOT deductible on your federal return.
  - If these $890 relate to your self‑employment/web design work (business supplies, software, client travel, etc.), report them as business expenses on Schedule C. Clarify which they are.

- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use in a 750 sq ft home):
  - Because you have self‑employment income from web design, you can claim a home office deduction for the portion used exclusively and regularly for that business.
  - Two methods:
    1. Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 155 sq ft * $5 = $775.
    2. Actual (regular) method: Allocate business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.6667%. Annual rent = $2,050 * 12 = $24,600. Business portion of rent = $24,600 * 0.206667 ≈ $5,086. (You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs in the same ratio if you have those bills.)
  - The home office deduction is claimed on Schedule C. If you use the actual method you generally need Form 8829 to calculate and support the deduction (Form 8829 is used for homeowners; for renters you still compute allocated expenses and report on Schedule C — some preparers still use 8829 logic).
  - Limitation: the home office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from the business. Any excess may be carried forward (but only against that business in future years).
  - For your situation: using the actual method (rent portion) produces a substantially larger deduction (~$5,086) than the simplified method ($775). Because your self‑employment gross is $8,900, actual method would reduce that to ~ $3,814 net before other business expenses, which is allowable (it doesn’t create a loss beyond the business income).

- Self‑employment tax and related deductions:
  - You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income. You calculate SE tax on Schedule SE.
  - You can deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.
  - You should also deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., the $890 expenses if they are business related, software, hosting, supplies, etc.).

- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A):
  - You may be eligible for up to 20% of your qualified business income as a deduction (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). With small net business income the QBI deduction will be small, but it may apply. It’s subject to taxable income limits and other rules.

Itemize vs Standard deduction
- For most taxpayers, choose whichever results in the larger deduction. Based on the items you provided:
  - Itemizable amount you explicitly listed = $2,000 charitable. (Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment, not an itemized deduction.)
  - Unless you have significant additional itemizable items you haven’t listed (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, high medical expenses, etc.), your itemized total is almost certainly far below the standard deduction.
- Standard deduction (married filing jointly): the standard deduction is substantially larger than $2,000 — in recent years it has been tens of thousands of dollars for MFJ. So it is almost certainly better to take the standard deduction for 2024 unless you have other sizeable deductible items you didn’t list.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable deductions (large mortgage interest, property tax + state income tax up to SALT cap, big medical expenses, etc.).

Estimated tax / withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe additional tax (income tax + self‑employment tax). If your employer withholding plus any estimated payments won’t cover that additional tax, you should make estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and other adjustments flow here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and business expenses, and claim home office deduction (or attach Form 8829 if using actual method)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — computes SE tax and the half‑SE deduction
- Form 8829 (if you use the actual method and are calculating business use of home expenses; if you use the simplified method, you don’t use 8829)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if applicable
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (charitable donations, SALT up to cap, etc.)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — only required if certain thresholds apply or you have certain foreign accounts; with your interest + dividends ($1,085) you are under typical Schedule B thresholds, but still report the amounts on Form 1040.
- Supporting forms you should collect:
  - W-2 from Riverside Hospital
  - 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC statements from web design clients, if provided (report income even if you didn’t get a 1099)
  - 1099‑INT from Discover
  - 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B from TD Ameritrade (or year‑end statement)
