Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Summary answer up front
- Likely best to take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) because the itemizable items you listed are small relative to the standard deduction.  
- You should report the $8,900 web‑design income on Schedule C (Form 1040) and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home‑office deduction is available for your self‑employment, and you can use either the simplified method (155 sq ft × $5 = $775) or the actual method (155/750 = 20.67% of allowable home expenses). The actual method applied to rent (2,050 × 12 = $24,600) gives about $5,080, which is larger than the simplified amount — but the deduction for the office space cannot exceed net income from that business for the year.  
- Student loan interest you paid in 2016–2019 is not deductible on your 2024 return; only interest you actually paid in the tax year is potentially deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income limits).  
- Charitable giving ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize. Unreimbursed employee expenses are generally not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA).  

Detailed guidance

1) Where to report each income item
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($82,350): report on Form 1040 (line for wages).  
- Web‑design income ($8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include them. Deduct business expenses on Schedule C.  
- Interest: Discover savings $890 and TD Ameritrade $195 → total $1,085. Report as interest income on Form 1040 (typically Schedule B only if total interest > $1,500; you’re below that). Expect 1099‑INT or 1099‑B statements.  

2) Home‑office deduction (how to treat it)
- Eligibility: You must use the 155 sq ft area exclusively and regularly for your web‑design business — you said it’s exclusive, so that part sounds satisfied.  
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per usable square foot up to 300 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775. Easy, no Form 8829 needed.  
  - Actual (recommended here if you only have rent and few other home costs): compute the business percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%. Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.). Example for rent only: annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600; 20.67% × $24,600 ≈ $5,083. That’s larger than the simplified amount.  
- Limits: The home‑office deduction is taken on Schedule C (Form 8829 if using actual method) and cannot exceed your net income from the business. If the home‑office expense would create a business loss, some of the deduction may be limited and carried forward. Using the actual method may require Form 8829.  
- If you are a renter (as your rent indicates) you can still take the home office deduction (you cannot depreciate the structure, but you can prorate rent and utilities).

3) Deductions you listed — what’s deductible for 2024
- Student loan interest ($4,400 from Navient 2016–2019): NOT deductible on your 2024 return unless that interest was actually paid in 2024. The current student loan interest deduction allows up to $2,500 of interest paid during the tax year and is subject to MAGI phaseouts. You need Form 1098‑E from the lender showing interest paid in the tax year to claim it. If you didn’t pay interest in 2024, you cannot claim that prior interest now.  
- Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. Keep your donation receipt; for cash donations you’ll need documentation.  
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890): if these are employee unreimbursed job expenses, they are not deductible under current law for most employees (TCJA suspension). If those expenses were actually business expenses for your web‑design self‑employment (e.g., software, hosting, supplies), they belong on Schedule C and ARE deductible. Please clarify whether these $890 are business expenses or unreimbursed employee expenses from the W‑2 job.  
- State & local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, etc.: you didn’t list these. They matter for itemizing. Remember SALT is limited to $10,000 total for state/local taxes + property tax.

4) Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- The 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than the single/itemized totals you listed. Given only $2,000 in charitable donations (and apparently no mortgage interest or big property taxes listed), your itemized total will very likely be far less than the standard deduction, so you should take the standard deduction.  
- Rough comparison from what you gave:
  - Itemizable items you listed = $2,000 (charity) + possible student loan interest (probably $0 for 2024 unless you paid interest this year) = ~$2,000. That’s well below the MFJ standard deduction. So standard deduction is almost certainly better.
- If you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, large property taxes, large unreimbursed medical expenses, or significant state income tax paid), re-run the comparison — if itemized total > standard deduction, itemize.

5) Other tax consequences to consider
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment tax on net earnings from the web design business (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.  
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income (Form 8995) from your Schedule C net profit, subject to rules and income limits. With $8,900 gross, you’ll likely have a small QBI deduction if you have profit.  
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income and only W‑2 withholding, you may need to make estimated tax payments or increase withholding to avoid penalties.

6) Forms you will likely need for 2024 tax filing
- Form 1040 — main tax form.  
- W‑2 (Riverside Hospital) — wages reported on Form 1040.  
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report web‑design income and business expenses.  
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — if you choose the actual method for home office (prorated rent, utilities, etc.). (If you use simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.)  
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if claiming the QBI (20% deduction), if applicable.  
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you decide to itemize (charitable contributions, state/local taxes, mortgage interest, etc.).  
- Schedule B — only if interest income > $1,500 or other conditions apply; otherwise not needed.  
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if you actually paid interest in the year).  
- 1099‑INT, 1099‑B, 1099‑NEC (if issued) — interest and contract work reporting; include originals/amounts on your return.  
- Keep receipts for charitable contributions, home‑office calculations, business receipts, and any loan interest docs.

7) Actionable next steps I recommend
- Clarify two items for a precise calculation: (a) Were the $4,400 student loan interest payments actually paid in 2024 (or only 2016–2019)? and (b) Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web‑design work (supplies/software), or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?  
- Gather documents: W‑2, 1099s, bank/interest statements, donation receipts, rent statements/utility bills (for home office), 1098‑E if any interest was paid in 2024.  
- If you want, I can run a quick rough tax calculation for you (estimate AGI, self‑employment tax, and whether actual home‑office or simplified is better) if you provide whether the $890 is business expense and whether any student loan interest was paid in the tax year and whether you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid).

Disclaimer
I’m not a certified tax professional. This is general guidance. For firm answers about phaseouts, exact limits, or complex situations, consult a CPA or tax preparer or use tax‑preparation software that will walk through the exact IRS rules and compute phaseouts and limits for 2024. Would you like me to run a numeric example using the actual home‑office method and the standard deduction (assuming the $890 is business expenses)?

