Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether itemizing likely helps, and which federal and Illinois forms you’ll need. A few short clarifying questions first (these affect the recommendations and calculations):

- Are the web-design earnings (the $8,900) from self-employment (you received 1099-NEC/treated as independent contractor income), or were you an employee for those clients?  
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web-design work (receipts) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job?  
- The $4,400 labeled “student loan interest (Navient, 2016–2019)” — is that the amount of interest you actually paid during the 2024 calendar year, or is it the total interest charged across earlier years? (Only interest paid in 2024 may be deductible on your 2024 return.)  
- Do you and your spouse have any mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state/local taxes withheld that would increase itemized deductions?

Assuming the common case (web-design income = self-employment; the $890 is business expense; the $4,400 is interest paid in 2024), here’s how things generally work and what to do:

1) Income you must report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $82,350 — report on Form 1040.  
- Self‑employment/web-design income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Form 1040). Use Schedule C to list income and business expenses.  
- Bank & investment interest: $890 (Discover) and $195 (TD Ameritrade) — report as interest income on Form 1040 (you’ll usually receive Form 1099‑INT / 1099‑B statements; keep them).  

2) Self-employment and business expense rules
- If the web-design work is self-employment, you report revenue on Schedule C. Business expenses directly related to the business are deductible on Schedule C (software, advertising, supplies, phone portion, business travel, and business portion of home expenses if applicable). The $890 you said as “unreimbursed expenses” — if those are business expenses for the web design and you have receipts, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, those are generally not deductible for tax years after 2017 under current law.  
- You will owe self-employment tax on net Schedule C earnings (Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.

3) Home office deduction — two methods and the quick math for your numbers
You said: exclusive office = 155 sq ft; total home = 750 sq ft; rent = $2,050/month.

- Business-use percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.67% (approx).

Two ways to claim:
A. Simplified method — $5.00 per square foot (max 300 sq ft). For 155 sq ft: 155 × $5 = $775. Report on Schedule C (no Form 8829 needed). Simple, fewer rules, no depreciation.

B. Actual-expense method — you allocate part of actual home expenses to the business. Example with your rent only (you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.):
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
  - Business portion = $24,600 × 20.67% ≈ $5,087.
  - If you also allocate utilities, insurance, etc., the deduction would be larger.

Important limitations:
- Home office deduction is only allowed if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.). Your 155 sq ft must be exclusive to the business.  
- The home-office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business in some respects — depreciation and some home-use components may be limited and carried forward if they would cause or increase a net loss. (If your total Schedule C expenses exceed your business gross income, some home-related deductions may be limited.)  
- For your numbers: actual method for rent alone (~$5,087) is larger than the simplified $775. But if your total business expenses (including home allocation) exceed gross receipts, you may have to limit some components. Using the simplified method avoids depreciation and carryforward complications.

Given your gross self-employment income of $8,900, either method is likely allowable without creating a major loss, but choosing actual method needs proper recordkeeping (rent, utilities, etc.). If you prefer simplicity and want to avoid depreciation/carryforward complexity, the simplified method is fine; if you want the biggest immediate reduction and you have supporting bills, the actual method will generally produce a larger deduction here.

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (reported on Form 1040 / Schedule 1) and the maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). You cannot deduct $4,400 in full; you can deduct up to $2,500 in 2024 if you are eligible and have actually paid that interest in 2024. Eligibility is phased out at higher MAGI for married filing jointly — with your reported income (W-2 + self-employment + interest), you are likely under the phaseout threshold, but I’ll confirm exact MAGI phaseout if you want. Lender should provide Form 1098‑E if interest paid exceeded reporting thresholds — keep that.

5) Charitable donation
- $2,000 cash donation to Doctors Without Borders is deductible if you itemize and the organization is a qualified charity (Doctors Without Borders is a qualified 501(c)(3)). Keep the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you do not get an extra deduction for that gift (except in limited years when special rules apply).

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890)
- For tax years 2018–2025, miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to the 2% floor (which include unreimbursed employee business expenses) are suspended for most employees. So if those $890 are unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses, they are not deductible. If they are self‑employment business expenses, put them on Schedule C.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction (general guidance)
- You must compare the total of itemized deductions (Schedule A) to the standard deduction for married filing jointly for tax year 2024. I don’t want to give the wrong exact standard-deduction number if rates have changed since my last data point, so please check the IRS standard-deduction amount for 2024 (or I can pull it for you if you’d like). Based on the items you listed, your only definite itemized amount is $2,000 charitable giving. Unless you also have significant state & local tax payments, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other deductible items, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total. So you will most likely take the standard deduction — but do the comparison once you add:
  - state income tax or sales taxes paid / property taxes (subject to $10k SALT cap),
  - mortgage interest,
  - charitable contributions,
  - large deductible medical expenses (that exceed 7.5% of AGI),
  - other allowable deductions.

8) Rough sample calculation (estimates, to show effect)
Assuming:
- W-2 wages $82,350  
- Net self-employment: rough example using actual home office (rent allocation ~$5,087) + business expense $890 => total business expenses ≈ $5,977; net self-employment income ≈ $8,900 − $5,977 ≈ $2,923. (If you use simplified home office instead, net self-employment income would be higher than this.)
- Interest income $890 + $195 = $1,085.
AGI before adjustments ≈ 82,350 + 2,923 + 1,085 = 86,358.
Possible adjustments: student loan interest (up to $2,500 if eligible) and half of self-employment tax (small in this example). Then compare itemized vs standard deduction. (This is illustrative — exact numbers depend on the facts you confirm and other deductions you have.)

9) Forms you’ll need (federal + state)
Federal:
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return) — main return.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web-design income and business expenses.  
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half of SE tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for the home office. (Not needed if you use the simplified method; you’ll just claim the simplified amount on Schedule C.)  
- Schedule 1 (if applicable) — for adjustments like student loan interest and half of self-employment tax (modern 1040 workflow may already include these lines).  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (for charitable deduction, SALT, mortgage interest, etc.).  
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC (if received), 1099‑INT, 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) and W-2 — these are not attached to Form 1040 but must be retained and used to fill the return.

Illinois state:
- IL‑1040 (Illinois Individual Income Tax Return) — file state return. Interest and self-employment income generally flow to IL return; Illinois has a flat income tax (check current rate). Keep IL-specific rules in mind (some deductions differ from federal).

Other items:
- Records/receipts for charitable gifts (your receipt), rent and utility bills (for home-office actual method), receipts for business expenses, and 1098‑E for student loan interest (if provided). Keep all records for at least three years.

10) Practical recommendations / next steps
- Confirm the clarifying items I asked at the top (especially whether the $4,400 was paid in 2024 and whether the $890 is business-related).  
- If the web-design work is indeed self-employment, prepare a Schedule C and decide whether to use simplified home office ($775) or actual allocation (~$5,087 for rent alone). If you want the larger actual deduction, gather rent receipts, utility bills, insurance, and any repair invoices. If you prefer simplicity, use the simplified method.  
- Compare total itemized deductions (including state & local taxes, mortgage interest, charitable gifts, etc.) to the 2024 standard deduction for MFJ, then choose the larger. I can run the comparison for you if you give me the amounts for mortgage interest, property taxes, state tax paid/withheld, and any other deductible items.  
- If you expect your self-employment income to grow or you expect to owe more tax, consider estimated quarterly payments next year to avoid penalties.  
- If anything is confusing or your situation has additional complications (e.g., carryover of prior-year home-office depreciation, business losses, spouse’s income or adjustments), consider a tax preparer or CPA — I can also help prepare a checklist or draft the numbers.

If you want, I can:
- Run exact calculations if you provide (a) confirmation about the student-loan interest payments in 2024, (b) whether the $890 unreimbursed is business-related, and (c) any state/local tax, mortgage interest, or property-tax amounts; or  
- Draft a checklist of documents and receipts to gather before filing; or  
