Thanks — that’s a good amount of detail. Below I’ll (A) list what is generally deductible from the items you gave, (B) explain the home-office options and which likely gives a bigger deduction, (C) say whether you should probably itemize or take the standard deduction, (D) list the federal (and state) forms you’ll likely need, (E) give an approximate illustration of how the self‑employment/home office items flow into your return, and (F) next steps / clarifying questions. I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance; I recommend you confirm with a tax professional before filing.

A. What you can (generally) deduct from the items you listed
- Self-employment/business expenses (web design): deductible on Schedule C. That includes ordinary and necessary expenses directly related to the business (software, supplies, internet portion, client-related expenses, the unreimbursed expenses you mentioned if they are business-related). These reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Home office: deductible only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business. You can choose either:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft; or
  - Actual-expense method: your business percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). See section B below for the math.
- Self-employment tax: you pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C earnings. Half the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (adjustment to income).
- Interest and dividends: report interest from Discover and the $195 from TD Ameritrade as interest/dividend income. These are taxable unless from a tax-exempt source. If total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 you’ll attach Schedule B (but your reported totals are below that).
- Charitable contributions: deductible if given to qualified charities and you have the receipt. Cash contributions to charities are itemized deductions on Schedule A (not included in the standard deduction). For any single cash gift over $250 you need a contemporaneous written acknowledgment from the charity (you said you have the receipt).
- Student loan interest: this is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500), subject to income (MAGI) limits and only for interest paid in the tax year being filed. Important: only interest paid in the 2024 tax year is deductible on your 2024 return — prior-year payments (2016–2019) are not deductible on the 2024 return. Also, the $2,500 limit is the maximum; phaseouts apply by income and filing status.

Not deductible (based on current law):
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses for W-2 employees (suspended by TCJA through 2025). But unreimbursed expenses for your self-employment work are deductible on Schedule C (see above).

B. Home-office: simplified vs actual method (your numbers)
You said: office = 155 sq ft exclusive; total home = 750 sq ft; rent = $2,050/month.

- Business percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.6667% (≈ 20.67%).
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Actual-expense business portion of rent = 20.6667% × $24,600 = $5,084 (rounded).

Simplified method:
- 155 sq ft × $5 = $775.

Which to choose:
- Actual-expense method (rent portion alone) yields about $5,084 vs $775 with the simplified method, so actual expenses give a substantially larger deduction. If you have other allocable home costs (a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) that increases the actual-method deduction further. Note: the home office deduction (actual method) is limited to net profit from the business (gross business income minus other business expenses). Any disallowed excess may be carried forward (rules are a little different for depreciation; keep records). The simplified method has no carryover.

C. Standard deduction vs itemize
- For 2024, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than an itemization that only includes $2,000 in charitable gifts. Unless you have significant other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses in excess of the AGI threshold, large unreimbursed casualty losses, etc.), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (doesn’t affect the choice to itemize) and charitable contributions only matter if total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.

Given only the values you provided (charitable $2,000), you will very likely take the standard deduction. If you have sizable state income tax paid, property taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable items, add those up and compare to the standard deduction.

D. Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 business income and business expenses (including the home-office deduction as part of your Schedule C expenses if you use the actual method; simplified method is reported differently but still on Schedule C).
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (half of SE tax deduction shows on Schedule 1; student loan interest deduction also reported on Schedule 1 if you qualify).
- Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income deduction) — you may qualify for the QBI deduction (20% of qualified business income) and use Form 8995 unless your situation requires the more complex Form 8995-A.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (probably not, unless you have more itemizable expenses than the standard deduction).
- Schedule B — only if interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (your totals appear below that threshold).
- Keep these informational forms you’ll receive and use: W-2 from Riverside Hospital; 1099-INT from Discover; 1099-DIV or 1099-B from TD Ameritrade (depending on whether it’s dividends or sales/gains); any 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC you receive from clients (even if you did not receive 1099s, you must still report the income); Form 1098-E (student loan interest) if Navient or other servicer issued it showing interest paid in 2024.
- State return: Illinois Form IL-1040 — Illinois tax rules are separate; include wages and interest on your IL return and any credits/deductions allowed by Illinois.

E. Rough numbers to illustrate how the pieces interact (approximate)
(using the actual home-office method and assuming the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses):
- Business gross income: $8,900
- Business expenses (other): $890
- Home office (actual, rent portion): $5,084
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $8,900 − $890 − $5,084 = $2,926
- Self-employment tax roughly: SE taxable amount = 92.35% × $2,926 ≈ $2,702; SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $2,702 ≈ $414
- Half SE tax deduction ≈ $207 (above-the-line)
- Total taxable income sources: W-2 $82,350 + interest/dividends $1,085 + net SE $2,926 = ~$86,361 (then subtract above-the-line items like half SE tax and any student loan interest you qualify for)
- Compare itemized vs standard: unless you have additional itemizable expenses beyond the $2,000 charity and whatever SALT/mortgage interest you paid, the 2024 standard deduction for MFJ (~$29,200) will likely be greater than your itemized total.

F. Next steps and clarifying questions I need to finalize guidance
1. Student loan interest:
   - Confirm when you paid the $4,400 in student loan interest. Only interest you actually paid in 2024 can be deducted on your 2024 tax return, and the maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). Do you have Form 1098-E from Navient for 2024? Also, was any of that interest paid by someone else or forgiven?
2. Unreimbursed expenses:
   - Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses related to your web-design self-employment? If yes, they belong on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses from your W-2 employment, they are not deductible (suspended through 2025).
3. 1099s / documentation:
   - Did you receive any 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC from clients for the $8,900 work? Any 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B, or 1098-E for 2024?
4. Other itemizable items:
   - Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, significant state/local taxes paid in 2024, or large unreimbursed medical expenses? These could raise itemized deductions above the standard deduction.
5. Spouse’s income:
   - Does your spouse have income or deductions that change AGI or phaseout thresholds (student loan interest phaseout, QBI interactions)?

G. Other items to consider
- QBI deduction: you likely qualify for a 20% qualified business income deduction on part of your net Schedule C income (subject to rules). That may lower your taxable income further; Form 8995 is used for most taxpayers.
- Estimated tax payments: because of SE tax and possible additional income tax, you may owe some tax when filing. If your self-employment income is recurring, consider making quarterly estimated payments next year to avoid penalties.
- Records: keep receipts for the charitable gift (you said you have it), documentation of home-office calculation (floor plan, square footage), business receipts, bank statements, and all 1099/W-2/1098-E forms.

