  - Schedule C net ≈ 8,900 − (775 + 890) = 7,235.
  - SE tax larger than with the actual method in this case (because actual homeoffice expense was larger).
- So, using the actual method appears more favorable here (larger business deduction) — but I need confirmation that the home office is eligible and that the $890 are business expenses.

9) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to report interest income, adjustments to income (student loan interest, half SE tax, etc.).
- Schedule C — report self‑employment income and business expenses (web design).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual method for the home office deduction.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given the info you provided).
- 1099s: attach the info as required (report all income even without 1099s).

State (Illinois)
- IL‑1040 — Illinois individual income tax return. Illinois starts with your federal AGI and has its own additions/subtractions; report the income you reported on the federal return. Illinois does not allow a federal home office deduction as a subtraction from IL income in most cases (it follows federal AGI but check IL specifics). Charitable contributions may have different state treatment. If you had IL income tax withheld from your W‑2, include the withholding on IL‑1040.

