Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’m not a CPA or attorney, so consider this general guidance and confirm any final calculations with a tax professional. A few quick clarifying questions first (answers change some details):
- Were any of the student-loan interest payments made in 2024 (and do you have a 1098-E from Navient for 2024)? Or is the $4,400 interest from 2016–2019 (i.e., not paid in 2024)?
- Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or other large deductible expenses (medical expenses, sizable state income or property taxes) for 2024?
- Did your 6 web-design clients issue 1099-NEC or 1099‑MISC forms to you for 2024?
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” for employee work expenses (as an employee) or business expenses related to your web design (self-employed)?

Below I assume the student-loan interest was not paid in 2024 (you’ll tell me if that’s not the case) and that you have no other major itemizable expenses beyond the $2,000 charitable gift.

1) What you can deduct (based on what you listed)
- W-2 wages ($82,350): included in gross income; no direct deduction except standard/itemized and payroll withholding.
- Self-employment (web design) income ($8,900): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against that income (supplies, software, advertising, phone/internet portion, the business part of your home office, etc.).
- Interest from Discover ($890) and TD Ameritrade ($195): taxable interest/dividends reported on 1040 (via 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV). Not deductible.
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible if you itemize (Schedule A) and have the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you generally cannot separately deduct this gift.
- Student-loan interest ($4,400 reported from 2016–2019): only interest you actually paid in the tax year (2024) is potentially deductible (up to $2,500, subject to AGI phaseouts) as an adjustment to income (i.e., above-the-line on Schedule 1). Interest from earlier years is not deductible on your 2024 return unless you actually paid it in 2024. You need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for interest paid in 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890): these are generally nondeductible for most employees for tax years 2018–2025 because of TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions. If these are self-employment/business expenses, deduct them on Schedule C instead.

2) Itemize vs standard deduction (married filing jointly)
- You reported only $2,000 in charitable contributions and no other itemizable items you listed. The standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially higher than $2,000 (so in almost all cases you will take the standard deduction unless you have sizable mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the SALT cap, significant medical expenses, or other large deductible items).
- Bottom line (based on provided items): take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions you didn’t list (mortgage interest, property tax, big state taxes, large unreimbursed medical expenses, etc.).

3) Home office — how to treat it
You said: rented home 750 sq ft, home office 155 sq ft, exclusive use.

- Calculate the business percentage:
  - 155 ÷ 750 = 0.2067 → ~20.67% business use of the home.

- Two methods to claim the home-office deduction:
  A) Simplified method
     - Multiply the square footage used for business (up to 300 sq ft) by the IRS simplified rate (historically $5/sq ft; confirm current year rate when filing).
     - Example (if rate = $5): 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction.
     - Pros: easier recordkeeping, no Form 8829 required.
     - Limitation: the simplified deduction cannot exceed your net self-employment income from that business.

  B) Actual-expense method
     - Allocate actual home expenses by business percentage. For a renter, rent is a deductible home expense allocated to business. Example:
       - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
       - Business portion = 24,600 × 20.667% ≈ $5,088.
     - You can also allocate business share of utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable home expenses.
     - To use actual method you typically file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with your Schedule C.
     - Limitation: the home-office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business for the year. If the allocated home-office expenses exceed your business income after other business expenses, you may have to carry forward the excess (Form 8829 rules). The simplified method’s deduction is also limited to net business income.

- Which method to pick?
  - Based on the numbers you provided, actual method gives a much larger deduction (≈$5,088) than simplified ($775). If your Schedule C has few other expenses, actual method will reduce your net self‑employment income much more.
  - However, confirm that the actual deduction won’t create disallowed loss; if you have other business expenses that already reduce the business profit, check limits. If you want simplicity and you don’t expect large home-office allocation to be needed or you prefer fewer forms, pick simplified.
  - If you use actual method, keep good records (rent, utilities, insurance, photos of exclusive-use area, measurement notes).

4) Self-employment tax and other considerations
- Net profit from self-employment is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security & Medicare) reported on Schedule SE (approx 15.3% on net earnings, but you compute using IRS rules; you can deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1).
- You may be eligible for the qualified business income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 1040, Form 8995 or 8995‑A) for pass-through/self‑employment income; eligibility depends on taxable income and type of business — check the rules or ask your CPA.
- If you expect to owe tax because of self-employment income and not enough withholding, you should make estimated quarterly tax payments to avoid penalties.

5) Forms and documents you’ll need (federal and Illinois)
Documents to collect:
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099-NEC / records of income and receipts for the $8,900 web-design income (and records for any business expenses).
- 1099‑INT from Discover Bank (interest $890).
- 1099‑DIV / 1099‑INT / 1099‑B from TD Ameritrade for the $195 (and any brokerage statements).
- Receipts for charitable donation (Doctors Without Borders) — written acknowledgement for contributions over $250.
- 1098‑E from your student loan servicer for any student-loan interest paid in 2024 (if applicable).
- Proof of rent and utility bills, and documentation of the home office square footage (for actual method).

Forms you’ll likely file (federal):
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for things like student-loan interest deduction (if eligible) and half of SE tax adjustment.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the web-design income and business expenses (including home office deduction if using actual; for simplified home office, put the simplified deduction on Schedule C).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual-home-expense method (business use of home).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely you won’t based on the items you gave).
- Form 8995 or Form 8995‑A — if claiming QBI deduction (if eligible).
- Any other forms related to credits/adjustments you may be eligible for.

State (Illinois):
- IL-1040 (Illinois individual tax return). Illinois taxes taxable income (flat rate); interest and dividends are generally included; there are Illinois-specific rules — check the state form instructions or ask a tax preparer.

6) Quick example (illustrative) using the numbers you gave
- Gross income components: W-2 $82,350 + web income $8,900 + interest $890 + brokerage $195 = $92,335 total gross.
- If you use the actual home-office allocation of rent (~$5,088) as a Schedule C business expense and have no other business expenses:
  - Schedule C net profit ≈ 8,900 − 5,088 = 3,812.
  - Self-employment tax applies to that net profit; you can deduct half of SE tax on Schedule 1.
  - Because the business profit remains positive after the home-office deduction, the full home-office allocation is usable.
- If you take the standard deduction for MFJ (much larger than $2,000 charity), you will likely take the standard deduction rather than itemizing.

7) Recommended next steps
- Confirm whether student-loan interest was paid in 2024 and whether you have Form 1098‑E.
- Gather W-2, 1099s, rent receipts, utility bills, receipts for business expenses, and your charitable receipt.
- Decide simplified vs actual home-office method. If you want to maximize deduction and can support the allocation, actual method (Form 8829) looks favorable given your rent and square footage; if you want simplicity, choose simplified.
- Consider consulting a CPA or tax professional to:
  - Confirm the student-loan interest rules for your situation.
  - Ensure correct handling of home-office limits and carryforward rules.
  - Check QBI eligibility and calculate self-employment tax/estimated payments.
  - Prepare federal and Illinois returns.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a checklist email you can send to a CPA with all this info.
- Run estimated tax calculations (self-employment tax and approximate federal tax) if you want rough numbers — tell me whether the $4,400 student interest was paid in 2024 and any other deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, etc.).

