What you can deduct (federal) — short answers
- Self‑employment income: report the $8,900 on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work (see below).
- Home office: You can claim a home office deduction for the business portion of your home used exclusively for the web‑design business. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775.
  - Actual (recommended here unless simplicity matters): allocate rent/utilities by square footage (155/750 ≈ 20.667%). On rent alone: $24,600 × 0.20667 ≈ $5,088. If you also have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., their business portions would add more.
  - Note: the home‑office deduction is claimed on Schedule C. If the actual home‑office deduction would exceed your business profit (before the home‑office amount), the excess is limited / carried forward per IRS rules.
- Business expenses: If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are expenses you incurred for the web‑design work (supplies, software, domain hosting, client travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for tax years after the TCJA (through 2025) except for a few limited categories.
- Bank/brokerage interest/dividends: Report the $890 and $195 on your Form 1040 (you’ll get 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B as applicable).
- Charitable contribution: $2,000 cash to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). No deduction if you take the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest: Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible (up to $2,500, as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040) and is subject to MAGI phaseouts. Interest you paid in 2016–2019 cannot be deducted on your 2024 return. If you paid student loan interest in 2024, look for Form 1098‑E from your lender.

Self‑employment tax
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.

Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- The standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly for tax year 2024 is $29,200.
- Known itemizable items you listed: $2,000 charitable gift. (You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes, or state and local taxes.)
- Example: even if you maxed the SALT cap ($10,000) + your $2,000 charitable = $12,000 — still below the $29,200 standard deduction. So based on what you’ve given, you will very likely take the standard deduction.
- If you have additional deductible items you didn’t list (significant mortgage interest, large property taxes, more charitable gifts, unusually large medical expenses), add those up. You should itemize only if your total Schedule A deductions exceed $29,200.

How the home office interacts with itemizing and limits
- The home‑office deduction is a business deduction on Schedule C (not an itemized deduction). So claiming the home office does not force you to itemize.
- The actual‑expense home office method (using your rent allocation) is larger here (~$5,088) than the simplified ($775). However, the home office deduction cannot create or increase a business loss for the activity — it’s limited to your net business income (gross business income minus business expenses, excluding the home office deduction). Any excess home‑office deduction may be carried forward to future years.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return)
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments or other income items)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web design income and business expenses
