How each item is treated
1. Self-employment (web-design) income
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You report gross receipts ($8,900) and subtract business expenses to get net profit/loss.  
- Business expenses can include supplies, software, advertising, business phone/Internet (business portion), and the home-office allocation (see below). Any 1099-NEC you received/issued should be included, but you must report the income even if you didn’t receive 1099s.

2. Home office
- You have two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 / sq ft up to 300 sq ft = 155 * $5 = $775 deduction.
  b) Regular (actual) method: allocate a share of allowable indirect expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.) by the business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 155 / 750 = 20.666...% (~20.67%).
    - Rent: $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 × 20.666% ≈ $5,088 allocated to the home office.
    - If you have utility bills or other shared home costs, the home-office share of those is also deductible under the actual method.
- For self-employed taxpayers, the home-office deduction is taken on Form 8829 (or calculated on Schedule C if simplified method applies) and reduces Schedule C net income. Because the actual method’s rent allocation (~$5,088) is much larger than the simplified amount ($775), the actual method will likely give you a bigger deduction — unless you don’t want to track other shared expenses. Note: Employees cannot claim the home-office deduction due to the 2018–2025 suspension, but as a self-employed web designer you can.

3. Self-employment tax
- Net self-employment profit (Schedule C) is subject to self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare). You calculate SE tax on Schedule SE.
- You can deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (this is above-the-line).

4. Interest income (Discover, TD Ameritrade)
- Report on Form 1040 as interest (1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B info). Include the full $1,085 (and any reported amounts on 1099s).

5. Student loan interest ($4,400)
- Student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1). BUT:
  - The deduction is limited to a maximum amount (historically $2,500) and phases out above certain MAGI levels. With the income you gave (W-2 + other income ~ $92,335 total before adjustments), you’re likely under the phaseout threshold and would be eligible for the full allowable deduction — but only up to the statutory cap (commonly $2,500).  
  - Important: the deduction is for interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for. You mentioned “Navient, 2016–2019” — if those $4,400 were paid in earlier years (2016–2019) they would not be deductible on your 2024 tax return. Please confirm whether $4,400 is interest you actually paid in tax year 2024 (or the tax year you’re filing).
  - Look for Form 1098-E from your loan servicer showing interest paid in 2024; if none, use your payment records.

