Below is a concise, practical summary based on the numbers you gave.

1) How to report each income item
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($82,350): Report on Form 1040 (W-2 box amounts).
- Web-design income ($8,900, 6 clients): Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If clients issued 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, include those and any business expenses. Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 and is subject to self-employment (SE) tax.
- Interest from Discover ($890): Report as interest income on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if total interest/dividends exceed the Schedule B threshold or you have foreign accounts).
- $195 from TD Ameritrade: confirm whether that is interest, dividends, or proceeds/gain from sales. If dividends, report as dividend income (possibly Schedule B). If proceeds from sales, you’ll get Form 1099-B and must report on Form 8949 / Schedule D.

2) Home-office deduction — two methods and which makes sense here
You qualify for a home-office deduction because you have self-employment income and the space is used exclusively and regularly for business.

Facts you gave:
- Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive
- Total residence: 750 sq ft
- Rent: $2,050/month → $24,600/year
- Business income: $8,900

a) Regular method (actual-expense allocation)
- Business-use percent = 155 / 750 = 20.6667%
- Home-office portion of rent = $24,600 × 20.6667% ≈ $5,084
- You would use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to calculate the allowed deduction. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., by that percentage if you have those costs.
- That $5,084 reduces your Schedule C gross receipts to arrive at net profit. Because $5,084 < $8,900, the home-office deduction is fully usable (it cannot exceed net business income).

b) Simplified method
- $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 home-office deduction (no Form 8829 required; you just enter it on Schedule C).
- Easier, but the deduction is much smaller than the regular method here.

Which to use? Based on the numbers you provided, the regular method will likely save you more tax (it reduces both income tax and SE tax), and because the home-office portion of rent is less than your business income, you can claim it fully. Use the regular method if you have records of rent and square footage (you do). Use simplified only if you prefer minimal recordkeeping or have no other allocable expenses.

Quick illustration (rounded):
- Regular method net self-employment profit ≈ $8,900 − $5,084 = $3,816.
- SE tax on that profit ≈ $3,816 × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $539 (half is deductible on Form 1040).
- Simplified method net profit ≈ $8,900 − $775 = $8,125 → much larger SE tax (~$1,148). So regular method here reduces both income tax and SE tax.

3) Student loan interest ($4,400)
- Only interest you actually paid in the tax year (2024) is deductible, and the deduction maximum is $2,500 (above-the-line, reported on Form 1040 Schedule 1) subject to MAGI phaseouts for married filing jointly.
- If the $4,400 was paid in earlier years (2016–2019), it is not deductible on your 2024 return. If some or all was paid in 2024, only the 2024 portion counts, up to $2,500 and subject to phaseout rules. Please confirm what year(s) those payments were made.

4) Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A) and you have the receipt (you said you do).
- If you take the standard deduction instead, you cannot deduct the $2,000 on your federal return (except for a few special provisions that don’t apply generally for 2024).
- Charitable cash contributions require a written acknowledgement from the organization for any single donation of $250+ — you said you have the receipt, which is good.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses: these are not deductible on federal returns for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- If these are business expenses for your web-design work, include them on Schedule C (they reduce net self-employment income and SE tax).

6) Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- Based on the items you listed, likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total:
  - Charitable: $2,000
  - Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (not an itemized amount), so it helps even if you take standard deduction (but only if it’s paid in 2024 and you qualify).
  - You did not list mortgage interest, state/local taxes paid, or large medical expenses — common itemized categories that could make itemizing worthwhile.
- Therefore, you will probably take the standard deduction (for married filing jointly). If you have additional itemizable amounts not listed (state income or real estate taxes, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical >7.5% of AGI, etc.), add those up to compare.

7) Forms you will likely need
Federal:
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — if you have adjustments or additional income
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — for web-design income and allowable business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the regular home-office method
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (likely not, based on provided items)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — only if interest/dividends exceed threshold or foreign accounts
- Form 8949 and Schedule D — if you have capital gains/losses from TD Ameritrade trades (1099-B)
- Keep Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) if you paid student loan interest in 2024
- Keep all 1099s: 1099-NEC (if applicable), 1099-MISC, 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B

State:
- Illinois IL-1040 (Illinois state return). Illinois will use your federal AGI in calculating the state tax.

8) Other considerations / next steps
- Gather all year-end tax documents: W-2, all 1099s (NEC, INT, DIV, B), 1098-E for loan interest if 2024 payments, donation receipt, rent/lease and utility documents (for home office). Confirm what the $195 from TD Ameritrade represents (dividend, interest, or brokerage sale).
- Confirm whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (Schedule C) or employee expenses (non-deductible).
- If you expect similar self-employment income next year, consider quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Because the home-office deduction and the student-loan interest rules can interact with AGI and phaseouts, and because some items (1099-B capital gains, student loan limits) depend on details, consider using tax software or consulting a CPA to finalize the return.

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers for a more precise estimated federal tax owed under both home-office methods (I’d use an assumed standard deduction amount and current rates), or
- Draft a checklist of the exact documents to collect before you file, or
- Walk through completing Schedule C and Form 8829 with your numbers step-by-step.

Which of those would you like next? And please confirm the student-loan timing and the nature of the $890 and the $195 from TD Ameritrade.

