1) Income and what must be reported
- W-2 wages: $82,350 — reported on Form 1040.
- Self-employment (web design) income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC, include those amounts.
- Interest from Discover savings: $890 — report as interest income (1099‑INT). If total interest/ordinary dividends < $1,500 you usually just report it on Form 1040; if bigger, you might also need Schedule B.
- TD Ameritrade $195 — report as interest or dividends (or sale proceeds if it’s from trades — then capital gains reporting may apply via Form 1099-B and Schedule D/Form 8949).

2) Deductions you specifically mentioned — which are allowed and how they work
- Student loan interest ($4,400 you listed): the student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (reduces your AGI) up to $2,500 for the tax year, subject to income phaseouts. You only can deduct interest actually paid in the tax year and it must be on a qualified student loan. You should receive (or request) Form 1098‑E from Navient showing interest paid. Clarify: is $4,400 the amount you paid in 2024 (one year) or a cumulative amount from earlier years? If it’s 2024 payments, your maximum deduction is $2,500 (unless your MAGI is over the phaseout threshold).
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You need the receipt (you said you have it). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this gift on Schedule A.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890): whether these are deductible depends on their nature:
  - If they are business expenses related to your web design side gig (supplies, software, advertising, client travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce self-employment net income.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (costs related to your W-2 job), they are NOT deductible for most taxpayers because those miscellaneous itemized deductions were suspended starting in 2018.
  Please confirm the nature of these $890 expenses.
- Home-office (see next section) — deductible as a business expense against your self-employment income if you meet the regular-and-exclusive-use rules.

3) Home-office deduction — how to treat it and a quick compare
You stated: exclusive 155 sq ft home office; total home 750 sq ft; rent $2,050/month.

- Business-use percentage = 155 / 750 = 0.20667 (about 20.67%).
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Allocated rent (actual-method) = 20.667% × $24,600 ≈ $5,084 (this is the portion of rent that can be claimed as part of actual home expenses).
- Simplified method = $5 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 (max 300 sq ft).

Which method to use:
- You can choose either the simplified method ($5/sq ft) or the actual-expense method. You generally choose the method that gives the larger deduction, but if you use the actual method you must prorate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) and complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach results to Schedule C.
- Example comparisons (using only rent + the $890 business expenses if those are business-related):
  - Actual method (rent allocation only) + $890 business expenses:
    - Home-office rent portion ≈ $5,084 + other allocated utilities/ins/etc (not included here) + $890 other business expenses = $5,974 total business expense → net business income = $8,900 − $5,974 = $2,926.
  - Simplified method + $890 business expenses:
    - Home-office = $775 + $890 = $1,665 → net business income = $8,900 − $1,665 = $7,235.
  - Which is better here? Based on rent alone, the actual method produces a larger deduction (≈$5,084 vs $775). So the actual method will likely lower your business net income more. Note: some rules limit the home-office deduction to the business gross income (i.e., you generally cannot create or increase a business loss from the home-office deduction beyond your business income; excess home-office deductions may carry forward). Also you can only take the home-office deduction if you use the area regularly and exclusively for business.

Effect on self-employment tax:
- Lowering net profit on Schedule C reduces both income tax and self-employment tax. You’ll also owe self-employment tax (Schedule SE) on your net self‑employment earnings (roughly 15.3% on net earnings), but you can deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income.

Other note: The Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) may apply to part of your net business income; if you have net profit from self-employment you may qualify to deduct up to 20% of qualified business income (subject to rules). That’s reported on Form 8995 or 8995‑A.

4) Itemize vs. standard deduction (given what you told me)
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than $2,000 of charitable giving (for recent years the MFJ standard deduction has been well into the tens of thousands). Based only on the items you listed (charitable $2,000 and no mortgage interest or large state & local tax/property tax), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction and not itemizing.
- Important exceptions: if you have significant mortgage interest, large property taxes and state income taxes (SALT) up to the $10,000 cap, large unreimbursed medical expenses (over 7.5% of AGI) or other sizeable itemizable amounts, itemizing could make sense. But with only $2,000 charitable giving shown, standard deduction is likely the right choice.
- Student loan interest is ABOVE-THE-LINE up to the limit (i.e., you get that benefit whether you itemize or not, subject to income limits and the $2,500 cap).

5) Forms you’ll likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for some adjustments and additional income lines (student loan interest deduction and some other items end up here depending on year’s form layout).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your web design income and business expenses (including home-office if using the simplified method; if using actual-method you complete Form 8829 and carry numbers to Schedule C).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax and deductible half-of-SE-tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home — if you use the actual expense method for the home-office deduction.
- Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — include any you received for clients (you’ll use them to complete Schedule C).
- Form 1099-INT — for bank interest (Discover).
- Form 1099-B / Form 1099-DIV — from TD Ameritrade if applicable; if you sold securities, you may need Form 8949 and Schedule D as well.
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement from Navient (use to substantiate the student loan interest deduction).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (e.g., your $2,000 donation plus other itemizable amounts).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — if you claim the QBI (20% pass-through deduction), depending on complexity and income.

Illinois state:
- Illinois Form IL‑1040 — Illinois requires a state return. IL tax rules differ from federal (e.g., no standard deduction vs exemptions differences), so you’ll report your federal AGI as a starting point and then follow IL adjustments. Keep your W-2 and 1099s for IL return as well.

6) Other important items and practical next steps
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self-employment income, you may owe self-employment tax and additional income tax beyond what’s withheld from your W-2. If you didn’t make estimated payments during the year you may owe when you file (and possibly penalties). Consider making estimated payments for next year if you expect similar self-employed income.
